tration. Dissolve the contents of 1 sachet in 200 mL of freshly boiled and cooled water."}, "generalDescription": "Hydrite oral rehydration powder is indicated for the prevention and treatment of dehydration caused by diarrhea in pediatric and adult patients. It effectively replaces water and electrolytes lost due to acute diarrhea, vomiting, high fever, or excessive fluid depletion resulting from strenuous physical exertion and athletic activities.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Saccharin Sodium", "activeIngredients": "Sodium Chloride 520 mg, Sodium Citrate 580 mg, Potassium Chloride 300 mg, Anhydrous Glucose 2.7 g"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Safe for use during lactation.", "pregnancy": "Safe for use during pregnancy."}}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:49:41.454	hydrite-oral-rehydration-salts-powder-for-dehydration-and-electrolyte-imbalance-30-sachets-x-4-1g	245
1712	Lamictal 25mg Lamotrigine Tablets for Epilepsy and Bipolar Disorder (3 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P03019	\N	\N	\N	Vietnam	164433.00	147990.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250508070559-0-P03019.jpg?versionId=pTNIxNA09MoujtinVFHvM0qSlMVzk4n.	Lamictal 25mg tablets indicated for the treatment of epilepsy and bipolar disorder (3 blisters x 10 tablets).	{"indications": ["Lamictal is indicated for use in the following cases:", "Epilepsy:", "Adults (over 12 years of age): Lamictal is indicated as adjunctive or monotherapy for the treatment of partial seizures and generalized seizures, including tonic-clonic seizures and seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.", "Children (2 to 12 years of age): Lamictal is indicated as adjunctive or monotherapy for the treatment of partial seizures and generalized seizures, including tonic-clonic seizures and seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.", "Following successful control of epilepsy with adjunctive therapy, concomitant antiepileptic medications may be withdrawn and patients continued on Lamictal monotherapy.", "Lamictal is indicated for monotherapy of typical absence seizures.", "Bipolar Disorder:", "Adults (18 years of age and older): Lamictal is indicated for the prevention of mood episode recurrences in patients with bipolar disorder, primarily preventing depressive episodes."], "sideEffects": ["Common, very common: ADR > 1/100", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Skin rash.", "Psychiatric disorders: Irritability, agitation.", "Nervous system disorders: Headache, somnolence, insomnia, dizziness, tremor.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.", "General disorders and administration site conditions: Fatigue.", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Arthralgia, back pain.", "Rare, very rare: ADR < 1/1000", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, alopecia.", "Blood and lymphatic system disorders: Leukopenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia, lymphadenopathy, hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis.", "Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity syndrome.", "Psychiatric disorders: Tics, hallucinations, confusion, nightmares.", "Nervous system disorders: Ataxia, nystagmus, aseptic meningitis, choreoathetosis, extrapyramidal effects, exacerbation of Parkinson's symptoms, increased seizure frequency.", "Eye disorders: Diplopia, blurred vision, conjunctivitis.", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Liver function abnormalities, hepatic failure.", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Lupus-like reactions."], "effectsOnDriving": "Two studies in volunteers demonstrated that the drug had no significant effect on fine visual-motor coordination, eye movements, body sway, or subjective sedation compared to placebo.\\nNeurological adverse reactions such as dizziness and diplopia have been reported. Therefore, patients should evaluate how the treatment affects them prior to driving or operating machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Lamotrigine is a use-dependent blocker of voltage-gated sodium channels. It inhibits sustained, rapid firing of neurons and suppresses pathological release of glutamate (an amino acid playing a key role in the generation of epileptic seizures), as well as inhibiting the bursts of action potentials that release glutamate."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Lamotrigine is rapidly and completely absorbed from the gut with no significant first-pass metabolism. Plasma protein binding is approximately 55%; displacement from plasma proteins is unlikely to cause toxicity.", "metabolism": "UDP-glucuronyl transferases are the enzymes identified as responsible for the metabolism of lamotrigine.", "elimination": "Elimination of lamotrigine occurs primarily via metabolism with subsequent excretion of the glucuronide-conjugated metabolite in the urine. Less than 10% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the urine. Only about 2% of the drug metabolites are excreted in the feces."}, "contraindications": ["Lamictal is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to lamotrigine or to any of the excipients of the medication."], "manufacturingInfo": {"storage": "Do not store above 30°C. Store in a dry place."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Treatment of epilepsy:\\nAdults (over 12 years of age):\\nThe initial monotherapy dose is 25 mg once daily for 2 weeks, followed by 50 mg once daily for the next 2 weeks.\\nThen the dose should be increased, by a maximum of 50 to 100 mg every 1 to 2 weeks, until the optimal response is achieved.\\nThe usual maintenance dose to achieve an optimal response is 100 to 200 mg/day given as a single dose or in two divided doses. Some patients require 500 mg of Lamictal/day to achieve the desired response.\\nChildren (2 years to 12 years of age):\\nTypical absence seizures:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.3 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nWeeks 3-4: 0.6 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 0.6 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 1-10 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 200 mg/day.\\nAdjunctive therapy with valproate:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.15 mg/kg/day (given once daily).\\nWeeks 3-4: 0.3 mg/kg/day (given once daily).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 0.3 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 1-5 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 200 mg/day.\\nAdjunctive therapy without valproate:\\nShould be used with phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, or other drugs that induce lamotrigine glucuronidation:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.6 mg/kg/day (given in 2 divided doses).\\nWeeks 3-4: 1.2 mg/kg/day (given in 2 divided doses).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 1.2 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 5-15 mg/kg/day (given in 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 400 mg/day.\\nShould be used with medications that do not significantly inhibit or induce lamotrigine glucuronidation:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.3 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nWeeks 3-4: 0.6 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 0.6 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 1-10 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 200 mg/day.\\nChildren under 2 years of age:\\nLamotrigine has not been studied as monotherapy in children under 2 years of age or as adjunctive therapy in children under 1 month of age.\\nThe safety and efficacy of lamotrigine in adjunctive therapy for partial seizures in children aged 1 month to 2 years have not been established. Therefore, Lamictal is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.\\nBipolar Disorder:\\nAdults (18 years of age and older):\\nAdjunctive therapy with glucuronidation inhibitors such as valproate:\\nWeeks 1-2: 12.5 mg (25 mg tablet taken every other day).\\nWeeks 3-4: 25 mg (once daily).\\nWeek 5: 50 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nTarget stabilization dose (Week 6): 100 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily) up to a maximum dose of 200 mg/day.\\nAdjunctive therapy with glucuronidation inducers in patients not taking valproate (this regimen should be used with phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, or other drugs that induce lamotrigine glucuronidation):\\nWeeks 1-2: 50 mg (once daily).\\nWeeks 3-4: 100 mg (given in 2 divided doses daily).\\nWeek 5: 200 mg (given in 2 divided doses daily).\\nTarget stabilization dose (Week 6): 300 mg/day, increasing to 400 mg/day if necessary in week 7 (given in 2 divided doses daily).\\nMonotherapy with Lamictal or adjunctive therapy in patients taking other drugs that do not significantly inhibit or induce lamotrigine glucuronidation:\\nWeeks 1-2: 25 mg (once daily).\\nWeeks 3-4: 50 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nWeek 5: 100 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nTarget stabilization dose (Week 6): 200 mg (range 100 to 400 mg) (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nChildren and adolescents (under 18 years of age):\\nNot recommended as safety and efficacy have not been established in this age group.\\nRenal impairment:\\nPlasma concentrations of lamotrigine were not significantly altered in single-dose studies in end-stage renal failure patients. However, accumulation of the glucuronide metabolite is anticipated; caution should be exercised in treating patients with renal impairment.\\nNote: The dosages above are for reference only. Specific dosing depends on the patient's physical condition and disease progression. Consult a physician or healthcare professional for an appropriate dosage.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and resume the normal dosing schedule. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Tablets should be swallowed whole and should not be chewed or crushed.\\nIf the calculated dose cannot be divided into lower-strength tablets (e.g., for young children with epilepsy or patients with hepatic impairment), the dose should be rounded to the nearest lower available tablet strength.", "overdoseManagement": "Acute ingestions of doses ranging from 10 to 20 times the maximum therapeutic dose have been reported, including fatal cases.\\nSymptoms of overdose include nystagmus, ataxia, altered consciousness, grand mal convulsions, and coma. QRS widening has also been observed in overdose patients.\\nIn case of overdose, the patient should be hospitalized and receive appropriate supportive care based on clinical manifestations or as directed by the national poison control center."}, "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Lamotrigine 25mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Lamotrigine passes into breast milk in highly variable amounts, resulting in infant serum lamotrigine concentrations of up to approximately 50% of the mother's serum concentration.\\nConsequently, in some breastfed infants, serum concentrations of lamotrigine may reach levels where pharmacological effects occur. The benefits of breastfeeding should be weighed against the potential risk of adverse effects in the infant.", "pediatric": "Lamotrigine has not been studied as monotherapy in children under 2 years of age or as adjunctive therapy in children under 1 month of age. The safety and efficacy of lamotrigine in adjunctive therapy for partial seizures in children aged 1 month to 2 years have not been established. Therefore, Lamictal is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Postmarketing data from more than 8,700 women exposed to lamotrigine monotherapy during the first trimester of pregnancy do not suggest a substantial increase in the risk of major congenital malformations, although data from some registries noted an increased risk of isolated oral clefts, whereas a completed case-control study showed no increased risk compared to other major birth defects.\\nLamictal should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.\\nPhysiological changes during pregnancy may affect lamotrigine concentrations and/or therapeutic efficacy. Decreased lamotrigine blood levels during pregnancy have been reported. Appropriate clinical management should be ensured for pregnant women receiving Lamictal."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Skin rashes have commonly been reported within 8 weeks of starting Lamictal treatment, with the majority being mild and self-limiting. However, serious rashes requiring hospitalization, such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, have also been reported.", "Caution should be exercised when administering the drug to patients with a history of allergy or rash to other antiepileptic drugs.", "Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis has been reported in patients taking Lamictal.", "Depressive symptoms and/or bipolar disorder can occur in patients with epilepsy, and there is evidence of an increased risk of suicide in patients with epilepsy and bipolar disorder.", "Patients being treated for bipolar disorder with Lamictal should be closely monitored for clinical worsening (including development of new symptoms) and suicidal ideation.", "Patients taking combined oral contraceptives generally require a higher maintenance dose of lamotrigine (up to double) to achieve maximal therapeutic response. Furthermore, Lamictal may decrease the efficacy of hormonal contraceptives in some patients.", "Lamictal inhibits renal tubular secretion via OCT 2 proteins; co-administration with primary OCT 2 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index, such as dofetilide, is not recommended.", "Brugada-type ECG changes have been observed in patients treated with the drug. Caution is advised when prescribing to patients with Brugada syndrome.", "Abrupt discontinuation of Lamictal may provoke rebound seizures. Lamictal dosage should be tapered gradually over a period of 2 weeks unless safety concerns (such as a rash) require immediate withdrawal."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue use and notify a physician or go to the nearest medical facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:49:52.768	lamictal-25mg-lamotrigine-tablets-for-epilepsy-and-bipolar-disorder-3-blisters-x-10-tablets	\N
99	Clamoxyl 250mg Oral Powder for Suspension for Bacterial Infections (Box of 12 Sachets)	P00605	\N	\N	Glaxo Wellcome Production	France	72273.00	63600.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250728074531-0-P00605.png?versionId=S.6NyMjnuTU2O7eypjxJATVOA1VibftN	Clamoxyl 250mg, containing amoxicillin, is an oral powder for suspension indicated for the treatment of susceptible bacterial infections involving the respiratory, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary tracts, Lyme disease, and endocarditis prophylaxis, as well as component of H. pylori eradication therapy.	{"indications": ["Treatment of infections caused by susceptible pathogens in the upper and lower respiratory tracts, gastrointestinal tract, and genitourinary tract", "Lyme disease", "Endocarditis prophylaxis", "Skin and soft tissue infections, osteomyelitis, and infections of the gallbladder and biliary tract", "Severe systemic bacterial infections", "Adjunctive therapy in combination with proton pump inhibitors and other antimicrobial agents for Helicobacter pylori eradication"], "sideEffects": ["Blood and lymphatic system disorders: Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, hemolytic anemia, prolongation of bleeding time and prothrombin time", "Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity reactions, angioneurotic edema, anaphylactic shock, serum sickness, hypersensitivity vasculitis", "Nervous system disorders: Hyperkinesia, dizziness, aseptic convulsions", "Infections and infestations: Mucocutaneous candidiasis", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, antibiotic-associated colitis (including pseudomembranous colitis), black hairy tongue", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, transient elevation of serum transaminases (AST and ALT)", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Maculopapular rash, urticaria, pruritus, erythema multiforme, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, bullous exfoliative dermatitis, acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP)", "Renal and urinary disorders: Interstitial nephritis, crystalluria"], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Probenecid", "effect": "Decreases renal tubular secretion of amoxicillin, resulting in increased and prolonged serum concentrations."}, {"drug": "Allopurinol", "effect": "Concurrent use increases the incidence of cutaneous rash and hypersensitivity reactions."}, {"drug": "Tetracyclines and other bacteriostatic agents", "effect": "May antagonize the bactericidal efficacy of amoxicillin."}, {"drug": "Oral contraceptives", "effect": "May reduce the efficacy of estrogen-containing oral contraceptives, potentially leading to breakthrough bleeding or contraceptive failure."}, {"drug": "Acenocoumarol or warfarin", "effect": "May abnormally prolong prothrombin time (elevated INR); appropriate monitoring is recommended when co-administered."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No known or observed effects on the ability to drive or operate machinery.", "contraindications": ["Contraindicated in patients with a known history of hypersensitivity to beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "GSK", "origin": "France", "storage": "Store below 30°C in a dry place", "packaging": "Box of 12 sachets", "manufacturer": "Glaxo Wellcome Production"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dosage is determined by patient age, body weight, renal function, and infection severity. The standard dosing regimen is divided into 3 administrations daily. Adults and pediatric patients weighing over 40 kg: 750 mg to 3 g daily (maximum 6 g daily). Pediatric patients weighing under 40 kg: 20 to 50 mg/kg/day (maximum 150 mg/kg/day). Renal dosage adjustment is mandatory in patients with renal impairment and those undergoing hemodialysis.", "administration": "For oral administration. Absorption of amoxicillin is not significantly impaired by the presence of food.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, fluid and electrolyte imbalance, and amoxicillin-induced crystalluria potentially progressing to renal failure. Treatment: Supportive and symptomatic care; amoxicillin is hemodialyzable."}, "generalDescription": "Clamoxyl 250mg, containing amoxicillin as the active pharmaceutical ingredient, is a broad-spectrum penicillin-class antibacterial indicated for the treatment of susceptible bacterial infections of the upper and lower respiratory tracts, gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts, skin and soft tissues, bones, and biliary tract, as well as for Lyme disease, endocarditis prophylaxis, and H. pylori eradication regimens.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Crospovidone, peach-lemon-strawberry flavoring agent, aspartame, magnesium stearate, lactose", "activeIngredients": "Amoxicillin 250 mg (supplied as amoxicillin trihydrate)"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Dosing adjustment is generally not required unless accompanied by compromised renal function.", "lactation": "Amoxicillin is excreted in breast milk in small quantities; use with caution and monitor the breastfed infant for potential sensitization or gastrointestinal disturbances.", "pregnancy": "Use only if clearly indicated after a formal risk-benefit assessment by the treating physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["A comprehensive clinical evaluation regarding prior hypersensitivity reactions to penicillins or cephalosporins must be conducted prior to initiation.", "Discontinue therapy immediately and institute emergency management if severe hypersensitivity or anaphylactic reactions occur.", "Avoid use if infectious mononucleosis is clinically suspected, due to the high incidence of rash associated with amoxicillin use in these patients.", "Prolonged or repeated therapy may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms or superinfection.", "Monitor for signs of pseudomembranous colitis in patients presenting with severe or persistent diarrhea.", "Exercise clinical caution and monitor coagulation parameters when co-administered with oral anticoagulants.", "Ensure adequate hydration and urinary output to minimize the risk of amoxicillin-associated crystalluria.", "Contains aspartame; use with caution in patients with phenylketonuria (PKU)."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Report any unexpected adverse reactions or hypersensitivity symptoms to a qualified healthcare professional promptly."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:18:59.977	clamoxyl-250mg-oral-powder-for-suspension-for-bacterial-infections-box-of-12-sachets	246
108	Lipitor (Atorvastatin Calcium) 10 mg Oral Tablets for Hypercholesterolemia and Dyslipidemia (3 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P00409	\N	\N	Pfizer	USA	527848.00	417000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520034551-0-P00409.png?versionId=L40bA41UE1Z7dxwJEvviNLGzKNCQUNzi	Lipitor 10 mg oral tablets contain atorvastatin, an HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor (statin) indicated for the treatment of hypercholesterolemia, dyslipidemia, and reduction of cardiovascular event risks.	{"indications": ["Treatment of primary hypercholesterolemia", "Treatment of mixed dyslipidemia", "Reduction in the risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular revascularization procedures"], "sideEffects": ["Common: Nasopharyngitis, allergic reactions, hyperglycemia, headache, pharyngolaryngeal pain, epistaxis, constipation, flatulence, dyspepsia, nausea, diarrhea, myalgia, arthralgia, pain in extremity, muscle spasms, joint swelling, back pain, liver function test abnormal, blood creatine phosphokinase increased.", "Uncommon: Hypoglycemia, weight gain, anorexia, nightmares, insomnia, dizziness, paresthesia, hypoesthesia, dysgeusia, vision blurred, tinnitus, vomiting, abdominal pain, eructation, pancreatitis, hepatitis, urticaria, skin rash, pruritus, alopecia, muscle fatigue, fatigue, asthenia, chest pain, peripheral edema, pyrexia, white blood cells urine positive.", "Rare: Thrombocytopenia, peripheral neuropathy, visual disturbance, cholestasis, angioedema, bullous dermatitis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, myopathy, myositis, rhabdomyolysis, tendon injury.", "Very rare: Anaphylaxis, hearing loss, hepatic failure, gynecomastia.", "Frequency unknown: Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ciclosporin, clarithromycin, itraconazole, HIV protease inhibitors)", "effect": "Increased plasma concentration of atorvastatin, markedly elevating the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis."}, {"drug": "Gemfibrozil, fibric acid derivatives, ezetimibe", "effect": "Increased risk of muscle-related adverse events, including rhabdomyolysis."}, {"drug": "Fusidic acid", "effect": "Increased risk of severe rhabdomyolysis; temporary discontinuation of atorvastatin is required during fusidic acid therapy."}, {"drug": "Digoxin", "effect": "Slightly increased steady-state plasma concentrations of digoxin."}, {"drug": "Oral contraceptives", "effect": "Increased plasma concentrations of norethindrone and ethinyl estradiol."}, {"drug": "Warfarin", "effect": "Prothrombin time monitoring is required upon initiation or dose adjustment."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Negligible influence on the ability to drive and use machines; however, caution is advised if dizziness occurs.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to the active substance or any ingredient of the formulation.", "Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations of serum transaminases exceeding 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN).", "Pregnant women, nursing mothers, and women of childbearing potential not utilizing effective contraception."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Pfizer", "origin": "USA", "storage": "Store below 30°C.", "packaging": "Box of 3 blisters x 10 tablets", "manufacturer": "Pfizer Pharmaceuticals LLC"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Initial dose ranges from 10 mg to 80 mg once daily, individualized according to baseline LDL-C levels, therapeutic goals, and patient response. Lipid panels should be analyzed within 2 to 4 weeks of initiation to adjust dosage.", "missedDose": "Administer the missed dose as soon as remembered. If it is close to the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose. Do not double the dose.", "administration": "Administered orally once daily at any time of day, with or without food.", "overdoseManagement": "No specific antidote exists. Provide symptomatic treatment and supportive measures as clinically indicated. Monitor hepatic function and serum CK levels. Hemodialysis does not significantly enhance drug clearance."}, "generalDescription": "Lipitor 10 mg is a lipid-lowering medication indicated to reduce serum cholesterol levels and prevent cardiovascular diseases. The active substance, Atorvastatin, belongs to the statin drug class and acts by competitively inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase, thereby suppressing hepatic cholesterol synthesis.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Calcium carbonate, microcrystalline cellulose, lactose monohydrate, croscarmellose sodium, polysorbate 80, hydroxypropyl cellulose, magnesium stearate, film coating (containing hydroxypropyl methylcellulose, polyethylene glycol 8000, titanium dioxide, talc), simethicone emulsion (containing simethicone, stearate emulsifiers, thickeners, benzoic acid, sorbic acid, purified water).", "activeIngredients": "Atorvastatin-Hemi calcium 1.5 H2O equivalent to Atorvastatin 10 mg."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Safety and efficacy profiles are comparable to the general adult population.", "lactation": "Contraindicated.", "pediatric": "Starting dose is 10 mg/day, titrated up to 20 mg/day depending on clinical response.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Perform liver enzyme tests prior to the initiation of therapy and periodically thereafter.", "Exercise caution in patients who consume substantial quantities of alcohol or have a history of hepatic disease.", "Risk of hemorrhagic stroke in patients with prior hemorrhagic stroke or lacunar infarction.", "Skeletal muscle effects: Myalgia, myositis, myopathy, and rhabdomyolysis risk (especially with higher dosages or concomitant drug interactions).", "Interstitial lung disease (presenting as dyspnea, non-productive cough, and fatigue).", "Increased risk of elevated blood glucose levels and new-onset diabetes mellitus."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:19:30.377	lipitor-atorvastatin-calcium-10-mg-oral-tablets-for-hypercholesterolemia-and-dyslipidemia-3	143
128	Thai Duong Menthol, Methyl Salicylate, and Ginger with Essential Oils Topical Liniment for Cold, Flu, Motion Sickness, and Musculoskeletal Pain (Bottle of 6mL)	P04239	\N	\N	Sao Thai Duong Joint Stock Company - Ha Nam Branch	Vietnam	40541.00	30000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20241107084417-0-P04239.png?versionId=TFWVhOacjXNzkdpvVC6GInLn.VUa.fqY	Thai Duong Ginger and Essential Oils Topical Liniment is an herbal formulation indicated for the symptomatic relief of common cold, influenza, motion sickness, nasal congestion, and dizziness. It provides warming and anti-inflammatory effects, aids in nasal decongestion, and alleviates associated discomfort and fatigue. Supplied in a 6mL bottle.	{"indications": ["Symptomatic relief of various aches and pains.", "Relief of cold extremities, numbness, fatigue, and as an adjunct in the prevention of rheumatic conditions.", "Relief of symptoms associated with influenza, 'wind-cold' syndrome (Traditional Medicine classification), and the common cold.", "Relief of motion sickness, nausea, and cold-induced abdominal pain.", "Relief of pruritus and discomfort from mosquito bites and insect stings."], "sideEffects": ["In the event of adverse reactions, discontinue use immediately and consult a physician or seek prompt medical attention at the nearest healthcare facility."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Other medications", "effect": "No clinically significant drug interactions have been observed to date."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Effects on ability to drive and operate machinery: No reports of adverse effects.", "contraindications": ["Pediatric patients under 30 months of age.", "Children with a history of epilepsy or febrile seizures."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Sao Thai Duong", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place, protected from direct sunlight, at temperatures not exceeding 30°C (86°F).", "packaging": "Carton box containing 1 bottle of 6 mL topical liniment.", "manufacturer": "Sao Thai Duong Joint Stock Company - Ha Nam Branch"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "For musculoskeletal aches and pains: Apply topically and massage into the forehead, temples, back muscles, shoulders, and neck. For cold extremities: Massage into the soles of the feet and palms of the hands, or add 10 to 20 drops to a basin of hot water for a foot soak. For cold and influenza symptoms: Massage into the forehead, temples, palms, wrists, shoulders, and neck; traditional coining ('cao gio') or scraping ('danh cam') techniques may be performed; or add 10 to 40 drops to hot water for steam inhalation. For motion sickness and abdominal pain: Apply topically to the abdomen, umbilical region, hypogastric area, and wrists. For insect bites: Apply sparingly to the affected dermal area.", "administration": "For external topical massage, therapeutic foot soak, or vapor inhalation."}, "generalDescription": "Thai Duong Ginger and Essential Oils Topical Liniment (6 mL) is a botanical extract formulation traditionally used to alleviate symptoms of common cold, influenza, rhinorrhea, nasal congestion, dizziness, and motion sickness. The formulation provides local warming effects, reduces inflammation, and facilitates nasal decongestion, thereby mitigating associated discomfort and fatigue.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Paraffin oil, Lily fragrance q.s. (quantum satis) to 6 mL.", "activeIngredients": "Menthol 1.74 g, Peppermint oil 0.24 mL, Cajuput oil 0.24 mL, Methyl salicylate 1.2 g, Camphor oil 0.12 mL, White basil oil 0.06 mL, Cinnamon oil 0.12 mL, Ginger extract (equivalent to ~2.2 mL extract) 3 g."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Use during lactation should be determined and advised by a physician based on an individual clinical evaluation.", "pregnancy": "Use during pregnancy should be determined and advised by a physician based on an individual risk-benefit assessment."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["For external use only. Do not ingest. Avoid contact with mucous membranes, eyes, and broken or open wounds."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Discontinue administration immediately and consult your physician or seek professional medical evaluation at the nearest healthcare facility."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:20:36.38	thai-duong-menthol-methyl-salicylate-and-ginger-with-essential-oils-topical-liniment-for-cold-flu	247
139	Drosperin (Drospirenone 3 mg / Ethinyl Estradiol 0.03 mg) Film-Coated Tablets for Oral Contraception and Hormonal Regulation (Box of 28 Tablets)	P04120	\N	\N	Abbott	Vietnam	209459.00	155000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520040021-0-P04120.png?versionId=FmNTp10KJNYC4E6e_L6AOIM5PhV5vpMS	Drosperin film-coated tablets contain drospirenone and ethinyl estradiol, indicated for oral contraception and endocrine regulation.	{"indications": ["Oral contraception", "Hormone replacement therapy for postmenopausal conditions", "Management of amenorrhea and oligomenorrhea secondary to ovarian dysfunction"], "sideEffects": ["Serious adverse reactions requiring immediate medical attention: acute abdominal pain; hemoptysis; severe headache; acute focal neurological deficits, visual disturbances or transient vision loss; thoracic, inguinal, or lower extremity pain; dyspnea; asthenia, paresthesia, or pain in the upper and lower extremities.", "Adverse effects requiring physician consultation: alterations in menstrual bleeding patterns, intermenstrual spotting, hypomenorrhea, abnormal vaginal bleeding, menorrhagia, secondary amenorrhea; migraine headaches, arterial hypertension, vulvovaginal candidiasis; depressive disorders; epigastric tenderness; icterus and scleral icterus.", "Common adverse reactions: mastalgia, breast tenderness, and increased tactile sensitivity; dizziness; nausea; peripheral edema (ankles and feet); chloasma on sun-exposed skin; hypertrichosis or alopecia; libido fluctuations; cutaneous photosensitivity."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Potassium-elevating agents, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplementation, angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs), heparin", "effect": "Increased risk of hyperkalemia."}, {"drug": "Enzyme-inducing antibiotics, barbiturates, phenytoin, carbamazepine, griseofulvin, primidone, rifampin, phenylbutazone, warfarin, hydrocortisone, prednisolone, cyclosporine, clofibrate, theophylline, morphine, salicylic acid, temazepam, atorvastatin, acetaminophen, ascorbic acid, St. John's Wort (Hypericum perforatum)", "effect": "May diminish contraceptive efficacy or alter the pharmacokinetic profile of co-administered agents."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No formal studies on the effects on the ability to drive and use machines have been performed; however, patients should exercise caution if experiencing adverse reactions such as dizziness or visual disturbances.", "contraindications": ["Vascular disease, thromboembolic disorders, coagulation abnormalities, active thrombosis.", "Cardiovascular disease, cerebrovascular hemorrhage.", "Known or suspected hormone-sensitive malignancies, including breast cancer, cervical cancer, endometrial cancer, and uterine cancer.", "Severe hepatic impairment, benign or malignant hepatic neoplasms.", "History of cholestatic jaundice during pregnancy or prior use of combined hormonal contraceptives.", "Severe renal impairment, adrenal insufficiency.", "Undiagnosed abnormal uterine or vaginal bleeding.", "Known or suspected pregnancy.", "Hypersensitivity to drospirenone, ethinyl estradiol, or any excipients contained within the formulation."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Abbott", "storage": "Store in the original packaging at room temperature, in a dry place, protected from light.", "packaging": "Carton containing 1 blister pack of 28 film-coated tablets (comprising 21 active beige tablets and 7 inactive white tablets).", "manufacturer": "Abbott"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "One tablet daily. Management of missed doses: If one active tablet is delayed by less than 12 hours, contraceptive protection is not reduced; take the missed tablet as soon as remembered and continue subsequent tablets at the usual time. If one active tablet is delayed by more than 12 hours, contraceptive protection may be reduced: take the missed tablet immediately (even if it means taking two tablets simultaneously) and use back-up barrier contraception for the next 7 days. If two consecutive active tablets are missed during Week 1 or Week 2, take two tablets on the day they are remembered and two on the following day, then continue with barrier contraception for 7 days. If two consecutive active tablets are missed in Week 3 or more than three tablets are missed across the first three weeks, discard the remaining blister pack, initiate a new pack immediately, and utilize barrier contraception for 7 days.", "administration": "Administer one tablet orally once daily at the same time each day for 28 consecutive days in the sequential order indicated on the blister pack housed within the compact case. Initiation begins with the first beige active tablet on Day 1 of the menstrual cycle. Upon completion of the active beige tablets, continue with the inactive white tablets.", "overdoseManagement": "In the event of acute overdosage, symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, and withdrawal bleeding in females. Management should consist of symptomatic and supportive care under medical supervision."}, "generalDescription": "Drosperin tablets, manufactured by Abbott, contain drospirenone and ethinyl estradiol as active pharmaceutical ingredients. This medicinal product is indicated for daily oral contraception, postmenopausal hormone replacement therapy, and the management of menstrual disorders such as amenorrhea and oligomenorrhea resulting from ovarian dysfunction.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Active beige tablets excipients: maize starch, povidone, lactose monohydrate, croscarmellose sodium, talc, magnesium stearate, hypromellose 2910, macrogol 6000, titanium dioxide, iron oxide yellow. Inactive white tablets excipients: microcrystalline cellulose, sodium starch glycolate, lactose monohydrate, talc, magnesium stearate, hypromellose 2910, macrogol 6000, titanium dioxide.", "activeIngredients": "Each active beige film-coated tablet contains 3 mg of drospirenone and 0.03 mg (30 mcg) of ethinyl estradiol."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Not recommended during lactation, as combined hormonal contraceptives may reduce breast milk production and trace amounts of active steroids excreted into breast milk may adversely affect the nursing infant.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated during pregnancy. If pregnancy occurs or is suspected during administration, discontinue treatment immediately and consult a physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Utilize additional non-hormonal contraception during the first 7 days of initial cycle administration, following missed doses, or when co-administered with enzyme-inducing medicinal products that reduce contraceptive efficacy.", "Tobacco smoking cessation is strongly advised during treatment with combined hormonal contraceptives, particularly in women over 35 years of age.", "Minimize prolonged ultraviolet or sun exposure due to the potential for photosensitivity reactions.", "Discontinue treatment and inform attending physicians or dental surgeons prior to elective scheduled surgeries or immobilization periods.", "In the event of severe gastrointestinal disturbances (e.g., vomiting or acute diarrhea), absorption may be incomplete, and supplementary barrier contraception should be employed.", "Routine clinical evaluations and gynecological monitoring are required during long-term therapy."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:21:12.866	drosperin-drospirenone-3-mg-ethinyl-estradiol-0-03-mg-film-coated-tablets-for-oral-contraception	3
