m levels and renal function (serum creatinine) during long-term therapy.", "Use with caution in patients with renal impairment, sarcoidosis, and immobilized patients with osteoporosis.", "Monitor serum calcium and phosphate levels in patients with renal impairment.", "Take into account the vitamin D content (400 IU) when prescribing other medications containing vitamin D.", "Exercise caution in patients with severe renal failure receiving aluminum-containing preparations due to increased aluminum absorption.", "Contains sodium (82.14 mg per tablet): To be taken into consideration for patients on a controlled sodium diet.", "Contains aspartame: May be harmful for individuals with phenylketonuria."]}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:49:59.206	calsource-600mg-400iu-effervescent-tablets-for-calcium-and-vitamin-d3-supplementation-prevention	377
1737	Emzinc 20 mg Zinc Acetate Tablets for Acute Diarrhea and Zinc Deficiency (Box of 100 Tablets)	P02531	\N	\N	Emzinc	Vietnam	336047.00	289000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250508070550-0-P02531.jpg?versionId=25m6B.x4J8xXH36Cjy3KhkYR7oo2Aec2	Emzinc is an oral zinc acetate formulation indicated for the management of acute diarrhea in conjunction with oral rehydration therapy and for the treatment of zinc deficiency.	{"indications": ["Treatment of acute diarrhea in conjunction with oral rehydration therapy.", "Treatment and prevention of zinc deficiency."], "sideEffects": ["At the recommended doses of 10 mg/day for infants under 6 months of age and 20 mg/day for children over 6 months, there are no reported adverse effects or side effects of zinc when administered alone, with vitamins, or with oral rehydration salts in severely malnourished patients or immunocompromised individuals. However, zinc overdose may lead to copper deficiency. Patients should report any adverse effects experienced during treatment to their physician.", "This is not a comprehensive list of all potential side effects, and others may occur. If you experience any adverse reactions, discontinue use immediately and notify your doctor or visit the nearest medical facility for timely management."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Iron and calcium supplements, tetracyclines, and phosphorus-containing compounds", "effect": "Absorption of zinc may be decreased."}, {"drug": "Zinc", "effect": "May decrease the gastrointestinal absorption of iron, tetracyclines, and fluoroquinolones."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No formal studies on the effects on the ability to drive and operate machinery have been performed.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Zinc is an essential micronutrient widely present in the human body, playing a crucial role in numerous metabolic processes, immune functions, and mucosal nutrition. Zinc reduces ion secretion, inhibits nitric oxide (NO) synthesis, and improves appetite, nutrient absorption, small intestinal enterocyte regeneration, intestinal enzyme recovery, intestinal permeability, body fluids, and immune cells.\\nOxidative damage generated by free radicals and nitric oxide (NO) is responsible for certain intestinal malabsorption syndromes and diarrhea. Under physiological conditions or mild stimulation of NO synthesis, the small intestine functions in an optimal absorption state. However, excessive NO production promotes the formation of cyclic nucleotides, which induce excretion and malabsorption.\\nStudies indicate that soluble zinc chelate complexes can modulate the NO-elevating effects in the small intestine. Recent research demonstrates that zinc inhibits adenosine 3',5'-cyclic monophosphate (cAMP)-regulated potassium channels, thereby regulating chloride secretion, while having minimal effects on calcium (Ca2+) and guanosine 3',5'-cyclic monophosphate (cGMP)-regulated chloride secretion. Zinc is recognized as a potential antioxidant providing both immediate and long-term protective effects."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Zinc is absorbed in the small intestine, and its absorption pharmacokinetics tend to saturate as the dose increases. The percentage of zinc absorbed does not correlate directly with the amount administered.\\nIn the bloodstream, approximately 80% of absorbed zinc is distributed to erythrocytes, with the majority of the remaining fraction bound to albumin and other plasma proteins.", "elimination": "The liver is the primary storage site for zinc. Zinc is excreted mainly via feces, with relatively small amounts eliminated through urine and sweat. Fecal excretion serves as a major elimination route not only for unabsorbed dietary zinc but also for endogenous zinc secreted from the intestinal tract."}, "contraindications": ["Zinc acetate tablets are contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to zinc acetate or any component of the formulation."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Emzinc", "storage": "Store at controlled room temperature below 25°C (77°F), protected from moisture and direct light. Do not store in the bathroom or freezer. Storage requirements may vary by product formulation; carefully read the storage instructions on the packaging or consult your pharmacist.\\nKeep out of reach of children and pets.\\nShelf life: 24 months from the date of manufacture."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "The recommended dose for managing diarrhea in infants under 6 months of age is 10 mg of elemental zinc once daily for 10-14 consecutive days. The recommended dose for managing diarrhea in children older than 6 months is 20 mg of elemental zinc once daily for 10-14 consecutive days.\\nNote: The above dosages are for reference only. The specific dosage depends on the patient's clinical condition and disease progression. To obtain an appropriate dosage regimen, consult a physician or healthcare professional.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, take it as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and resume the regular dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to make up for a missed one.", "administration": "For oral administration.", "overdoseManagement": "Do not exceed the prescribed dosage. Taking more medication will not improve symptoms; instead, it may cause acute toxicity or serious adverse effects. If an overdose of Emzinc is suspected, go to the nearest hospital emergency room or medical center immediately. Bring the medicine box, container, or label to provide healthcare providers with necessary information.\\nAcute oral overdose with inorganic zinc salts is rarely reported in humans. In the event of an overdose, unabsorbed zinc should be removed from the stomach by gastric lavage as quickly as clinically indicated. Plasma zinc levels should be measured, and chelation therapy with heavy metal chelators should be considered if plasma zinc levels are significantly elevated (>1000 mcg/dL). Additionally, any clinical signs or symptoms of toxicity should receive appropriate supportive care."}, "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Excipients q.s. to 1 tablet", "activeIngredients": "Zinc Acetate equivalent to elemental zinc 20 mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Lactation: Zinc is excreted in human breast milk, and zinc-induced secondary copper deficiency in breastfed infants may theoretically occur. Therefore, caution should be exercised, and alternative infant feeding methods or cessation of breastfeeding may be considered during maternal treatment with high-dose zinc acetate.", "pediatric": "The recommended dose for controlling acute diarrhea in children under 6 months of age is 10 mg of elemental zinc once daily for 10-14 consecutive days. The recommended dose for controlling diarrhea in children older than 6 months is 20 mg of elemental zinc once daily for 10-14 consecutive days.", "pregnancy": "Pregnancy: Clinical studies conducted in pregnant women have not shown that zinc acetate increases the risk of congenital malformations when administered across all three trimesters. If this medication is used during pregnancy, the potential for fetal harm is considered very low; however, because studies cannot entirely rule out potential risks, zinc acetate should only be used during pregnancy when clearly clinically indicated."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Zinc is a copper antagonist. Similar to other copper antagonists, treatment with zinc acetate carries a risk of secondary copper deficiency. This is particularly harmful to pediatric patients, as copper is essential for normal growth, hematopoiesis, and neurological development."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Report any adverse drug reactions encountered during treatment promptly to your attending physician."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:51:05.754	emzinc-20-mg-zinc-acetate-tablets-for-acute-diarrhea-and-zinc-deficiency-box-of-100-tablets	381
