dyslipidemia associated with type 2 diabetes mellitus.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Hypromellose, sodium docusate, sucrose, sodium lauryl sulfate, lactose monohydrate, microcrystalline cellulose, anhydrous colloidal silica, crospovidone, magnesium stearate, polyvinyl alcohol, titanium dioxide (E171), talc, soybean lecithin, xanthan gum.", "activeIngredients": "Fenofibrate (INN) 145mg, formulated as nanoparticles."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "No dosage adjustment necessary for elderly patients without renal impairment.", "lactation": "Contraindicated.", "pediatric": "Contraindicated.", "pregnancy": "Should only be used after a rigorous benefit-risk assessment."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Address secondary causes of hypercholesterolemia prior to initiating fenofibrate therapy.", "Monitor serum lipids, transaminases, creatinine, and complete blood count periodically.", "Discontinue therapy if muscle toxicity develops (myalgia, muscle weakness, CPK levels exceeding 5 times the upper limit of normal).", "Exercise caution when administered concomitantly with coumarin anticoagulants, HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, cyclosporin, colchicine, or bile acid sequestrants.", "Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should consult a physician.", "Risks include gallstones, pancreatitis, and severe high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) reductions."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If any adverse reactions occur during treatment with the medication, discontinue use immediately and notify a physician or report to the nearest medical facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:46:39.507	lipanthyl-nt-145mg-tablets-containing-fenofibrate-for-hypercholesterolemia-and-reduction-of	250
1651	Ultracet 37.5mg/325mg Tablets for Moderate to Severe Pain Management (3 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P00969	\N	\N	\N	Vietnam	284810.00	225000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250508070602-0-P00969.jpg?versionId=8PEChhlhlcTkHRsEWZSN78g7vW3gTD5_	Ultracet 37.5mg/325mg Tablets are indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute pain (3 blisters x 10 tablets).	{"sideEffects": ["When using Ultracet, patients may experience adverse drug reactions (ADRs). These events most frequently involve the central nervous system and the gastrointestinal tract, with nausea, dizziness, and somnolence being the most commonly reported.", "Additionally, the following adverse reactions have been frequently noted, albeit with lower overall frequencies: General disorders: Asthenia, fatigue, hot flushes. Central and peripheral nervous system: Headache, tremor. Gastrointestinal system: Abdominal pain, constipation, diarrhea, dyspepsia, flatulence, dry mouth, vomiting. Psychiatric disorders: Anorexia, anxiety, confusion, euphoria, insomnia, nervousness, restlessness. Skin and appendages: Pruritus, rash, increased sweating.", "Clinically significant adverse reactions less frequently reported with Ultracet include: Body as a whole: Chest pain, rigors, syncope, withdrawal syndrome. Cardiovascular disorders: Hypertension, aggravation of hypertension, hypotension. Central and peripheral nervous system: Ataxia, convulsions, hypertonia, migraine, aggravation of migraine, involuntary muscle contractions, paresthesia, stupor, vertigo. Gastrointestinal system: Dysphagia, melena, tongue edema. Ear and labyrinth disorders: Tinnitus. Heart rate and rhythm disorders: Arrhythmia, palpitation, tachycardia. Liver and biliary system: Abnormal hepatic function tests. Metabolic and nutritional disorders: Weight loss. Psychiatric disorders: Amnesia, depersonalization, depression, drug abuse, emotional lability, hallucinations, impotence, nightmares, suicidal ideation. Red blood cell disorders: Anemia. Respiratory system: Dyspnea. Urinary system: Albuminuria, micturition disorder, oliguria, urinary retention. Vision disorders: Abnormal vision.", "Other clinically significant adverse effects of tramadol hydrochloride reported in clinical trials or post-marketing surveillance include: Orthostatic hypotension, allergic reactions (including anaphylaxis, urticaria, Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis), cognitive dysfunction, suicidal ideation, and hepatitis. Laboratory abnormalities include increased creatinine. Serotonin syndrome (symptoms of which may include fever, agitation, tremor, and irritability) occurs when tramadol is co-administered with serotonergic agents such as selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and MAO inhibitors.", "Post-marketing experience with tramadol-containing products reveals rare reports of delirium, miosis, mydriasis, and speech disorders, and very rarely movement disorders. Post-marketing monitoring indicates that tramadol rarely alters the effect of warfarin, including prolongation of prothrombin time. Hypoglycemia has been reported very rarely in patients receiving tramadol. Most of these patients had risk factors, including diabetes mellitus, renal impairment, or advanced age.", "Hyponatremia and/or SIADH (Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone Secretion) have been rarely reported with tramadol use, typically in patients with predisposing risk factors such as elderly patients or those concurrently taking medications associated with hyponatremia.", "Other clinically significant adverse effects of paracetamol reported in clinical trials or post-marketing data: Allergic reactions (primary skin rashes) or secondary hypersensitivity reactions caused by paracetamol are rare and can be managed by discontinuing the drug and instituting symptomatic treatment if necessary. Some reports indicate that paracetamol may cause hypoprothrombinemia when administered concomitantly with warfarin compounds. In other studies, prothrombin time remained unchanged."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Non-selective MAO inhibitors", "effect": "Risk of serotonin syndrome: Diarrhea, tachycardia, flushing, tremor, confusion, even coma."}, {"drug": "Selective MAO-A inhibitors", "effect": "Extrapolated from non-selective MAO inhibitors. Risk of serotonin syndrome: Diarrhea, tachycardia, flushing, tremor, confusion, even coma."}, {"drug": "Selective MAO-B inhibitors", "effect": "Central nervous system excitation symptoms suggestive of serotonin syndrome: Diarrhea, tachycardia, flushing, tremor, confusion, even coma. In patients newly treated with MAO inhibitors, delay treatment with tramadol for two weeks."}, {"drug": "Carbamazepine", "effect": "Concomitant administration of tramadol hydrochloride with carbamazepine significantly increases tramadol metabolism. Patients taking carbamazepine may experience a significant reduction in the analgesic efficacy of the tramadol component in Ultracet."}, {"drug": "Quinidine", "effect": "Tramadol is metabolized to M1 (the active metabolite) by CYP2D6. Concomitant administration of quinidine with tramadol results in increased tramadol concentrations. The clinical consequences of this finding are unknown."}, {"drug": "Warfarin and derivatives", "effect": "When medically appropriate, periodic evaluation of prothrombin time should be performed when Ultracet and these agents are co-administered, due to reports of increased INR (International Normalized Ratio) in some patients."}, {"drug": "CYP2D6 inhibitors (such as fluoxetine, paroxetine, and amitriptyline)", "effect": "May inhibit the metabolism of tramadol."}, {"drug": "Cimetidine", "effect": "Concomitant administration of Ultracet with cimetidine does not result in clinically significant changes in tramadol pharmacokinetics."}, {"drug": "Opioid receptor antagonists (buprenorphine, nalbuphine, pentazocine)", "effect": "Reduced analgesic efficacy due to competitive receptor blockade, leading to a risk of withdrawal syndrome."}, {"drug": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and triptans", "effect": "Risk of serotonin syndrome (confusion, agitation, fever, sweating, ataxia, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, and diarrhea)."}, {"drug": "Other opioid derivatives (including antitussive agents and substitution treatments), benzodiazepines, and barbiturates", "effect": "Increased risk of respiratory depression, which can be fatal in cases of overdose."}, {"drug": "Other centrally acting analgesics (other opioid derivatives, alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, other anxiolytics, hypnotics, sedating antidepressants, sedating antihistamines, neuroleptics, centrally acting antihypertensives, thalidomide, and baclofen)", "effect": "May cause additive central nervous system depression. Impairment of alertness can make driving and operating machinery hazardous."}, {"drug": "CYP3A4 inhibitors (such as ketoconazole and erythromycin)", "effect": "May inhibit the metabolism of tramadol (N-demethylation) and potentially inhibit the metabolism of the active O-demethylated metabolite. The clinical significance of this interaction has not been studied."}, {"drug": "Drugs that lower the seizure threshold (bupropion, SSRI antidepressants, tricyclic antidepressants, and neuroleptics)", "effect": "Concomitant use of tramadol with these agents may increase the risk of seizures."}, {"drug": "Metoclopramide or domperidone", "effect": "May increase the absorption rate of paracetamol."}, {"drug": "Cholestyramine", "effect": "Decreases the absorption of paracetamol."}, {"drug": "Ondansetron (5-HT3 antiemetic antagonist)", "effect": "Increases the requirement for tramadol in patients with postoperative pain."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Ultracet may impair mental and/or physical abilities required for the performance of potentially hazardous tasks such as driving a vehicle or operating machinery.", "contraindications": ["Patients with a known hypersensitivity to tramadol, paracetamol, any other component of this product, or to opioids.", "Cases of acute poisoning with alcohol, hypnotics, narcotics, centrally acting analgesics, opioids, or psychotropic drugs.", "Patients receiving monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors or within 14 days of discontinuing such therapy.", "Patients with severe hepatic impairment or epilepsy not controlled by treatment."], "manufacturingInfo": {"storage": "Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C in the original packaging."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Unless otherwise prescribed, Ultracet is dosed as follows: Adults and adolescents aged 16 years and older: The use of Ultracet should be restricted to patients with moderate to severe pain requiring combination therapy of paracetamol and tramadol. Dosage must be individualized based on pain severity and patient response. The maximum dose of Ultracet is 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed for pain relief, up to a maximum of 8 tablets per day. Ultracet may be taken regardless of food intake. If repeated use or long-term treatment with Ultracet is required due to the nature and severity of the condition, careful and regular monitoring should be undertaken, with periodic evaluation of the need for continued treatment. Children (under 16 years of age): Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established. Elderly patients: In general, safety and pharmacokinetic profiles do not differ significantly between patients aged - 65 years and younger patients. Dosage adjustment is generally not required in patients aged - 75 years without clinical manifestations of hepatic or renal impairment. In patients over 75 years of age, elimination may be prolonged; therefore, dosage intervals should be extended as appropriate based on the patient's clinical status. Renal impairment: In patients with creatinine clearance < 30 mL/min, it is recommended to extend the dosing interval of Ultracet so that the total dose does not exceed 2 tablets every 12 hours. Hepatic impairment: Ultracet is contraindicated in patients with severe hepatic impairment. In moderate hepatic impairment, careful consideration should be given to extending the dosing interval. Note: The dosages stated above are for reference only. Specific dosing depends on individual physical constitution and disease progression. Consult a physician or qualified healthcare professional for appropriate dosing.", "missedDose": "If you miss a dose, take it as soon as possible. However, if it is almost time for your next dose, skip the missed dose and resume your regular dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to make up for a missed one.", "administration": "Ultracet tablets are administered orally.", "overdoseManagement": "Ultracet is a combination product. Clinical manifestations of acute overdose may include signs and symptoms of tramadol toxicity, paracetamol toxicity, or both. Initial symptoms of tramadol overdose may include respiratory depression and/or seizures. Initial symptoms within the first 24 hours of paracetamol overdose include gastrointestinal irritation, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, malaise, pallor, and diaphoresis. Human Experience: Tramadol: Serious consequences of tramadol overdose include respiratory depression, somnolence, coma, seizures, cardiac arrest, and death. Paracetamol: Administration of very high doses of paracetamol may cause hepatotoxicity in some patients. Early symptoms following hepatotoxic overdose include gastrointestinal irritation, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, malaise, pallor, and diaphoresis. Clinical and laboratory evidence of hepatic toxicity may appear 48 to 72 hours post-ingestion. Management: Overdose of Ultracet may result in fatal multi-drug overdose; expert consultation is advised. Although naloxone reverses some, but not all, symptoms of tramadol overdose, the risk of seizures is also increased with naloxone administration. Based on experience with tramadol, hemodialysis is not recommended for managing overdose as it removes less than 7% of the administered dose over a 4-hour dialysis period. Primary management of Ultracet overdose consists of supporting adequate ventilation along with other supportive measures. Measures to reduce drug absorption should be implemented, such as mechanically induced emesis or administration of syrup of ipecac if the patient is alert (with intact laryngeal reflexes), followed by activated charcoal (1 g/kg) after gastric emptying. The first dose of charcoal should be given with a suitable cathartic; subsequent doses should alternate with cathartics. Hypotension is usually hypovolemic in origin and should be treated with fluid resuscitation. Vasopressors and other supportive measures should be employed as indicated. Endotracheal intubation should be performed prior to gastric lavage in unconscious patients and to support respiration. In adults and children, regardless of the quantity of paracetamol ingested, or if the ingestion time is uncertain, plasma paracetamol concentrations should be measured and treatment with N-acetylcysteine initiated. If assaying is unavailable and the estimated paracetamol ingestion exceeds 7.5 to 10 g in adults and adolescents, or 150 mg/kg in children, treatment with N-acetylcysteine should be initiated and continued throughout the full therapeutic course."}, "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Cellulose powder, pregelatinized starch, sodium starch glycolate, corn starch, purified water, magnesium stearate, light yellow Opadry, and carnauba wax.", "activeIngredients": "Tramadol Hydrochloride (37.5mg), Acetaminophen (Paracetamol) (325mg)"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "In general, safety and pharmacokinetic profiles do not differ significantly between patients aged - 65 years and younger patients. Dosage adjustment is typically unnecessary in patients aged - 75 years without clinical evidence of hepatic or renal impairment. In patients over 75 years of age, elimination may be prolonged; therefore, dosing intervals may be extended based on clinical condition.", "lactation": "Ultracet is not recommended for nursing mothers as the safety of tramadol in infants and neonates has not been established.", "pediatric": "The safety and efficacy of Ultracet in pediatric patients below 16 years of age have not been established.", "pregnancy": "Tramadol crosses the placenta. Adequate and well-controlled studies have not been conducted in pregnant women. Safe use during pregnancy has not been established. Tramadol should not be used in pregnant women."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Seizures: Seizures have been reported in patients receiving tramadol within the recommended dose range. Spontaneous post-marketing reports indicate that the risk of seizures increases with doses exceeding the recommended range. The risk of seizures is increased in patients taking tramadol concurrently with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs), and other tricyclics (e.g., cyclobenzaprine, promethazine), or opioids. Administration of tramadol may enhance the seizure risk in patients taking MAO inhibitors, neuroleptics, or other drugs that lower the seizure threshold. The risk may also be increased in patients with epilepsy, a history of seizures, or recognized risk factors for seizures (such as head trauma, metabolic disorders, alcohol or drug withdrawal, central nervous system infections). In tramadol overdoses, administration of naloxone may increase the seizure risk.", "Anaphylactoid reactions: Patients with a history of anaphylactoid reactions to codeine and other opioids may be at increased risk and therefore should not receive Ultracet.", "Respiratory depression: Administer Ultracet with caution in patients at risk for respiratory depression. Administration of high doses of tramadol with anesthetics, general anesthetics, or alcohol may cause respiratory depression. Manage such cases as an overdose. Caution is required if naloxone is used, as it may precipitate seizures.", "Ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolism of tramadol: Ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers convert tramadol to its active metabolite (M1) more rapidly and completely than other patients, resulting in higher-than-expected serum M1 concentrations and an increased risk of respiratory depression. Alternative analgesics, dose reduction, or close monitoring for signs of tramadol toxicity (such as respiratory depression) are recommended for known ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers.", "Use with central nervous system depressants: Ultracet must be used with caution and in reduced dosages in patients concurrently receiving CNS depressants such as alcohol, opioids, anesthetics, phenothiazines, hypnotics, or sedatives.", "Increased intracranial pressure or head trauma: Administer Ultracet with caution in patients with increased intracranial pressure or head injury.", "Patients with opioid addiction: Ultracet should not be used in patients with opioid dependency. Tramadol can re-addict patients who were previously dependent on other opioids.", "Use with alcohol: In chronic heavy alcohol abusers, the risk of hepatotoxicity is significantly increased when excessive paracetamol is ingested.", "Withdrawal symptoms: Withdrawal symptoms may occur if Ultracet is discontinued abruptly. Panic attacks, severe anxiety, hallucinations, paresthesia, tinnitus, and unusual central nervous system symptoms have been very rarely reported upon abrupt discontinuation of tramadol hydrochloride. Clinical experience suggests that withdrawal symptoms may be mitigated by gradual dose reduction.", "Use with MAO inhibitors and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs): Extreme caution is required when administering Ultracet concomitantly with monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors. Concomitant administration of tramadol with MAO inhibitors or SSRIs increases the risk of adverse events, including seizures and serotonin syndrome.", "Renal impairment: The use of Ultracet in patients with renal impairment has not been studied. In patients with a creatinine clearance of less than 30 mL/min, it is recommended that the dosing interval be adjusted so that the total dose does not exceed 2 tablets every 12 hours.", "Hepatic impairment: Ultracet should not be administered to patients with severe hepatic impairment. The risk of paracetamol toxicity is higher in patients with non-cirrhotic alcoholic liver disease.", "Serious skin reactions: Very rare reports of serious skin reactions such as acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP), Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS), and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) have been associated with paracetamol use. Patients should be informed of the signs of serious skin reactions, and use of the drug should be discontinued at the first appearance of skin rash or any other sign of hypersensitivity. Physicians should warn patients about the signs of serious cutaneous adverse reactions such as SJS, TEN, Lyell's syndrome, and AGEP.", "Hyponatremia: Hyponatremia has been rarely reported with Ultracet use, typically in patients with predisposing risk factors, such as elderly patients or those concurrently taking medications associated with hyponatremia. In some reports, hyponatremia appeared to be secondary to the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) and resolved upon discontinuation of Ultracet and appropriate management (e.g., fluid restriction). Monitoring for signs and symptoms of hyponatremia is recommended in at-risk patients during Ultracet therapy.", "General precautions: Tolerance and physical or psychological dependence may develop, even at therapeutic doses. The clinical need for analgesic treatment should be reviewed on an ongoing basis. Do not exceed the recommended dose of Ultracet. Do not administer Ultracet concurrently with other products containing tramadol or paracetamol. Paracetamol overdose may cause hepatotoxicity in some patients."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Inform your physician immediately of any adverse reactions experienced during treatment with this medication."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:47:51.236	ultracet-37-5mg-325mg-tablets-for-moderate-to-severe-pain-management-3-blisters-x-10-tablets	\N
