, inform your physician if you have a history of increased intraocular pressure (glaucoma), cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes mellitus, ocular bacterial infection, or hyperthyroidism."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue use immediately and notify your physician or seek prompt medical attention at the nearest healthcare facility."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:37:36.451	osla-redi-ophthalmic-solution-tetrahydrozoline-hcl-5mg-and-zinc-sulfate-25mg-for-ocular-allergy	275
1494	Levothyrox 100mcg Tablets for Hypothyroidism and Benign Thyroid Nodule (2 Blisters x 15 Tablets)	P26254	\N	\N	\N	Detailed origin information is not provided in the supplied data.	87086.00	60960.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250421101351-0-P26254.jpg?versionId=DPCUF.DrKK8VujZDJ8hQu8Cst.Owctgg	Levothyrox 100mcg tablets indicated for the treatment of benign euthyroid goiter, hypothyroidism replacement therapy, and thyroid cancer suppression (2 blisters x 15 tablets).	{"indications": ["Treatment of benign euthyroid goiter.", "Prevention of goiter relapse following surgery for benign euthyroid goiter, depending on postoperative thyroid hormone status.", "Replacement therapy in hypothyroidism.", "Suppressive therapy in thyroid cancer.", "Concomitant therapy with antithyroid medications in hyperthyroidism management.", "Diagnostic use in thyroid suppression testing (specifically for Levothyrox 100mcg tablets)."], "sideEffects": ["When individual tolerance limits for levothyroxine sodium are exceeded or in the event of overdose, clinical symptoms typical of hyperthyroidism may occur, particularly if the dose is increased too rapidly at the start of treatment. These include cardiac arrhythmias (such as atrial fibrillation and extrasystoles), tachycardia, palpitations, anginal pain, headache, muscle weakness and cramps, flushing, fever, vomiting, menstrual irregularities, pseudotumor cerebri, tremor, restlessness, insomnia, hyperhidrosis, weight loss, and diarrhea. In such cases, the daily dose should be reduced or the medication withheld for several days. Treatment may be resumed cautiously once adverse reactions have subsided. Hypersensitivity to any component of Levothyrox may cause cutaneous and respiratory allergic reactions. Angioedema has been reported in rare cases."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Antidiabetic medications", "effect": "Levothyroxine may decrease the therapeutic effect of antidiabetic drugs. Therefore, blood glucose levels should be monitored frequently when thyroid hormone therapy is initiated, and antidiabetic dosages adjusted if necessary."}, {"drug": "Coumarin derivatives", "effect": "The anticoagulant effect may be potentiated as levothyroxine displaces anticoagulants from plasma protein binding sites, thereby increasing the risk of hemorrhages, such as gastrointestinal or central nervous system bleeding, especially in elderly patients. Frequent monitoring of coagulation parameters is required at the start of and during treatment. Anticoagulant dosage adjustment may be necessary."}, {"drug": "Protease inhibitors (e.g., ritonavir, indinavir, lopinavir)", "effect": "May affect the efficacy of levothyroxine. Close monitoring of thyroid hormone parameters is recommended, and levothyroxine dosage adjustment may be required."}, {"drug": "Phenytoin", "effect": "May affect levothyroxine efficacy by displacing it from serum proteins, resulting in an increased fraction of free T4 (fT4) and free T3 (fT3). Furthermore, phenytoin increases hepatic metabolism of levothyroxine. Close monitoring of thyroid hormone parameters is advised."}, {"drug": "Cholestyramine, colestipol", "effect": "Inhibit the absorption of sodium levothyroxine. Sodium levothyroxine should be administered 4 to 5 hours prior to the intake of these agents."}, {"drug": "Aluminum-containing medications, iron preparations, calcium carbonate", "effect": "Aluminum-containing drugs (antacids, sucralfate) have been reported in literature to decrease the efficacy of levothyroxine. Levothyroxine should be administered at least 2 hours prior to aluminum-containing medications. The same precaution applies to iron preparations and calcium carbonate."}, {"drug": "Salicylates, dicumarol, high-dose furosemide (250mg), clofibrate", "effect": "May displace sodium levothyroxine from plasma proteins, resulting in an elevated free T4 fraction."}, {"drug": "Orlistat", "effect": "Hypothyroidism and/or reduced control of hypothyroidism may occur when orlistat and levothyroxine are taken concurrently. This may be due to impaired absorption of iodine salts and/or levothyroxine."}, {"drug": "Sevelamer", "effect": "May reduce levothyroxine absorption. Patients are advised to monitor thyroid function parameters at the initiation and end of combination therapy; levothyroxine dosage adjustment may be necessary."}, {"drug": "Tyrosine kinase inhibitors (e.g., imatinib, sunitinib)", "effect": "May reduce the efficacy of levothyroxine. Patients are recommended to monitor thyroid function at the start and end of combination therapy; dosage adjustment of levothyroxine may be required."}, {"drug": "Propylthiouracil, glucocorticoids, sympatholytic agents, amiodarone, and iodine-containing contrast media", "effect": "These substances inhibit the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3. Due to its high iodine content, amiodarone can trigger both hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism. Particular caution is warranted in cases of nodular goiter with potentially unrecognized autonomous thyroid function."}, {"drug": "Sertraline, chloroquine/proguanil", "effect": "Decrease levothyroxine efficacy and elevate serum TSH levels."}, {"drug": "Enzyme-inducing agents (e.g., barbiturates, carbamazepine)", "effect": "May increase hepatic clearance of levothyroxine."}, {"drug": "Estrogens (estrogen-containing contraceptives or hormone replacement therapy)", "effect": "Women using estrogen-containing contraceptives or postmenopausal women undergoing hormone replacement therapy may experience increased levothyroxine requirements."}, {"drug": "Soy-containing products", "effect": "May reduce intestinal absorption of levothyroxine. Dosage adjustment of Levothyrox may be necessary, particularly at the start or cessation of soy supplement intake."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No studies on the effects on the ability to drive and use machines have been performed. However, since levothyroxine is identical to the naturally occurring thyroid hormone, Levothyrox is not expected to influence the ability to drive and operate machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Synthetic levothyroxine in Levothyrox exerts identical effects to the natural hormone predominantly secreted by the thyroid gland. It is peripherally converted to T3 in target organs and, similarly to endogenous hormone, exhibits its specific effects at T3 receptors. Exogenous and endogenous levothyroxine are indistinguishable.", "pharmacologicalClass": "Synthetic thyroid hormone."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "When administered orally, levothyroxine is absorbed almost entirely from the upper small intestine. Depending on the pharmaceutical formulation, absorption can reach up to 80%. Tmax is approximately 5 to 6 hours.", "metabolism": "The liver contains about 1/3 of the total extrathyroidal levothyroxine pool, which rapidly exchanges with serum levothyroxine. Thyroid hormones are primarily metabolized in the liver, kidneys, brain, and skeletal muscles.", "elimination": "Metabolites are excreted via urine and feces. The total metabolic clearance rate of levothyroxine is approximately 1.2 L plasma/day."}, "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to the active substance or any of the excipients.", "Untreated adrenal insufficiency.", "Untreated pituitary insufficiency.", "Untreated thyrotoxicosis.", "Initiation of treatment with Levothyrox is contraindicated in acute myocardial infarction, acute myocarditis, and acute pancarditis.", "Combination therapy of levothyroxine and antithyroid agents for hyperthyroidism is contraindicated during pregnancy (see section Pregnancy and Lactation)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Levothyrox", "origin": "Detailed origin information is not provided in the supplied data.", "storage": "Store in a cool, dry place protected from light, at temperatures below 30°C. Keep out of reach of children.", "packaging": "Detailed packaging specifications are not provided in the supplied data.", "manufacturer": "Detailed manufacturer information is not provided in the supplied data."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "The recommended dosages serve as general guidelines. Individual requirements should be determined based on laboratory tests and clinical evaluations. Since serum T4 and fT4 levels may be elevated in some patients, serum thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) concentration is a more reliable standard for monitoring therapy. Thyroid hormone therapy should be initiated at a low dose and increased gradually every 2 to 4 weeks until full replacement dosage is achieved. For neonates and infants with congenital hypothyroidism where rapid replacement is vital, the recommended initial dosage is 10 to 15 mcg/kg body weight daily during the first 3 months. Thereafter, dosage should be adjusted individually based on clinical findings and thyroid hormone/TSH levels. In elderly patients, patients with coronary artery disease, and patients with severe or chronic hypothyroidism, caution is required when initiating thyroid hormone therapy (i.e., starting with a low dose, slow dose titration at prolonged intervals, and frequent thyroid hormone monitoring). Suboptimal replacement doses may result in incomplete TSH suppression and should be taken into consideration. Clinical experience shows that lower doses are sufficient in low-weight patients and patients with large nodular goiter.\\n\\nIndication | Recommended Daily Dosage (mcg sodium levothyroxine/day)\\n---|---\\nTreatment of benign euthyroid goiter | 75 - 200\\nPrevention of relapse after surgery for benign euthyroid goiter | 75 - 200\\nReplacement therapy in adult hypothyroidism\\n* Initial dose | 25 - 50\\n* Maintenance dose | 100 - 200\\nReplacement therapy in pediatric hypothyroidism\\n* Initial dose | 12.5 - 50\\n* Maintenance dose | 100 - 150 mcg/m - body surface area\\nSuppressive therapy in thyroid cancer | 150 - 300\\nConcomitant therapy with antithyroid medication in hyperthyroidism | 50 - 100\\nApplicable exclusively to Levothyrox 100mcg Tablets\\nDiagnostic use in thyroid suppression testing\\n2 weeks and 1 week prior to the test | 200\\n\\nThe daily dose may be administered as a single daily dose. Note: The dosages above are for reference only. Specific dosing depends on individual patient status and disease progression. Consult a physician or qualified medical professional for appropriate dosing.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and continue with the regular dosing schedule. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Administer the total daily dose in the morning on an empty stomach, half an hour before breakfast, with a small amount of liquid (e.g., half a glass of water). Infants should be given the full daily dose at least 30 minutes before the first meal of the day. Tablet(s) can be disintegrated in a small amount of water to form a suspension, which should be prepared immediately before administration and taken with additional fluid.", "overdoseManagement": "An elevation of serum T3 is a more reliable indicator of overdose than elevated T4 or fT4 levels. Acute hypermetabolic symptoms appear following an overdose (see Adverse Reactions). Depending on the extent of the overdose, treatment should be interrupted and diagnostic tests performed. Symptoms include pronounced beta-sympathomimetic effects such as tachycardia, agitation, anxiety, and hyperkinesia, which can be mitigated with beta-blockers. Plasmapheresis may be indicated in cases of extreme overdose. Seizures have been reported in isolated susceptible patients exceeding individual tolerance limits. Levothyroxine overdose can induce hyperthyroidism and acute psychotic episodes, particularly in patients at risk for psychiatric disorders. Cases of sudden cardiac death have been reported following long-term levothyroxine abuse. In case of emergency, immediately contact emergency services or the nearest local medical facility."}, "generalDescription": "Levothyrox 100°g contains levothyroxine, a synthetic form of natural thyroid hormone, utilized for the management of various thyroid-related disorders.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Detailed excipient information is not provided in the supplied data.", "activeIngredients": "Levothyroxine"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "In elderly patients, patients with coronary artery disease, and patients with severe or chronic hypothyroidism, caution is required when initiating thyroid hormone therapy (i.e., starting with a low initial dose, slow dose titration at prolonged intervals, and frequent thyroid hormone monitoring).", "lactation": "Levothyroxine is excreted into breast milk during lactation, but at recommended therapeutic doses, concentrations achieved are insufficient to cause hyperthyroidism or TSH suppression in the neonate.", "pediatric": "The recommended initial dosage for neonates and infants with congenital hypothyroidism is 10 to 15 mcg/kg body weight daily during the first 3 months. Thereafter, dosage should be adjusted individually based on clinical findings and thyroid hormone/TSH levels. The maintenance dose for children is 100 - 150 mcg/m - body surface area.", "pregnancy": "Levothyroxine therapy must be consistently maintained throughout pregnancy. Dosage requirements may increase during pregnancy. Clinical experience shows no evidence of teratogenicity or fetotoxicity in humans at recommended therapeutic doses. Very high levothyroxine doses during pregnancy may adversely affect fetal and postnatal development. Combination therapy of hyperthyroidism with levothyroxine and antithyroid medications is contraindicated during pregnancy, as such combinations require higher doses of antithyroid drugs which can cross the placenta and induce neonatal hypothyroidism. Diagnostic thyroid suppression tests should not be performed during pregnancy, as the use of radioactive iodine in pregnant women is strictly contraindicated."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Prior to initiating thyroid hormone therapy or a thyroid suppression test, the following conditions must be excluded or treated: Coronary insufficiency, angina pectoris, severe atherosclerosis, hypertension, hypopituitarism, and adrenal insufficiency. Autonomous thyroid disorders should also be excluded or treated prior to starting therapy.", "When initiating levothyroxine therapy in patients at risk for psychotic disorders, it is recommended to start with a low initial dose and increase the dosage gradually. Patient monitoring is advised. If signs of psychotic disorders occur, levothyroxine dosage adjustment should be considered.", "Mild drug-induced hyperthyroidism must be avoided in patients with coronary insufficiency, heart failure, or tachyarrhythmias. Frequent monitoring of thyroid hormone parameters is mandatory in these cases.", "In secondary hypothyroidism, the underlying cause must be diagnosed prior to replacement therapy, and if necessary, appropriate replacement therapy for compensated adrenal insufficiency must be initiated.", "When autonomous thyroid disease is suspected, TRH stimulation testing or suppression scintigraphy should be performed prior to treatment.", "Supraphysiological serum levels of levothyroxine must be avoided in hypothyroid postmenopausal women to prevent an increased risk of osteoporosis; rigorous monitoring of thyroid function is required.", "Levothyroxine should not be administered in hyperthyroid states unless combined with antithyroid medication for hyperthyroidism treatment.", "Thyroid hormones are not suitable for weight reduction. Physiological doses do not cause weight loss in euthyroid patients. Supraphysiological doses may cause serious or life-threatening adverse reactions (see Overdose).", "Once treatment with levothyroxine is established, dose titration based on clinical response and laboratory test results is recommended when switching brand or product.", "Hypothyroidism and/or reduced control of hypothyroidism may occur when orlistat and levothyroxine are co-administered (see Interactions). Patients taking levothyroxine should consult a physician before initiating orlistat therapy, as orlistat and levothyroxine administration times may need to be separated and the levothyroxine dose adjusted. Serum hormone monitoring is strongly recommended.", "This medicinal product contains lactose; therefore, patients with rare hereditary galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medication.", "For diabetic patients and patients on anticoagulant therapy, please refer to the Drug Interactions section."], "adverseReactionManagement": "In the event of adverse reactions, discontinue use immediately and notify a physician or visit the nearest medical facility for timely management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:39:35.371	levothyrox-100mcg-tablets-for-hypothyroidism-and-benign-thyroid-nodule-2-blisters-x-15-tablets	\N
