t healthcare facility."}, "generalDescription": "Meteospasmyl is a combination medication commonly used to treat symptoms of gastrointestinal disorders such as abdominal pain, intestinal spasms, flatulence, and bloating. The active components typically include simethicone (an antiflatulent agent that reduces gas in the stomach and intestines) and alverine citrate (an antispasmodic that relieves pain caused by smooth muscle spasms in the gut). Meteospasmyl provides rapid relief from digestive discomfort, improving the patient's quality of life.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Gelatin, glycerol, titanium dioxide, purified water", "activeIngredients": "Alverine citrate 60mg, Simethicone 300mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Simethicone: No effects on breastfed newborns/infants are anticipated since systemic absorption of simethicone is negligible. Lack of data on the excretion of alverine in breast milk. Meteospasmyl should not be used during breastfeeding.", "pregnancy": "Simethicone: No adverse effects are expected during pregnancy as systemic absorption of simethicone is negligible. Alverine: There are no adequate teratogenicity data available in animals. Clinically, to date, no malformative or foetotoxic effects have been reported. However, monitoring of exposed pregnancies is insufficient to exclude all risks. Therefore, as a precautionary measure, Meteospasmyl should not be used during pregnancy."}, "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue use immediately and inform a doctor or visit the nearest medical facility for timely management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:14:51.395	meteospasmyl-capsules-for-abdominal-distension-and-functional-bowel-disorders-60mg-300mg-2-blisters	127
14	Voltaren Emulgel 1% Topical Gel for Localized Pain and Inflammation Relief (20g Tube)	P01421	\N	\N	Novartis	Switzerland	77528.00	69000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20241107084410-0-P01421.png?versionId=iwbgYRDZaR_Uetw95jqlfXRSld2XRodn	Voltaren Emulgel 1% topical gel relieves localized pain and inflammation (20g tube).	{"sideEffects": ["Frequency of adverse reactions: Very common: > 1/10", "Common: > 1/100, < 1/10", "Uncommon: > 1/1000, < 1/100", "Rare: > 1/10000, < 1/1000", "Very rare: < 1/10000", "Immune system disorders: Very rare: Hypersensitivity reactions (including urticaria), angioedema.", "Respiratory system: Very rare: Asthma.", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Common: Rash, eczema, erythema, dermatitis (including contact dermatitis), pruritus.", "Rare: Bullous dermatitis.", "Very rare: Photosensitivity, pustular rash."], "effectsOnDriving": "None.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity: Do not use if allergic to diclofenac or any excipients, including isopropanol or propylene glycol.", "Asthma, urticaria, acute rhinitis: Contraindicated in patients with a history of asthma, urticaria, or acute rhinitis triggered by acetylsalicylic acid or other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen.", "Pregnancy: Do not use during the last 3 months of pregnancy."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Novartis", "origin": "Switzerland", "storage": "Do not store above 30°C. Protect from heat.", "packaging": "Box of 1 tube, 20g", "manufacturer": "Novartis Consumer Health SA"}, "usageInstructions": {"administration": "Adults and adolescents aged 12 years and older: Depending on the size of the affected area requiring treatment, apply 2 - 4g of Voltaren Emulgel (approximately the size of a cherry to a walnut, sufficient to treat an area of about 400 - 800 cm2), 3 - 4 times daily to the affected site and rub in gently. Duration of treatment depends on clinical indication and therapeutic response. Treatment should be re-evaluated after 2 weeks if symptoms do not improve. Voltaren Emulgel should not be used for more than 14 days. Wash hands thoroughly after application (unless treating fingers for osteoarthritis). Voltaren Emulgel may also be used as adjunctive therapy with other dosage forms of Voltaren. Children under 12 years: The use and safety of Voltaren Emulgel in children under 12 years have not been systematically evaluated; therefore, use is not recommended."}, "generalDescription": "Voltaren Emulgel 1% is a topical gel with analgesic and anti-inflammatory properties.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Carbomer, macrogol cetostearyl ether, cocoyl caprylocaprate, diethylamine, isopropyl alcohol, liquid paraffin, mineral oil, perfume cream 45, propylene glycol, purified water.", "activeIngredients": "100g of Voltaren Emulgel contains 1.16g of diclofenac diethylamine (equivalent to 1g of diclofenac sodium)."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Lactation: It is not known whether topically applied diclofenac is excreted in human breast milk. Do not apply on breasts or large skin areas, and avoid prolonged use.", "pediatric": "Children under 12 years: The use and safety of Voltaren Emulgel in children under 12 years have not been systematically evaluated; therefore, use is not recommended.", "pregnancy": "Pregnancy: There are no adequate controlled studies on the use of Voltaren Emulgel during pregnancy. Contraindicated during the third trimester of pregnancy due to the risk of premature closure of the ductus arteriosus and inhibition of labor. Animal studies do not indicate direct or indirect harmful effects on pregnancy."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Important note: Systemic side effects: Low when topical diclofenac is applied compared to oral diclofenac formulations.", "Long-term or large-scale use: If Voltaren Emulgel is applied to large skin areas or over an extended period, the incidence of systemic side effects may increase. In such cases, refer to the Summary of Product Characteristics (SmPC) for oral diclofenac formulations.", "Inform your physician of any adverse effects experienced during treatment.", "Caution: Instructions for use: Read the package insert carefully before use. Consult a physician if further information is required.", "Skin surface: Apply only to intact, non-damaged skin and avoid open wounds. Avoid contact with eyes and mucous membranes.", "Propylene glycol: May cause mild local skin irritation in some patients.", "Occlusive dressings: The product should not be used with occlusive dressings."]}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:14:54.724	voltaren-emulgel-1percent-topical-gel-for-localized-pain-and-inflammation-relief-20g-tube	120
15	Duspatalin Retard 200mg Sustained-Release Capsules for Gastrointestinal Functional Disorder Pain (3 Blisters x 10 Capsules)	P01414	\N	\N	\N	Vietnam	74157.00	66000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250508070600-0-P01414.jpg?versionId=ZRduFvqgEC7Qixp1M8EHhTjd_kQzVxgY	Duspatalin retard 200mg capsules for the symptomatic treatment of pain associated with gastrointestinal functional disorders (3 blisters x 10 capsules).	{"sideEffects": ["Like all medications, Duspatalin may cause adverse reactions. If you experience any side effects not listed in this leaflet, or if any adverse reaction becomes severe, inform your attending physician or pharmacist immediately. Adverse drug reactions have been spontaneously reported during post-marketing surveillance; therefore, an exact frequency cannot be reliably estimated from available data. These are predominantly allergic reactions, primarily limited to dermatologic manifestations. Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Rash (urticaria), sudden facial swelling (edema), angioedema (swelling of the face, lips, tongue, or throat, leading to difficulty breathing or swallowing), exanthema. Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity reactions (anaphylactoid reactions). Reporting suspected adverse reactions: Reporting suspected adverse reactions after authorization of the medicinal product is critically important. It facilitates continuous monitoring ofalach benefit-risk balance of the medicinal product. Healthcare professionals are requested to report any suspected adverse reactions via the national pharmacovigilance reporting system."], "effectsOnDriving": "No formal studies investigating the effects on the ability to drive and operate machinery have been conducted. The pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic profiles of mebeverine, supported by extensive post-marketing surveillance data, indicate no adverse impact on the psychomotor performance required for driving and operating heavy machinery.", "contraindications": ["Duspatalin is strictly contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity or allergy to mebeverine hydrochloride or to any of the excipients contained in this medication."], "manufacturingInfo": {"storage": "Store in a dry place protected from light, at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.,Keep this medication out of the sight and reach of children."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and adolescents aged 18 years and older: One capsule, administered 2 to 3 times daily. Take the capsules orally with a sufficient quantity of water approximately 20 minutes before meals. Do not chew the capsules, as the polymer coating is designed to ensure sustained release of the active ingredient. Special populations: No specific dosage adjustment studies have been conducted in geriatric patients, or in patients with hepatic and/or renal impairment. Based on post-marketing data, no specific safety risks have been identified in elderly patients or in those with compromised hepatic or renal function; therefore, dosage titration is not warranted in these patient cohorts. Note: The dosage recommendations provided above are for reference only. The precise individualized dosage regimen depends on the patient's clinical condition and disease severity. Consult your physician or qualified healthcare professional to determine the appropriate therapeutic dosage.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as you remember. However, if it is almost time for your next scheduled dose, omit the missed dose and resume your regular dosing regimen. Do not take a double dose to compensate for a forgotten individual dose.", "administration": "If you have any further questions concerning the use of this product, consult your physician or pharmacist.,Swallow the capsules whole with an adequate volume of water (at least 100 mL). Do not chew, crush, or break the capsules, as the specialized coating is essential for the sustained-release mechanism.", "overdoseManagement": "In the event of an acute overdose with Duspatalin, contact your physician or regional poison control center immediately. Clinical manifestations: In reported cases of mebeverine overdose, symptoms were either absent, mild, and rapidly self-limiting. Central nervous system hyperexcitability may theoretically occur in severe cases. Overdose symptomatology primarily involves neurological and cardiovascular parameters. Management: There is no specific pharmacological antidote. Symptomatic and supportive treatment is recommended. Gastric lavage should only be considered if a massive overdose is confirmed within approximately 1 hour of ingestion. Routine measures to decrease gastrointestinal absorption are generally unnecessary. In case of a medical emergency, activate local emergency medical services or transport the patient to the nearest emergency healthcare facility immediately."}, "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Capsule core (pellets): Magnesium stearate, polyacrylate dispersion 30%, talc, hypromellose, methacrylic acid - ethyl acrylate copolymer (1:1) dispersion 30%, glycerol triacetate,Capsule shell: Gelatin, titanium dioxide (E171),Printing ink: Shellac (E904), propylene glycol, strong ammonia solution, potassium hydroxide, black iron oxide (E172)", "activeIngredients": "Mebeverine hydrochloride 200mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "It remains unknown whether mebeverine or its active metabolites are excreted into human breast milk. The excretion of mebeverine into milk has not been evaluated in lactating animal models. Therefore, Duspatalin must not be administered during breastfeeding.", "pregnancy": "Clinical data regarding the administration of mebeverine to pregnant women are extremely limited. Preclinical reproductive toxicity studies performed in animals are insufficient. Consequently, Duspatalin should not be used during pregnancy unless explicitly prescribed and deemed essential by a physician."}}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:14:57.978	duspatalin-retard-200mg-sustained-release-capsules-for-gastrointestinal-functional-disorder-pain-3	\N
16	Lipistad 20mg Atorvastatin Film-Coated Tablets for Hypercholesterolemia and Dyslipidemia (3 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P01221	\N	\N	Stella Pharm	Germany	57571.00	40300.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520035156-0-P01221.png?versionId=w8o4iMMoNR04O7lxp093MFBsCvDU9RM7	Lipistad 20mg film-coated tablets contain atorvastatin and are indicated for the treatment of hypercholesterolemia and dyslipidemia (packaged in 3 blisters x 10 tablets).	{"indications": ["Primary hypercholesterolemia and mixed dyslipidemia", "Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia", "Cardiovascular disease prevention"], "sideEffects": ["Nasopharyngitis", "Thrombocytopenia", "Allergic reactions", "Hypersensitivity", "Hyperglycemia", "Hypoglycemia, weight gain, anorexia", "Nightmares, insomnia", "Headache", "Dizziness, paresthesia, hypoesthesia, dysgeusia, amnesia", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Blurred vision", "Visual disturbances", "Tinnitus", "Hearing loss", "Pharyngolaryngeal pain, epistaxis", "Constipation, flatulence, dyspepsia, nausea, diarrhea", "Vomiting, upper and lower abdominal pain, eructation, pancreatitis", "Hepatitis", "Cholestasis", "Liver failure", "Urticaria, skin rash, pruritus, alopecia", "Angioneurotic edema, bullous dermatitis including erythema multiforme, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and toxic epidermal necrolysis", "Myalgia, arthralgia, pain in extremity, muscle spasms, joint swelling, back pain", "Neck pain, muscle fatigue", "Myopathy, myositis, rhabdomyolysis, tendon injury, occasionally complicated by tendon rupture", "Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy", "Gynecomastia", "Malaise, asthenia, chest pain, peripheral edema, fatigue, pyrexia", "Abnormal liver function tests, blood creatine kinase increased", "White blood cells in urine", "Sexual dysfunction", "Depression", "Interstitial lung disease", "Diabetes mellitus"], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., cyclosporine, telithromycin, clarithromycin, delavirdin, stiripentol, ketoconazole, voriconazole, itraconazole, posaconazole, and HIV protease inhibitors including ritonavir, lopinavir, atazanavir, indinavir, darunavir, etc.)", "effect": "Marked increase in plasma atorvastatin concentrations and increased risk of myopathy. Co-administration should be avoided if possible. If concomitant use is unavoidable, lower initial and maximum doses should be considered, and appropriate clinical monitoring is recommended."}, {"drug": "Moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., erythromycin, diltiazem, verapamil, and fluconazole)", "effect": "Increased plasma concentrations of atorvastatin and increased risk of myopathy. Consideration should be given to reducing the maximum dose of atorvastatin, accompanied by appropriate clinical monitoring."}, {"drug": "CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., efavirenz, rifampin, St. John - s Wort)", "effect": "Decreased plasma concentrations of atorvastatin. Close monitoring is recommended to ensure therapeutic efficacy."}, {"drug": "Transporter inhibitors (e.g., cyclosporine)", "effect": "Increased systemic exposure to atorvastatin."}, {"drug": "Fibric acid derivatives, gemfibrozil, erythromycin, immunosuppressants, azole antifungals, high-dose niacin (> 1 g/day), colchicine", "effect": "Increased risk of myopathy."}, {"drug": "Fusidic acid", "effect": "Increased risk of rhabdomyolysis. Temporarily discontinue atorvastatin during treatment with fusidic acid."}, {"drug": "Digoxin", "effect": "Slight increase in steady-state digoxin concentrations. Appropriate monitoring of patients taking digoxin is recommended."}, {"drug": "Oral contraceptives", "effect": "Increased concentrations of norethindrone and ethinyl estradiol. No specific clinical recommendation."}, {"drug": "Phenazone", "effect": "No significant effect on phenazone clearance."}, {"drug": "Tipranavir/Ritonavir", "effect": "Increased atorvastatin concentrations. Reduce maintenance dose of atorvastatin."}, {"drug": "Fosamprenavir/Ritonavir", "effect": "Increased atorvastatin concentrations. Reduce maintenance dose of atorvastatin."}, {"drug": "Boceprevir", "effect": "Increased atorvastatin concentrations. Atorvastatin dose should not exceed 20mg daily during co-administration."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Not known.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to any component of the medication", "Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations of serum transaminases exceeding 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN)", "Pregnancy, lactation, or women of childbearing potential not using adequate contraceptive measures. Atorvastatin should only be administered to women of childbearing age when such patients are highly unlikely to conceive and have been informed of the potential hazards to the fetus."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Stella", "origin": "Germany", "storage": "Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, protect from direct sunlight.", "packaging": "Box of 3 blisters x 10 tablets", "manufacturer": "Stella Pharm"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dosage ranges from 10mg to 80mg once daily. Initial and maintenance doses should be individualized based on baseline LDL-C levels, treatment goals, and patient response. Following initiation and/or during titration of atorvastatin, lipid levels should be analyzed within 2 to 4 weeks, and the dosage adjusted accordingly.\\nFor pediatric patients aged 10 years and older with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, the recommended starting dose is 10mg daily. The dose may be increased up to 80mg daily according to response and tolerability.\\nAtorvastatin therapy should be avoided in patients receiving cyclosporine, HIV protease inhibitors (tipranavir and ritonavir), or hepatitis C protease inhibitors (telaprevir).\\nIn HIV patients receiving lopinavir combined with ritonavir, caution should be exercised when prescribing atorvastatin, and the lowest necessary dose should be used.\\nIn patients receiving clarithromycin, itraconazole, or HIV patients receiving saquinavir combined with ritonavir, darunavir and ritonavir, fosamprenavir, or fosamprenavir and ritonavir, atorvastatin treatment should be limited to 20mg, and appropriate clinical assessment is required to ensure the lowest necessary dose is utilized.\\nIn patients receiving the HIV protease inhibitor nelfinavir or the hepatitis C protease inhibitor boceprevir, atorvastatin treatment should be limited to 40mg, and appropriate clinical assessment is required.", "administration": "Atorvastatin can be administered at any time of day, with or without food. Before initiating treatment with atorvastatin, attempts should be made to control hypercholesterolemia through appropriate diet, exercise, and weight reduction in obese patients, as well as treatment of underlying medical conditions. Patients should maintain a standard cholesterol-lowering diet during atorvastatin therapy.", "overdoseManagement": "There is no specific treatment for atorvastatin overdose. If overdose occurs, patients should be treated symptomatically and supportive measures instituted as required. Due to extensive plasma protein binding, hemodialysis is unlikely to significantly enhance atorvastatin clearance."}, "generalDescription": "Atorvastatin is a statin medication used to lower blood cholesterol and triglyceride levels, thereby reducing the risk of cardiovascular diseases.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Microcrystalline cellulose, lactose monohydrate, croscarmellose sodium, povidone K30, polysorbate 80, magnesium stearate, hypromellose, macrogol 6000, titanium dioxide, talc", "activeIngredients": "Atorvastatin 20mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Safety and efficacy profiles in patients over 70 years receiving recommended doses are comparable to the general population.", "lactation": "Contraindicated during breastfeeding. It is unknown whether atorvastatin is excreted in human breast milk; due to the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants, mothers taking atorvastatin must not breastfeed.", "pediatric": "Pediatric use should be supervised by physicians experienced in managing pediatric dyslipidemia, with regular assessment of treatment progression. For heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients aged 10 years and older, the recommended starting dose is 10mg daily, titratable up to 80mg daily based on response and tolerability. Atorvastatin is not indicated for children under 10 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated during pregnancy. Women of childbearing potential must use effective contraception. Administer only when pregnancy has been ruled out and the patient has been informed of potential fetal hazards."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Liver effects: Liver enzyme tests should be performed before initiating statin therapy and periodically as clinically indicated. Patients with increased transaminase levels should be monitored until the abnormality resolves. If ALT or AST elevations persistently exceed 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN), reduction of dose or withdrawal of atorvastatin is recommended. Caution should be exercised in patients who consume substantial quantities of alcohol and/or have a history of liver disease.", "Musculoskeletal effects: Creatine phosphokinase (CPK) should be measured before starting treatment in the following conditions: Renal impairment, hypothyroidism, personal or family history of hereditary muscular disorders, history of muscular toxicity with a statin or fibrate, history of liver disease and/or heavy alcohol consumption, elderly patients (> 70 years) with risk factors for rhabdomyolysis. If CPK levels are > 5 times ULN at baseline, treatment should not be initiated. Myopathy (defined as muscle pain or weakness accompanied by CPK increases > 10 times ULN) should be considered in any patient presenting with diffuse myalgia, muscle tenderness, or weakness, and/or marked CPK elevation. Patients should be advised to promptly report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness, particularly if accompanied by malaise or fever. Atorvastatin therapy should be discontinued if markedly elevated CPK levels occur or if myopathy is diagnosed or suspected. The risk of myopathy during statin treatment is increased with concurrent administration of cyclosporine, fibric acid derivatives, erythromycin, niacin, azole antifungals, colchicine, telaprevir, boceprevir, or tipranavir/ritonavir. Many of these agents inhibit CYP3A4 metabolism and/or hepatic transport. Consideration must be given to lower starting and maintenance doses when co-administering atorvastatin with these agents. Concomitant use of atorvastatin and fusidic acid should be avoided; temporary cessation of atorvastatin is recommended during fusidic acid therapy. Atorvastatin may cause elevated CPK. Rare cases of rhabdomyolysis with secondary acute renal failure due to myoglobinuria have been reported. A history of renal impairment may be a risk factor for rhabdomyolysis. Temporary or permanent discontinuation of atorvastatin is required in patients with acute or severe conditions predisposing to renal failure secondary to rhabdomyolysis (e.g., severe acute infection, hypotension, major surgery, trauma, severe metabolic, endocrine, and electrolyte disorders, or uncontrolled seizures).", "Hemorrhagic stroke: A post-hoc analysis of a clinical study in 4,731 patients without coronary heart disease who had a stroke or transient ischemic attack (TIA) within the preceding 6 months showed a higher incidence of hemorrhagic stroke in patients randomized to atorvastatin 80mg compared to placebo (55 vs. 33 patients). Patients with hemorrhagic stroke at baseline appeared at increased risk for recurrent hemorrhagic stroke (7 vs. 2 patients). However, among patients treated with atorvastatin 80mg, any stroke (265 vs. 311) and coronary heart disease events (123 vs. 204) were less frequent.", "Endocrine function: Increases in HbA1c and fasting blood glucose levels have been reported with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, including atorvastatin. However, the risk of hyperglycemia is outweighed by the reduction in vascular risk afforded by statins. Caution is warranted in patients with predisposing risk factors for muscle injury. Statins carry a risk of adverse musculoskeletal effects such as myopathy and myositis, particularly in vulnerable populations including patients over 65 years, uncontrolled hypothyroidism, and renal impairment. Regular monitoring of adverse effects during therapy is essential. Baseline creatine kinase (CK) testing should be performed in cases of potential drug interactions and in high-risk patient groups.", "Concomitant therapy with other agents: The risk of rhabdomyolysis is increased when atorvastatin is administered concomitantly with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors or transport proteins. Alternative non-interacting therapies should be considered where possible. If co-administration is necessary, lower maximum doses of atorvastatin and appropriate clinical monitoring are required. Do not use atorvastatin concomitantly with systemic formulations of fusidic acid or within 7 days of stopping fusidic acid treatment.", "Pediatric population: No clinically significant effects on growth and sexual maturation were observed in a 3-year study evaluating overall maturation and development, Tanner staging, and height/weight measurements.", "Interstitial lung disease: Exceptional cases of interstitial lung disease have been reported with certain statins, particularly during long-term therapy. Presenting features may include dyspnea, non-productive cough, and deterioration in general health status. If interstitial lung disease is suspected, statin therapy should be discontinued.", "Excipients: Lipistad contains lactose. Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, total lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medicine."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:01.286	lipistad-20mg-atorvastatin-film-coated-tablets-for-hypercholesterolemia-and-dyslipidemia-3-blisters	39
19	Depakine 200mg Gastro-Resistant Tablets for Epilepsy and Mania (Bottle of 40 Tablets)	P02197	\N	\N	Sanofi	France	141772.00	112000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250508070554-0-P02197.jpg?versionId=1oH_hSjW9RH..AAiqtDih9WRD_ZqxbXO	Depakine 200mg gastro-resistant tablets for the treatment of epilepsy and manic episodes in bipolar disorders (Bottle of 40 tablets).	{"indications": ["Epilepsy: Treatment of various forms of epilepsy.", "Mania: Treatment and prevention of manic episodes in bipolar affective disorders."], "sideEffects": ["Very common (> 10%), common (> 1% and < 10%), uncommon (> 0.1% and < 1%), rare (> 0.01% and < 0.1%), very rare (< 0.01%), not known (cannot be estimated from available data).", "Congenital malformations and developmental disorders (see Pregnancy and Breastfeeding).", "Blood and lymphatic system disorders: Common: Anemia, thrombocytopenia (see Precautions). Uncommon: Pancytopenia, leukopenia. Rare: Bone marrow failure, including pure red cell aplasia, agranulocytosis, macrocytic anemia, macrocytosis.", "Investigations: Rare: Decreased coagulation factors (at least one), abnormal coagulation tests (such as prolonged prothrombin time, prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time, prolonged thrombin time, prolonged INR) (see Precautions and Pregnancy and Breastfeeding), biotin deficiency/biotinidase deficiency.", "Nervous system disorders: Very common: Tremor. Common: Extrapyramidal disorder, stupor, somnolence, convulsion, memory impairment, headache, nystagmus, dizziness (which may occur a few minutes after intravenous injection and resolves spontaneously within minutes). Uncommon: Coma, encephalopathy, lethargy, reversible parkinsonism, ataxia, paresthesia. Rare: Reversible dementia associated with reversible cerebral atrophy, cognitive disorder. Stupor and lethargy sometimes leading to transient coma/encephalopathy, which may be isolated or associated with increased seizure frequency during treatment and resolve upon discontinuation or dose reduction. These cases are very common in polytherapy (especially with phenobarbital or topiramate) or after sudden valproate dose increase.", "Ear and labyrinth disorders: Common: Deafness.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Very common: Nausea. Common: Vomiting, gingival disorders (mainly gingival hyperplasia), stomatitis, epigastric pain, diarrhea occurring frequently at the start of treatment and usually resolving within a few days without stopping the drug. Observed minutes after intravenous injection and resolving spontaneously within minutes. Uncommon: Pancreatitis, sometimes fatal (see Special Warnings).", "Renal and urinary disorders: Uncommon: Renal failure. Rare: Enuresis, tubulointerstitial nephritis, reversible Fanconi syndrome (a defect in proximal renal tubular function leading to glucosuria, proteinuria, phosphaturia, and uricosuria) with unclear mechanism.", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Common: Hypersensitivity, transient alopecia with or without dose relation. Uncommon: Angioedema, rash, hair disorders (such as abnormal hair texture, hair color change, abnormal hair growth). Rare: Toxic epidermal necrolysis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, erythema multiforme, Drug Rash with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS).", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Uncommon: Decreased bone mineral density, osteopenia, osteoporosis, and fractures in patients on long-term Depakine 200mg therapy (mechanism unknown). Rare: Systemic lupus erythematosus (see Precautions), rhabdomyolysis (see Precautions).", "Endocrine disorders: Uncommon: Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH), hyperandrogenism (hirsutism, virilism, acne, male-pattern baldness, or increased androgen). Rare: Hypothyroidism (see Pregnancy and Breastfeeding).", "Nutrition and metabolism disorders: Common: Hyponatremia, weight gain. Weight gain should be carefully monitored as a risk factor for polycystic ovary syndrome (see Precautions). Rare: Hyperammonemia (see Precautions), obesity. Isolated and moderate hyperammonemia without liver function changes may occur, is transient, and does not require treatment cessation. Hyperammonemia associated with neurological symptoms has been reported, requiring further diagnostic evaluations.", "Neoplasms benign, malignant and unspecified (including cysts and polyps): Rare: Myelodysplastic syndrome.", "Vascular disorders: Common: Hemorrhage (see Precautions and Pregnancy and Breastfeeding). Uncommon: Vasculitis.", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Common: Liver injury (see Special Warnings).", "Reproductive system and breast disorders: Common: Menstrual disorders. Uncommon: Amenorrhea. Rare: Male infertility, polycystic ovaries.", "Psychiatric disorders: Common: Confusional state, hallucinations, aggression, agitation, attention disorder. Rare: Abnormal behavior, psychomotor hyperactivity, learning disorder.", "Management of ADRs: If side effects occur, discontinue use and inform a doctor or go to the nearest medical facility for prompt management."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Sedatives, MAO inhibitors, antidepressants, and benzodiazepines", "effect": "Depakine 200mg may potentiate their effects; clinical monitoring and appropriate dose adjustment are recommended."}, {"drug": "Lithium", "effect": "Depakine 200mg does not affect serum lithium levels."}, {"drug": "Phenobarbital", "effect": "Depakine 200mg increases serum phenobarbital concentrations (due to inhibition of hepatic catabolism) and sedation may occur, especially in children. Therefore, clinical monitoring is required during the first 15 days of combination therapy, phenobarbital dose must be reduced immediately if sedation occurs, and plasma phenobarbital levels should be monitored accordingly."}, {"drug": "Primidone", "effect": "Depakine 200mg increases plasma primidone levels and intensifies its side effects (such as sedation), which disappear with long-term therapy. Clinical monitoring is necessary, especially at the start of combination therapy, with appropriate dose adjustment."}, {"drug": "Phenytoin", "effect": "Depakine 200mg decreases total plasma phenytoin concentration. Additionally, Depakine 200mg increases free phenytoin fraction, potentially causing overdose symptoms (valproic acid displaces phenytoin from plasma protein binding sites and reduces its hepatic catabolism). Clinical monitoring and free phenytoin assessment are required when monitoring phenytoin levels."}, {"drug": "Carbamazepine", "effect": "Clinical toxicity has been reported when valproate is used concomitantly with carbamazepine; valproate may potentiate carbamazepine toxicity. Clinical monitoring is required, especially at the start of combination therapy, with appropriate dose adjustment."}, {"drug": "Lamotrigine", "effect": "Depakine 200mg decreases lamotrigine metabolism and increases its half-life almost twofold. This interaction may increase lamotrigine toxicity, particularly severe skin rashes. Clinical monitoring is required, especially at the start of combination therapy, with appropriate dose adjustment (reduction of lamotrigine dose)."}, {"drug": "Zidovudine", "effect": "Valproate may increase plasma zidovudine concentrations, increasing zidovudine toxicity."}, {"drug": "Felbamate", "effect": "Valproic acid may decrease mean felbamate clearance by up to 16%."}, {"drug": "Olanzapine", "effect": "Valproic acid may decrease plasma olanzapine concentrations."}, {"drug": "Rufinamide", "effect": "Valproic acid may increase plasma rufinamide concentrations, depending on valproic acid concentration. Caution is required, especially in children where this effect is more pronounced."}, {"drug": "Enzyme-inducing antiepileptics (including phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine)", "effect": "Decrease serum valproic acid concentrations. Dosage must be adjusted based on clinical response and blood levels during combination therapy."}, {"drug": "Felbamate and valproate", "effect": "Decrease valproic acid clearance by 22 - 50%, resulting in increased plasma valproic acid concentrations. Valproate dosage must be monitored."}, {"drug": "Mefloquine", "effect": "Increases valproic acid metabolism and possesses convulsive properties; seizures may occur upon co-administration."}, {"drug": "Aspirin", "effect": "When co-administered with highly protein-bound drugs (aspirin), free valproic acid concentrations may increase."}, {"drug": "Vitamin K-dependent anticoagulants", "effect": "Closely monitor prothrombin ratio when vitamin K antagonists are used."}, {"drug": "Cimetidine or erythromycin", "effect": "Serum valproic acid concentrations may increase (due to reduced hepatic metabolism) when co-administered with cimetidine or erythromycin."}, {"drug": "Carbapenem antibiotics (panipenem, meropenem, imipenem, etc.)", "effect": "Decreased blood valproic acid concentrations have been reported when co-administered with carbapenems, dropping by 60 - 100% within 2 days, sometimes accompanied by convulsions. Due to rapid onset and significant reduction, concomitant use of carbapenems in patients stabilized on valproic acid should be avoided. If co-administration cannot be avoided, closely monitor blood valproate levels."}, {"drug": "Rifampicin", "effect": "May decrease blood valproic acid concentrations, leading to loss of therapeutic efficacy. Valproate dose adjustment may be necessary during concomitant rifampicin therapy."}, {"drug": "Protease inhibitors (lopinavir, ritonavir)", "effect": "Concomitant use with protease inhibitors like lopinavir and ritonavir decreases plasma valproate concentrations."}, {"drug": "Cholestyramine", "effect": "Concomitant use with cholestyramine decreases plasma valproate concentrations."}, {"drug": "Topiramate or acetazolamide", "effect": "Concomitant use of valproate with topiramate or acetazolamide may be associated with encephalopathy or hyperammonemia. Patients on this combination must be closely monitored for signs and symptoms of encephalopathy and hyperammonemia."}, {"drug": "Quetiapine", "effect": "Concomitant use with valproate may increase the risk of leukopenia."}, {"drug": "Hormonal contraceptives (oestroprogestatives)", "effect": "Valproate generally lacks enzyme-inducing properties; therefore, it does not reduce the efficacy of oestroprogestative hormonal contraceptives in women."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Depakine 200mg may cause somnolence, especially when taken with other antiepileptic drugs or substances that increase sleepiness. If you experience this side effect or your condition is uncontrolled and you continue to have seizures, do not drive or operate machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Anticonvulsant effects are utilized to treat various seizure types in animals and epilepsy in humans. Clinical and experimental studies on valproate suggest two types of anticonvulsant mechanisms. The first is a direct pharmacological effect related to plasma and brain concentrations of valproate. The second appears to be indirect and is likely related to valproate metabolites remaining in the brain, changes in neurotransmitters, or direct effects on cell membranes. The most widely accepted hypothesis involves gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), the concentration of which increases following valproate administration. Valproate shortens the intermediate phases of sleep while increasing slow-wave sleep.", "pharmacologicalClass": "Valproate exerts its primary pharmacological effects mainly on the central nervous system."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "The bioavailability of valproate after oral administration is close to 100%. Most of the drug is distributed in blood and rapidly exchanges with extracellular fluid. Valproate also distributes into cerebrospinal fluid and brain; CSF concentrations closely correlate with free serum drug concentrations.", "metabolism": "Valproate is excreted mainly in the urine after metabolism via glucuronide conjugation and beta-oxidation.", "elimination": "Valproate is excreted primarily via urine after metabolism by glucuronide conjugation and beta-oxidation. Valproate is dialyzable, but hemodialysis removes only the free fraction of valproate (approximately 10%)."}, "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to sodium valproate or any excipients of the drug.", "Acute or chronic hepatitis.", "Personal or family history of severe hepatic impairment, especially drug-related.", "Hepatic porphyria (an inherited disorder related to abnormal production of porphyrin pigments).", "History of liver disease or severe pancreatic or hepatic dysfunction.", "Urea cycle disorders.", "Known or suspected mitochondrial disorders."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Depakine", "origin": "France", "storage": "Store Depakine 200mg below 30°C, protect from moisture. Keep out of sight and reach of children. Do not use past the expiration date printed on the outer carton. Do not dispose of via wastewater or household waste; consult your pharmacist regarding proper disposal methods for unused medications to ensure environmental protection.", "packaging": "Bottle of 40 gastro-resistant tablets", "manufacturer": "Sanofi Winthrop Industrie"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Epilepsy: This formulation is unsuitable for pediatric patients under 6 years of age due to aspiration risk. Alternative pharmaceutical formulations (e.g., oral solution) are more appropriate. Daily dosage is determined by age and body weight, taking into account individual sensitivity to valproate. Adults: Initial dose of 600mg daily, increasing by 200mg every 3 days until seizure control is achieved, usually within a range of 1000mg to 2000mg daily (20 - 30mg/kg/day). If seizures remain uncontrolled, the dosage may be increased up to 2500mg daily. Pediatric patients over 20kg: Initial dose should be 400mg daily regardless of body weight, then titrated until control is achieved, usually 20 - 30mg/kg/day. If uncontrolled, may increase up to 35mg/kg/day. Pediatric patients under 20kg: 20mg/kg daily; in severe clinical cases, higher dosages (> 40mg/kg/day) can be utilized strictly under therapeutic drug monitoring of plasma valproate levels, hematology, and clinical chemistry. Elderly patients: Dosage should be individualized based on seizure control. Mania: Recommended initial dosage is 1000mg daily (20mg/kg body weight). Titrate rapidly to achieve the desired clinical response at the lowest effective dose. Recommended maintenance dosage for bipolar disorder ranges from 1000mg to 2000mg daily, not exceeding 3000mg daily. Adjust according to individual clinical response. Female children, female adolescents, women of childbearing potential, and pregnant women: Treatment with Depakine 200mg must be initiated and supervised by a specialist experienced in the management of epilepsy or bipolar disorder. Utilize only when alternative therapies are ineffective or not tolerated; risk-benefit assessment must be thoroughly reviewed at each routine clinical visit. Preferably prescribe as monotherapy at the lowest effective dose. Utilize extended-release formulations when feasible to avoid high plasma concentration peaks. Divide the total daily dose into at least 2 single doses. Initiation: If previously treated with other antiepileptics, introduce sodium valproate gradually to reach the optimal therapeutic dose in approximately 2 weeks, then taper concomitant medications. If treatment-naive, escalate the dosage every 2 - 3 days to achieve the optimal dose within 1 week. Duration of therapy: Strictly adhere to prescribed dosage and duration; do not discontinue therapy without consulting a physician.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, administer it as soon as possible. However, if it is nearly time for the next scheduled dose, omit the missed dose and resume the regular dosing regimen. Do not double doses.", "administration": "Depakine 200mg is for oral administration. Swallow tablets whole with an adequate amount of water; do not chew or crush. Divide the total daily dose into 2 - 3 divided doses, preferably administered with meals.", "overdoseManagement": "In the event of an overdose, contact a physician or emergency department immediately. Common clinical signs include coma, muscular hypotonia, hyporeflexia, miosis, respiratory depression, metabolic acidosis, and hypotension/circulatory collapse/shock which may be fatal. Fatal outcomes have been reported following massive overdoses, although favorable clinical recovery is more frequent. Symptoms may vary, and seizures have been reported despite extremely high plasma concentrations. Increased intracranial pressure leading to cerebral edema has been documented. The sodium content in sodium valproate preparations may precipitate hypernatremia in overdosage. Hospital management is strictly supportive and symptomatic: Gastric lavage performed up to 10 - 12 hours post-ingestion, alongside continuous monitoring of respiratory and cardiovascular function. Naloxone has proven successful in isolated case reports. In massive overdoses, hemodialysis or extracorporeal renal replacement therapy may be clinically effective."}, "generalDescription": "Depakine 200mg is indicated for the treatment of epilepsy and manic episodes in bipolar disorders.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Excipients q.s. 1 tablet (including colloidal anhydrous silica, titanium dioxide, hypromellose, macrogol, etc.)", "activeIngredients": "Sodium Valproate 200mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Dosage must be individualized based on clinical seizure control.", "lactation": "Valproate is excreted into human breast milk in low quantities (1 - 10% of maternal serum concentrations). Hematologic disorders have been reported in breastfed neonates and infants. A clinical decision must be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or discontinue valproate therapy, weighing the developmental and health benefits of breastfeeding against the clinical therapeutic need for the mother. Never discontinue anti-epileptic therapy without prior consultation with a physician.", "pediatric": "Unsuitable for pediatric patients under 6 years of age (aspiration/choking hazard); alternative pharmaceutical formulations (e.g., oral solution) are preferred. Pediatric patients over 20kg: Initial dose of 400mg daily, titrated to 20 - 30mg/kg daily (maximum 35mg/kg/day). Pediatric patients under 20kg: 20mg/kg daily (dosages exceeding 40mg/kg/day necessitate intensive clinical and laboratory monitoring). Monotherapy is preferred in children under 3 years of age. Avoid salicylates in pediatric patients under 3 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated in female children, female adolescents, women of childbearing potential, and pregnant women unless alternative treatments are ineffective or not tolerated. Effective contraception is mandatory. If pregnancy is planned or confirmed, treatment must be re-evaluated: for bipolar disorder, valproate therapy should be discontinued; for epilepsy, treatment must not be discontinued without a comprehensive re-evaluation of the risk-benefit ratio. If therapy is continued, utilize the lowest effective divided daily doses, preferably extended-release formulations. Supplementation with folic acid (5mg daily) prior to conception is recommended, although the prevention of valproate-induced teratogenicity remains unproven. Specialized prenatal specialist monitoring is strongly advised."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Caution with Depakine 200mg: Very rarely, Depakine 200mg may cause life-threatening hepatic injury (hepatitis) or acute pancreatitis. Your physician will order laboratory blood tests to regularly monitor liver function, particularly during the initial 6 months of therapy. Report immediately if you experience: sudden fatigue, anorexia, asthenia, lethargy, somnolence, recurrent vomiting, epigastric or abdominal pain, jaundice, or recurrence of seizures despite adequate adherence.", "Severe hepatic injury: Frequency: Very rare, occasionally fatal. High-risk populations: Infants and pediatric patients under 3 years of age presenting with severe seizures, particularly those associated with brain damage, mental retardation, or congenital metabolic/degenerative disorders, especially in polytherapy regimens. After 3 years of age, the associated risk decreases significantly. The majority of severe hepatic injuries manifest within the first 6 months of treatment. Early warning signs: Early diagnosis relies primarily on clinical symptoms preceding jaundice (fatigue, anorexia, lethargy, somnolence, recurrent vomiting, abdominal pain, seizure recurrence). Patients or caregivers must report these clinical signs immediately for comprehensive liver function evaluation (specifically prothrombin ratio). Discontinue Depakine 200mg promptly if the prothrombin ratio is abnormally depressed, accompanied by concurrent biochemical abnormalities (decreased fibrinogen and clotting factors, elevated bilirubin, elevated transaminases). Concomitant administration of salicylates must be strictly avoided due to shared metabolic pathways.", "Pancreatitis: Very rare, potentially fatal acute pancreatitis has been reported. Pediatric patients are at particular risk, with incidence decreasing with advancing age. Additional risk factors include severe seizures, neurological impairment, or concurrent anticonvulsant therapy. Hepatic impairment occurring concurrently with pancreatitis substantially escalates mortality risk. Patients presenting with acute abdominal pain must undergo immediate medical evaluation. Discontinue valproate therapy if pancreatitis is diagnosed.", "Female children, female adolescents, women of childbearing potential, and pregnant women: Depakine 200mg must not be administered in these patient populations unless alternative therapies are ineffective or not tolerated, due to significant teratogenic and developmental risks in offspring exposed in utero. The risk-benefit ratio must be re-evaluated at every clinical visit, at puberty, and when planning pregnancy or upon pregnancy confirmation. Effective contraception must be maintained throughout the entire course of treatment. Prescribers must ensure patients receive comprehensive risk counseling (via patient information guides and risk acknowledgment documentation): including the nature and severity of teratogenicity and developmental risks, the absolute necessity of effective contraception, the requirement for regular medical follow-up, and the urgency of consulting a physician immediately upon suspected pregnancy. Patients planning conception should transition to alternative therapeutic options pre-conception whenever possible.", "Suicidal ideation and behavior: Reported in patients treated with antiepileptic agents across various clinical indications. Meta-analysis of randomized placebo-controlled clinical trials demonstrates a small incremental risk. Monitor patients continuously for emergent signs of suicidal ideation and seek immediate medical intervention if warranted.", "Carbapenem antibiotics: Concomitant administration of sodium valproate and carbapenem agents is strongly discouraged.", "Known or suspected mitochondrial disorders: Valproate may precipitate or exacerbate clinical symptoms of underlying mitochondrial disorders caused by mutations in mitochondrial DNA or the POLG nuclear gene. Acute liver failure and liver-related fatalities occur at a higher frequency in patients with hereditary neurometabolic syndromes caused by POLG mutations (e.g., Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome). Screen for POLG mutations if suggested by family history or unexplained encephalopathy, refractory epilepsy, status epilepticus, psychomotor retardation, axonal sensorimotor neuropathy, ataxia, myopathy, nystagmus, or severe migraine with occipital prodrome. Perform baseline liver function tests prior to initiating therapy and periodically during the first 6 months in at-risk patients. Perform complete blood counts (platelet count, bleeding time) prior to initiation, prior to surgical interventions, or in the event of hematoma formation or spontaneous hemorrhage. Exercise caution when administering to patients with systemic lupus erythematosus. Evaluate urea cycle function if a urea cycle enzymatic deficiency is clinically suspected. Counsel patients regarding potential weight gain. Patients with carnitine palmitoyltransferase (CPT) type II deficiency are at heightened risk for rhabdomyolysis. Avoid alcohol consumption during therapy.", "Pediatric patients: Monotherapy is strongly preferred in patients under 3 years of age, carefully weighing potential therapeutic benefits against the severe risks of hepatotoxicity and pancreatitis. Avoid concomitant salicylate administration in pediatric patients under 3 years of age due to heightened hepatotoxicity risk.", "Renal impairment: Dosage reduction is required. Clinical evaluation must guide dosage titration since routine plasma concentration monitoring may be misleading."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue administration immediately and notify a physician or report to the nearest medical facility for prompt clinical management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:11.057	depakine-200mg-gastro-resistant-tablets-for-epilepsy-and-mania-bottle-of-40-tablets	85
28	Acemuc Kids 200mg Acetylcysteine Granules for Expectorant and Cough Relief in Pediatric Patients (Box of 30 Sachets)	P00050	\N	\N	Vietnam.	Vietnam	3575.00	2967.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/product/20250723035639-0-P00050_1.jpg?versionId=NFbxPZreqUuoD6.ro3_1hdhXjExXwX42	Acemuc Kids 200mg containing Acetylcysteine formulated as oral granules for mucolytic therapy and expectoration in pediatric patients (Box of 30 sachets).	{"indications": ["Indicated for the treatment of respiratory conditions characterized by excessive, viscous mucus secretions and impaired expectoration, including acute bronchitis, pneumonia, and chronic pulmonary diseases."], "sideEffects": ["Administration of high doses may induce gastrointestinal adverse events, including epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Antitussive agents lacking secretomotor properties or causing reduced bronchial secretion (atropine-like agents)", "effect": "Concomitant administration is clinically contraindicated and pharmacologically irrational due to the risk of pulmonary secretions accumulation."}], "contraindications": ["Phenylketonuria, due to the presence of aspartame as an excipient.", "History of bronchial asthma or bronchospasm (conferring an elevated risk of severe bronchospastic reactions to acetylcysteine-containing preparations).", "Known hypersensitivity to acetylcysteine, structurally related compounds (such as carbocisteine, erdosteine, or mecysteine), or any component of the excipient formulation.", "Pediatric patients under 24 months of age."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Sanofi (France)", "storage": "Store in a dry, cool environment protected from direct sunlight and excessive heat.", "packaging": "Box containing 30 oral powder sachets.", "manufacturer": "Vietnam."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and pediatric patients over 7 years of age: 1 sachet three times daily. Pediatric patients aged 2 to 7 years: 1 sachet twice daily.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, administer it as soon as remembered. However, if the time for the subsequent scheduled dose is near, omit the missed dose and resume the standard dosing schedule. Do not administer a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Dissolve the contents of the sachet completely in approximately one-half glass of water prior to oral administration.", "overdoseManagement": "Acetylcysteine demonstrates a relatively high margin of safety. Nevertheless, acute overdose may occur, particularly following intravenous administration or high-dose oral ingestion during the management of paracetamol toxicity. Clinical manifestations of overdose include hypotension, respiratory depression, bronchospasm, hemolysis, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), and acute renal failure. Management: Transfer the patient immediately to a medical facility for specialized supportive and symptomatic care."}, "generalDescription": "Acemuc Kids 200mg is an oral granule formulation containing the active pharmaceutical ingredient Acetylcysteine, which functions as a mucolytic agent to decrease mucus viscosity and facilitate the clearance of pulmonary secretions from the respiratory tract. It is clinically indicated for the management of respiratory disorders associated with thick, tenacious mucus and expectoration difficulties, such as bronchitis, pneumonia, and chronic obstructive pulmonary diseases.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "aspartame, orange flavoring, FD&C Yellow No. 6, lactose monohydrate, purified water.", "activeIngredients": "Acetylcysteine 200mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Use during lactation is not recommended due to the absence of established clinical safety data regarding excretion into human breast milk.", "pediatric": "Strictly contraindicated in pediatric patients under 24 months of age.", "pregnancy": "Reproductive and developmental toxicity studies in animals have revealed no evidence of teratogenic potential; however, adequate and well-controlled clinical data in pregnant women are currently lacking."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Physiological coughing is a critical defense mechanism of the bronchopulmonary tract and should not be suppressed unnecessarily.", "Administer with extreme caution in patients with a clinical history of bronchial asthma, active peptic ulcer disease, or compromised respiratory function."], "adverseReactionManagement": "In the event of persistent gastrointestinal intolerance or adverse reactions, dosage reduction should be considered."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:17.481	acemuc-kids-200mg-acetylcysteine-granules-for-expectorant-and-cough-relief-in-pediatric-patients	280
30	Metrogyl Denta Metronidazole 10mg Oral Mucosal Gel for Periodontitis and Gingivitis (10g Tube)	P00103	\N	\N	Unique Pharmaceutical Labs	Unique Pharmaceutical Labs	34177.00	27000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250806034948-0-P00103.png?versionId=YyQ2yhjwpAuf8CDXnbqe5lkPfIqPnsRi	Metrogyl Denta oral mucosal gel indicated for the treatment of periodontitis, gingivitis, and dental caries, supplied in a 10g tube.	{"indications": ["Treatment of periodontitis, gingivitis, and dental caries."], "sideEffects": ["Due to minimal systemic absorption following topical oral mucosal application, the risk of systemic adverse reactions is low.", "The most frequently observed adverse reactions are local and directly associated with the application site, including a persistent bitter taste and transient localized hypersensitivity.", "Headache has been reported during therapy."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "oral anticoagulants of the coumarin/warfarin type", "effect": "Potentiation of the anticoagulant effect, leading to an increased risk of bleeding, has been reported when topical or systemic metronidazole is administered concomitantly with warfarin-type oral anticoagulants."}, {"drug": "disulfiram", "effect": "Concomitant administration of metronidazole and disulfiram may precipitate acute confusional states or psychotic reactions."}], "effectsOnDriving": "None or negligible influence on the ability to drive and use machines.", "contraindications": ["Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to metronidazole, nitroimidazole derivatives, or chlorhexidine (excipient)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Unique Pharmaceutical Labs", "origin": "Unique Pharmaceutical Labs", "storage": "Store in a dry place at temperatures below 30°C, protected from direct light and moisture. Do not freeze.", "packaging": "10g collapsible aluminum or laminate tube", "manufacturer": "Unique Pharmaceutical Labs"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Administer twice daily. Dosage may be individualized based on the extent and severity of the affected dentition. Note: The recommended dosage is provided for reference purposes only. The precise dosage regimen must be determined by the treating physician or dental professional based on clinical presentation and disease severity.", "missedDose": "Apply the missed dose as soon as remembered. If it is nearly time for the subsequent scheduled application, omit the missed dose and resume the standard dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to compensate for a forgotten application.", "administration": "For dental and oral mucosal use only. Apply a sufficient quantity of Metrogyl Denta topically to form a thin layer along the gingival margins or affected dental sites.", "overdoseManagement": "There is no clinical experience with acute overdose of metronidazole oral gel in humans. The acute oral toxicity of metronidazole was demonstrated to exceed 5 g/kg (the maximum dose administered) in rodent toxicity studies."}, "generalDescription": "Metrogyl Denta oral mucosal gel is a localized therapeutic formulation indicated for periodontal and dental conditions. This medicinal product is manufactured by Unique Pharmaceutical Labs.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Chlorhexidine Gluconate Solution, Propylene Glycol, Carbomer 940, Disodium Edatate, Sodium Hydroxide, Purified Water", "activeIngredients": "Metronidazole Benzoate equivalent to Metronidazole 10mg, Chlorhexidine Gluconate Solution equivalent to Chlorhexidine 0.25% w/w"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Use with standard clinical caution in geriatric patients, taking into account concomitant medications and underlying systemic comorbidities.", "lactation": "Metronidazole is excreted into breast milk, but systemic exposure following topical application of Metrogyl Denta is minimal. The risk to the nursing infant is considered very low; however, administration during lactation should be undertaken only when clinically indicated.", "pediatric": "Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been firmly established; use in children requires careful clinical evaluation by a healthcare professional.", "pregnancy": "Metronidazole should not be administered during the first trimester of pregnancy unless considered strictly essential by the treating physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["In susceptible patients, systemic absorption of metronidazole may induce a disulfiram-like reaction (alcohol intolerance), characterized by flushing, abdominal cramps, nausea, and tachycardia."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Patients should promptly report any adverse or unexpected clinical effects experienced during the course of therapy to their physician or dentist."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:20.608	metrogyl-denta-metronidazole-10mg-oral-mucosal-gel-for-periodontitis-and-gingivitis-10g-tube	282
40	Strepsils Honey Lemon 1.2mg/0.6mg Throat Soothing Lozenges for Pharyngitis and Sore Throat (2 Blisters x 12 Lozenges)	P00029	\N	\N	Reckitt Benckiser (thailand) Ltd.	Thailand	22866.00	18750.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250415090625-0-P00029.jpg?versionId=c85X2_9my8Mc3ZO6kZ6LkUgvwfo2u1II	Strepsils Honey Lemon (2 blisters x 12 lozenges) - Honey and lemon flavored medicated lozenges for throat soothing and symptomatic relief of sore throat and pharyngitis.	{"indications": ["Helps soothe the throat and relieve symptoms of sore throat and pharyngitis."], "sideEffects": ["In rare cases, gastrointestinal disorders such as diarrhea and abdominal discomfort, as well as glossodynia (tongue pain) associated with lozenge use, have been reported, although they do not follow a specific clinical pattern.", "Similar adverse events have also been observed in a subset of patients receiving lactose-containing placebos."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "No known clinically significant drug interactions.", "effect": ""}], "effectsOnDriving": "None.", "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Reckitt Benckiser", "origin": "Thailand", "storage": "Store at controlled room temperature ( - 30°C)", "packaging": "Carton containing 2 blisters x 12 lozenges", "manufacturer": "Reckitt Benckiser (Thailand) Ltd."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dissolve one lozenge slowly in the mouth every 2 to 3 hours as needed. Do not exceed a maximum dose of 8 lozenges per 24 hours.", "administration": "Dissolve one lozenge slowly in the mouth.", "overdoseManagement": "No clinical cases of human overdose have been recorded. To achieve an equivalent oral median lethal dose (LD50) in mice, administration of more than 80 lozenges/kg of body weight would be required."}, "generalDescription": "Strepsils Honey Lemon is a medicated lozenge containing dual antibacterial agents combined with natural ingredients such as honey and lemon, designed to soothe the oropharyngeal mucosa and alleviate symptoms of sore throats and pharyngitis.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Menthol, terpene-free lemon oil, tartaric acid, sucrose, liquid glucose, honey, quinoline yellow.", "activeIngredients": "Each medicated lozenge contains: 2,4-Dichlorobenzyl Alcohol 1.2 mg, Amylmetacresol 0.6 mg."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "No special precautions required based on standard clinical usage.", "pediatric": "Exercise caution as young children may choke on lozenges. Contraindicated or not recommended for use in children under 6 years of age.", "pregnancy": "No special precautions required based on standard clinical usage."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Read the package insert carefully before use.", "Consult a physician or healthcare professional if further clinical information is required.", "Keep in mind that young children may be at risk of choking on solid lozenges.", "No special precautions required under standard administration.", "Ensure you read and fully understand the instructions for use on the packaging or follow the directions of a physician or pharmacist prior to administration.", "Avoid excessive consumption to minimize the risk of gastrointestinal adverse reactions.", "Strepsils Honey Lemon is generally not recommended for pediatric patients under 6 years of age. Consult a physician before administering to young children."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Inform your physician, pharmacist, or healthcare provider immediately of any adverse reactions experienced while using this pharmaceutical product."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:36.866	strepsils-honey-lemon-1-2mg-0-6mg-throat-soothing-lozenges-for-pharyngitis-and-sore-throat-2	283
