Topical overdosage may cause generalized hypertrichosis. Accidental ingestion may cause systemic effects (fluid retention, tachycardia, hypotension). Management includes diuretics, beta-adrenergic blocking agents, or intravenous saline as clinically indicated."}, "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Minoxidil 20mg/ml"}}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:44:34.038	minox-2-20mg-ml-edol-topical-solution-for-androgenetic-alopecia-and-alopecia-areata-box-of-1-bottle	278
41	Kremil-S United Aluminum Hydroxide, Magnesium Carbonate, and Simethicone 178mg/233mg/30mg Chewable Tablets for Gastric Pain, Heartburn, and Acid Indigestion (Box of 10 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P03105	\N	\N	United International Pharma Co., Ltd. (Vietnam)	Vietnam	14286.00	12000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250415090444-0-P03105.jpg?versionId=wJ6bZQ_cRztraLASy.0mAzgkcVBszZPo	Kremil-S United Chewable Tablets for the treatment of stomach pain, relief of gastric burning, heartburn, and acid indigestion (Box of 10 blisters x 10 tablets).	{"indications": ["Treatment of conditions related to the stomach and gastrointestinal tract, such as acid indigestion, dyspepsia, and flatulence."], "sideEffects": ["Hypersensitivity reactions such as pruritus, urticaria, angioedema, and anaphylactic reactions.", "Diarrhea or constipation.", "Abdominal pain.", "Hypermagnesemia.", "Hyperaluminemia.", "Hypophosphatemia. Prolonged use, high doses, or even normal doses in patients with low-phosphorus diets may lead to increased bone resorption, hypercalciuria, and osteomalacia."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Other medications", "effect": "Kremil-S should not be taken concomitantly with other medications as it may alter their absorption if administered within 1 hour."}, {"drug": "Tetracyclines, vitamins, ciprofloxacin, ketoconazole, hydroxychloroquine, chloroquine, chlorpromazine, rifampicin, cefdinir, cefpodoxime, levothyroxine, rosuvastatin", "effect": "Aluminum-containing antacids may interfere with the absorption of these drugs."}, {"drug": "Levothyroxine", "effect": "May bind to Simethicone, delaying or decreasing its absorption."}, {"drug": "Polystyrene sulfonate", "effect": "Use with caution due to the risk of reduced resin-binding capacity for potassium, with a risk of metabolic alkalosis in renal impairment patients (reported with aluminum hydroxide and magnesium hydroxide), and intestinal obstruction (reported with aluminum hydroxide)."}, {"drug": "Citrate", "effect": "Aluminum hydroxide and citrates may increase aluminum levels, especially in patients with renal impairment."}, {"drug": "Salicylates", "effect": "Urinary alkalinization due to magnesium hydroxide may alter the excretion of certain drugs, with an observed increase in salicylate excretion."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No relevant information available.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to the active substances or any of the excipients.", "Patients with severe debility or renal impairment.", "Hypophosphatemia."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "United Pharma", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C", "packaging": "Box of 10 blisters x 10 chewable tablets", "manufacturer": "United International Pharma Co., Ltd. (Vietnam)"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults: Take 1-2 tablets approximately 1 hour after meals and at bedtime, or when experiencing pain, with a total dose not exceeding 8 tablets per day, or as prescribed by a physician. Not recommended for children.", "missedDose": "The medication is used on an as-needed basis rather than on a fixed schedule, so missed doses are unlikely to occur.", "administration": "Chew the tablets before swallowing. Take approximately 1 hour after meals and at bedtime, or as needed for pain.", "overdoseManagement": "Acute overdose symptoms combining aluminum hydroxide and magnesium salts include diarrhea, abdominal pain, and vomiting. Large doses may cause or aggravate intestinal obstruction and paralytic ileus in patients at risk. Aluminum and magnesium are eliminated via the urinary tract; treatment of acute overdose includes intravenous calcium gluconate, rehydration, and forced diuresis. In cases of impaired renal function, hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis is necessary."}, "generalDescription": "Kremil-S chewable tablets are indicated for the treatment of conditions related to the stomach and gastrointestinal tract, such as heartburn, indigestion, and flatulence.", "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Simethicone 30mg, Magnesium hydroxide 233mg, Aluminum hydroxide 178mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Due to limited maternal absorption when used at recommended doses, magnesium and aluminum hydroxide salts, if any, are excreted into breast milk in minimal amounts. Simethicone is not absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract. There is no effect on breastfed newborns/infants as the exposure to aluminum hydroxide, magnesium hydroxide, and simethicone in nursing mothers is negligible.", "pregnancy": "There are no clinical data available on the safety of Kremil-S in pregnant women."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Aluminum hydroxide may cause constipation, and overdose of magnesium salts can cause decreased bowel motility. Large doses may cause or aggravate intestinal obstruction and paralytic ileus in high-risk patients such as those with renal impairment or the elderly.", "Aluminum hydroxide is poorly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, and systemic effects are rare in patients with normal renal function. However, excessively high or prolonged doses, or even normal doses in patients on a low-phosphorus diet, may lead to hypophosphatemia (due to aluminum binding to phosphate) accompanied by increased bone resorption and hypercalciuria, with a risk of osteomalacia. Consult a physician in cases of prolonged use or in patients at risk of hypophosphatemia.", "In patients with renal impairment, blood concentrations of both aluminum and magnesium increase. In these patients, prolonged use of high doses of aluminum and magnesium salts may lead to dementia and microcytic anemia.", "The use of aluminum hydroxide may be unsafe in patients with porphyria undergoing hemodialysis.", "This product contains aspartame, a source of phenylalanine. It may be harmful to patients with phenylketonuria (PKU), a rare genetic disorder that causes phenylalanine accumulation because the body cannot eliminate it properly."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Inform your physician of any adverse reactions experienced while using this medication."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:40.086	kremil-s-united-aluminum-hydroxide-magnesium-carbonate-and-simethicone-178mg-233mg-30mg-chewable	245
44	Ceclor 125mg Powder for Oral Suspension - Second-Generation Cephalosporin Antibacterial Agent for Susceptible Bacterial Infections (Bottle of 60ml)	P02755	\N	\N	Not Available	Not available	125349.00	107800.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250508070607-0-P02755.jpg?versionId=8t3nYKursvfIc2dxip2VggsR6qD2eCdk	Ceclor 125mg powder for oral suspension is a second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic indicated for the treatment of susceptible bacterial infections.	{"indications": ["Treatment of upper and lower respiratory tract infections.", "Treatment of urinary tract infections.", "Treatment of skin and skin structure infections.", "Treatment of otitis media.", "Treatment of pharyngitis and tonsillitis."], "sideEffects": ["Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported in approximately 1.5% of patients, manifesting as morbilliform eruptions (1 in 100 cases). Pruritus, urticaria, and positive Coombs' tests occur at a lower frequency of less than 1 in 200 cases each. Serum sickness-like reactions have been reported rarely with the use of cefaclor. These reactions are characterized by erythema multiforme, rashes, and other skin manifestations accompanied by arthritis/arthralgia, with or without fever.", "These reactions differ from classic serum sickness in that they are infrequently accompanied by lymphadenopathy and proteinuria, lack circulating immune complexes, and leave no sequelae. Occasionally, isolated symptoms may occur, but these do not constitute a serum sickness-like reaction. While further studies are underway, serum sickness-like reactions appear to be due to hypersensitivity and occur more frequently during or following a second (or subsequent) course of cefaclor therapy.", "These reactions are reported more frequently in children than in adults, with incidence rates ranging from 1/200 (0.5%) in a focused trial to 2/8,346 (0.024%) across all clinical trials (incidence in children in clinical trials was 0.055%) up to 1/38,000 (0.003%) in spontaneous reporting systems.", "Signs and symptoms usually appear a few days following initiation of therapy and resolve within a few days after drug discontinuation. Hospitalization for these reactions has been reported, but hospital stays are typically short (average of 2 to 3 days, according to post-marketing surveillance reports). In hospitalized patients, symptoms ranged from mild to severe, with the majority of severe symptoms occurring in children. Antihistamines and glucocorticoids help alleviate these signs and symptoms. No serious sequelae have been reported.", "More severe hypersensitivity reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, and anaphylaxis, occur rarely. Anaphylactoid symptoms may manifest as isolated reactions including angioedema, asthenia, edema (facial edema and extremities), dyspnea, paresthesia, syncope, or vasodilation. Anaphylactic reactions may occur more commonly in patients with a history of penicillin allergy. Hypersensitivity reactions may persist for several months, though this is very rare.", "Gastrointestinal adverse effects occur in approximately 2.5% of patients, most commonly diarrhea (1 in 70 cases). Pseudomembranous colitis may occur during or after antibiotic treatment. Nausea and vomiting are infrequent.", "As with some penicillins and other cephalosporins, transient hepatitis and cholestatic jaundice have been reported very rarely.", "Other effects considered to be related to antibiotic therapy include eosinophilia (1 in 50 patients), genital pruritus, vaginitis, and rarely, thrombocytopenia and reversible interstitial nephritis.", "Other adverse effects with uncertain causal relationship to the drug include:", "Central Nervous System: Reversible hyperactivity, anxiety, nervousness, insomnia, confusion, hypertonia, dizziness, hallucinations, and somnolence have been reported rarely.", "Transient clinical laboratory abnormalities have also been reported; although their definitive etiology is undetermined, they are provided as reference information for clinicians:", "Hepatic: Slight elevations in AST (SGOT), ALT (SGPT), or alkaline phosphatase (1 in 40).", "Hematopoietic system - like other beta-lactam antibiotics, transient lymphocytosis, leukopenia, and more rarely, hemolytic anemia, aplastic anemia, agranulocytosis, and clinically significant reversible neutropenia.", "Reports have also noted an increase in prothrombin time with or without clinical bleeding in patients receiving concurrent cefaclor and coumadin.", "Renal: Slight elevations in BUN or serum creatinine (less than 1 in 500) or abnormal urinalysis results (less than 1 in 200).", "Certain cephalosporins can precipitate seizures, particularly in patients with renal impairment when dosage is not reduced. If seizure activity associated with the drug occurs, the drug should be discontinued. Anticonvulsant therapy may be administered if clinically indicated."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Antacids containing magnesium or aluminum hydroxide", "effect": "Decreases the rate and extent of cefaclor absorption."}, {"drug": "H2 receptor antagonists", "effect": "Does not alter the rate or extent of cefaclor absorption."}, {"drug": "Probenecid", "effect": "Inhibits the renal excretion of cefaclor."}, {"drug": "Coumadin", "effect": "Reports of increased prothrombin time with or without clinical bleeding."}, {"drug": "Oral anticoagulants", "effect": "Some reports indicate an enhanced anticoagulant effect."}], "effectsOnDriving": "The drug has virtually no influence on the ability to drive and use machines. However, as dizziness has occasionally been reported, physicians may use their clinical discretion in advising patients regarding driving or operating machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Cefaclor is a second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that exerts bactericidal action by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis.", "pharmacologicalClass": "Cephalosporin Antibiotic"}, "contraindications": ["Contraindicated in patients with a known history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin-class antibiotics.", "Precautions: Before therapy with cefaclor is instituted, careful inquiry should be made to determine whether the patient has had previous hypersensitivity reactions to cefaclor, cephalosporins, penicillins, or other drugs. If cefaclor is to be administered to penicillin-sensitive patients, caution should be exercised because cross-allergenicity, including anaphylaxis, among beta-lactam antibiotics has been documented.", "If an allergic reaction to cefaclor occurs, the drug should be discontinued. When indicated, appropriate therapy should be administered, such as vasopressor amines, antihistamines, or corticosteroids.", "Antibiotics, including cefaclor, should be administered with caution to any patient who has demonstrated some form of allergy, particularly to drugs. Pseudomembranous colitis has been reported with nearly all broad-spectrum antibiotics (including macrolides, semi-synthetic penicillins, and cephalosporins); therefore, it is important to consider its diagnosis in patients who present with diarrhea subsequent to the administration of antibacterial agents. Such colitis may range in severity from mild to life-threatening. Mild cases usually respond to drug discontinuation alone. Moderate to severe cases should be managed with appropriate therapeutic measures."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Ceclor", "origin": "Not available", "storage": "Store at controlled room temperature 15° - 30°C (59° - 86°F). After reconstitution (for oral suspension in use): Store for 14 days at 2° - 8°C (36° - 46°F). Do not use after the expiration date printed on the label.", "packaging": "Powder for oral suspension", "manufacturer": "Not available"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults: The usual adult dosage is 250 mg every 8 hours. For pneumonia and bronchitis: 250 mg administered 3 times daily. For sinusitis: 250 mg 3 times daily for 10 days. For more severe infections (such as pneumonia) or those caused by less susceptible organisms, doses may be doubled. Doses of 4 g/day have been administered safely to normal subjects for 28 days, although the total daily dosage should not exceed recommended limits. For the treatment of acute uncomplicated gonococcal urethritis in males and females: A single 3 g dose administered concurrently with 1 g of probenecid.\\nChildren: The usual recommended daily dosage for children is 20 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 8 hours. For bronchitis and pneumonia: 20 mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses. For more severe infections, otitis media, and infections caused by less susceptible organisms, 40 mg/kg/day may be administered in divided doses. The maximum daily dose is 1 g/day.\\nDirections for oral suspension:\\nDose of 20 mg/kg/day (Note: teaspoon (tsp)).\\nChild weight 9 kg: 125 mg/5 ml strength: 1/2 tsp, 3 times/day.\\nChild weight 18 kg:\\n125 mg/5 ml strength: 1 tsp, 3 times/day; or 2 tsp, 3 times/day.\\n250 mg/5 ml strength: 1/2 tsp, 3 times/day.\\nDose of 40 mg/kg/day\\nChild weight 9 kg:\\n125 mg/5 ml strength: 1 tsp, 2 times/day.\\n250 mg/5 ml strength: 2 tsp, 2 times/day.\\nChild weight 18 kg: 250 mg/5 ml strength: 1 tsp, 2 times/day.\\nFor the treatment of otitis media and pharyngitis: The total daily dosage may be divided into 2 doses administered every 12 hours.\\nCefaclor may be administered to patients with impaired renal function; dosage adjustment is generally not required in such cases.\\nIn severe renal impairment, dosage should be adjusted as follows: If creatinine clearance is 10 - 50 ml/min, administer 50% of the standard dose; if creatinine clearance is less than 10 ml/min, administer 25% of the standard dose.\\nIn the treatment of beta-hemolytic streptococcal infections, a therapeutic course of cefaclor should be administered for at least 10 days.\\nNote: The dosages above are for reference only. The specific dosage depends on the patient's clinical condition and disease severity. Consult a physician or healthcare professional for appropriate dosing.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and resume the normal dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Cefaclor is administered orally.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms of toxicity following an overdose of cefaclor may include nausea, vomiting, epigastric distress, and diarrhea. The severity of epigastric distress and diarrhea is dose-related. If other symptoms are present, they may be secondary to an underlying disease state, an allergic reaction, or toxicity from concomitant medications.\\nIn managing overdosage, consider the possibility of multiple drug overdoses, drug-drug interactions, and abnormal pharmacokinetics in the patient. Gastric lavage is not necessary unless the ingested amount has been 5 times the normal recommended dose.\\nProtect the patient's airway, support ventilation and perfusion. Monitor and maintain vital signs, blood gases, and serum electrolytes carefully. Gastrointestinal absorption of drugs may be reduced by giving activated charcoal, which in many cases is more effective than emesis or lavage; consider activated charcoal in lieu of or in addition to gastric lavage. Repeated doses of activated charcoal may accelerate elimination of some drugs that have been absorbed.\\nAirway protection is essential during gastric lavage or administration of activated charcoal. Forced diuresis, peritoneal dialysis, hemodialysis, or charcoal hemoperfusion have not been established as beneficial for cefaclor overdosage."}, "generalDescription": "Ceclor Suspension is a second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic used to treat bacterial infections caused by susceptible organisms.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Not available", "activeIngredients": "Cefaclor"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Specific information is not available.", "lactation": "Small amounts of cefaclor have been detected in human breast milk following administration of a single 500 mg dose. Average levels in milk were 0.18, 0.20, 0.21, and 0.16 mg/L at 2, 3, 4, and 5 hours, respectively. Trace amounts were detected 1 hour post-dose. The effect on nursing infants is unknown. Caution should be exercised when cefaclor is administered to a nursing woman.", "pediatric": "The usual recommended daily dosage is 20 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 8 hours. For bronchitis and pneumonia: 20 mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses. For more severe infections, otitis media, and infections caused by less susceptible organisms, 40 mg/kg/day may be administered in divided doses. The maximum daily dose is 1 g/day.", "pregnancy": "Reproduction studies have been performed in mice and rats at doses up to 12 times the human dose and in ferrets at doses up to 3 times the maximum human dose, and have revealed no evidence of impaired fertility or harm to the fetus due to cefaclor. However, there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.\\nLabor and delivery: The effect of cefaclor on labor and delivery is unknown."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Prolonged use of cefaclor may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms. Careful observation of the patient is essential. If superinfection occurs during therapy, appropriate measures should be taken.", "A positive direct Coombs' test has been reported during treatment with cephalosporin antibiotics. It should be recognized that a positive Coombs' test may be due to the drug - for example, in hematology studies or in transfusion cross-matching procedures when minor blood group incompatibility typing is performed using antiglobulin reagents, or on newborns whose mothers have received cephalosporin antibiotics prior to delivery.", "Cefaclor should be administered with caution in patients with markedly impaired renal function. Because the half-life of cefaclor in anuria is 2.3 to 2.8 hours, dosage adjustments are generally not necessary in moderate to severe renal impairment. However, clinical observation and laboratory studies should be conducted with caution due to limited clinical experience in this patient population.", "Prescribing antibiotics, including cephalosporins, in the absence of a proven or strongly suspected bacterial infection is unlikely to provide benefit to the patient and increases the risk of the development of drug-resistant bacteria. Caution should be exercised when prescribing cephalosporins to patients with gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis.", "Effect on laboratory tests: Administration of cefaclor may result in a false-positive reaction for glucose in the urine using Benedict's and Fehling's solutions and Clinitest tablets, but not with Testape (Glucose Enzymatic Test Strip, USP).", "Reports indicate enhanced anticoagulant effects when cefaclor is administered concomitantly with oral anticoagulants.", "As with other beta-lactam antibiotics, probenecid inhibits the renal tubular secretion of cefaclor.", "Carcinogenesis, mutagenesis, impairment of fertility: Studies have not been performed to determine potential carcinogenic or mutagenic effects. Reproduction studies have revealed no evidence of impaired fertility."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Adverse effects can often be mitigated by reducing the dosage.\\nIf adverse reactions occur, discontinue use immediately and notify a physician or seek medical attention at the nearest healthcare facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:49.749	ceclor-125mg-powder-for-oral-suspension-second-generation-cephalosporin-antibacterial-agent-for	284
47	Montiget 10mg Montelukast Sodium Tablets for Prophylaxis and Chronic Treatment of Asthma (Box of 2 Blisters x 7 Tablets)	P02712	\N	\N	Mekophar	Vietnam	99750.00	79800.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250728082908-0-P02712.png?versionId=LrM6gYRx.max73L8ejRw_fzDOxx5Nsvt	Montiget 10mg tablets indicated for the prophylaxis and chronic treatment of bronchial asthma and allergic rhinitis (Box of 2 blisters x 7 tablets).	{"indications": ["Treatment and prophylaxis of symptoms of asthma and allergic rhinitis."], "sideEffects": ["Headache", "Abdominal pain", "Cough", "Dizziness", "Nausea", "Diarrhea", "Allergic reactions (rash, pruritus, swelling, severe dizziness, dyspnea)", "Psychiatric changes (agitation, hallucinations, depression, suicidal ideation and behavior)", "Eosinophilia (causing inflammatory symptoms, rash, respiratory problems, and flu-like symptoms)"], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Certain other medications", "effect": "Potential interactions may occur. Consult a physician regarding all current medications, dietary supplements, and herbal products in use."}], "effectsOnDriving": "There is no specific information regarding the effects on the ability to drive and operate machinery.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the drug."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Montiget", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store at temperatures below 30°C. Keep in a dry place, protected from direct light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.", "packaging": "Box containing 2 blisters, 7 tablets per blister.", "manufacturer": "Mekophar Chemical Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and pediatric patients aged 15 years and older: Administer 1 tablet (10mg) daily. Pediatric patients under 15 years of age: Consult a physician for appropriate dosing.", "missedDose": "No information available regarding missed doses.", "administration": "Administer in the evening. Take with water to facilitate swallowing. Swallow whole; do not chew or crush the tablet."}, "generalDescription": "Montiget 10mg tablets (2 blisters x 7 tablets) are indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms. The active pharmaceutical ingredient is Montelukast sodium, a leukotriene receptor antagonist. Montelukast works by blocking leukotrienes in the body, which reduces inflammation and prevents allergic reactions, thereby alleviating symptoms of asthma and allergic rhinitis. Montiget 10mg tablets are manufactured by Mekophar Chemical Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company and are typically packaged in a box containing 2 blisters of 7 tablets each.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Microcrystalline cellulose, Lactose monohydrate, Hydroxypropyl cellulose, Croscarmellose sodium, Magnesium stearate", "activeIngredients": "Montelukast 10mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Consult a physician prior to use.", "pediatric": "Administration to children under 15 years of age requires specific guidance from a physician to ensure appropriate dosage.", "pregnancy": "Consult a physician prior to use."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Pregnant and lactating women: Consult a physician prior to use.", "Patients with underlying medical conditions (hepatic impairment, renal impairment, cardiovascular disease, psychiatric disorders): Use with caution and consult a physician.", "Pediatric patients under 15 years of age: Specific guidance from a physician is required.", "Do not arbitrarily increase or decrease the dosage.", "Do not discontinue the medication abruptly.", "Monitor for adverse reactions and report immediately to a physician if any unusual symptoms occur.", "Store the medication in a cool, dry place, away from direct sunlight and high temperatures."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Discontinue use and contact a physician if allergic reactions or other severe symptoms occur. For mild adverse effects, continue monitoring and report to a physician if the condition does not improve or worsens. The physician may adjust the dosage or change the medication."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:15:59.587	montiget-10mg-montelukast-sodium-tablets-for-prophylaxis-and-chronic-treatment-of-asthma-box-of-2	63
48	Metformin Stella 500mg Film-Coated Tablets for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (Box of 3 Blister Packs x 10 Tablets)	P03109	\N	\N	Stella Pharm	Vietnam	27647.00	23500.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520035632-0-P03109.png?versionId=DjN3TRvW8JYNY8AKz50uhnEVaQm2HEtq	Metformin Stella 500mg tablets are indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus.	{"indications": ["This product is typically indicated for patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus whose hyperglycemia cannot be controlled by diet and exercise alone."], "sideEffects": ["Gastrointestinal: Anorexia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, epigastric fullness, constipation, heartburn.", "Skin: Rash, urticaria, photosensitivity.", "Metabolic: Decreased vitamin B12 absorption/levels.", "Hematological: Blood dyscrasias, aplastic anemia, hemolytic anemia, bone marrow depression, thrombocytopenia, agranulocytosis.", "Metabolic/Other: Lactic acidosis."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Decreased efficacy: Drugs that tend to produce hyperglycemia (e.g., diuretics, corticosteroids, phenothiazines, thyroid products, estrogens, oral contraceptives, phenytoin, nicotinic acid, sympathomimetics, calcium channel blockers, and isoniazid) may lead to a loss of blood glucose control.", "effect": ""}, {"drug": "Increased efficacy: Furosemide increases the maximum plasma and blood concentration of metformin without a significant change in metformin renal clearance during single-dose studies.", "effect": ""}, {"drug": "Increased toxicity: Cationic drugs (e.g., amiloride, digoxin, morphine, procainamide, quinidine, quinine, ranitidine, triamterene, trimethoprim, and vancomycin) that are eliminated by renal tubular secretion have the potential for interaction with metformin by competing for common renal tubular transport systems. Cimetidine increases the peak plasma and whole blood metformin concentration by 60%; therefore, coadministration of metformin and cimetidine should be avoided.", "effect": ""}], "effectsOnDriving": "Monotherapy with metformin hydrochloride does not cause hypoglycemia and therefore has no effect on the ability to drive or operate machinery. However, patients should be alerted to the risk of hypoglycemia when metformin hydrochloride is used in combination with other antidiabetic agents (such as sulfonylureas, insulin, or repaglinide).", "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Stella", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a tight container in a dry place. Do not store above 30°C.", "packaging": "Box of 3 blister packs x 10 tablets", "manufacturer": "Stellapharm Joint Venture Co., Ltd. (Vietnam)"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults: The usual starting dose is 1 tablet, twice daily (taken with breakfast and dinner). Dosage increases should be made in increments of 1 tablet every week, up to a maximum dose of 5 tablets per day. Doses up to 4 tablets per day may be administered in 2 divided doses daily. If a dose of 5 tablets per day is required, it should be divided into 3 doses per day (taken with meals) to improve gastrointestinal tolerability.\\nChildren - 10 years and adolescents: Monotherapy and in combination with insulin: The usual starting dose is 1 tablet once daily. After 10 to 15 days, the dose should be adjusted based on blood glucose measurements. The maximum recommended dose is 2g/day, divided into 2 - 3 doses.\\nElderly: The starting and maintenance doses should be conservative due to potential age-related reductions in renal function. Elderly patients should generally not be titrated to the maximum dose of metformin.\\nRecommendations for use in patients with renal impairment:\\n+ Assess renal function prior to initiation of metformin therapy and periodically thereafter.\\n+ Metformin is contraindicated in patients with an eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73m - .\\n+ Initiation of metformin therapy is not recommended in patients with an eGFR between 30 - 45 mL/min/1.73m - .\\n+ In patients taking metformin whose eGFR falls below 45 mL/min/1.73m - , assess the risk-benefit of continuing treatment.\\n+ Discontinue metformin if the patient's eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73m - .\\nHepatic impairment: Due to the risk of lactic acidosis (rarely occurring but carrying an approximately 50% mortality rate), metformin should be avoided in patients with clinical or laboratory evidence of hepatic disease.\\nDiscontinuation of metformin for iodinated radiocontrast imaging procedures:\\nIn patients with an eGFR between 30 - 60 mL/min/1.73m - , in patients with a history of hepatic impairment, alcoholism, or heart failure, or in patients who will receive intra-arterial iodinated contrast, discontinue metformin at the time of or prior to the iodinated radiocontrast imaging procedure. Re-evaluate eGFR 48 hours after the imaging procedure; restart metformin only if renal function is stable.", "administration": "Metformin Stella 500mg is administered orally, with or after meals."}, "generalDescription": "Metformin Stella 500mg tablets contain the active ingredient metformin, an oral antidiabetic agent used in the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Metformin works by decreasing hepatic glucose production, decreasing intestinal absorption of glucose, and improving insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization. This product is generally indicated for patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus who cannot achieve adequate glycemic control through diet and exercise alone. Metformin Stella 500mg tablets are conveniently packaged, easy to administer, and stable, supporting long-term and consistent therapeutic management for patients.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Povidone K25, magnesium stearate, hypromellose, Opadry white 02F28644.", "activeIngredients": "Metformin hydrochloride 500mg"}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Lactic acidosis: Postmarketing cases of metformin-associated lactic acidosis have resulted in death, hypothermia, hypotension, and resistant bradycardia. The onset of metformin-associated lactic acidosis is often subtle, accompanied only by nonspecific symptoms such as malaise, myalgia, respiratory distress, somnolence, and abdominal discomfort. Metformin-associated lactic acidosis is characterized by elevated blood lactate concentrations (> 5 mmol/L), anion gap acidosis (without evidence of ketonuria or ketonemia), and an increased lactate/pyruvate ratio; metformin plasma levels are generally > 5 mcg/mL.", "Risk factors for metformin-associated lactic acidosis include renal impairment, concomitant use of certain drugs (e.g., carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as topiramate), age 65 years old or older, radiologic studies with contrast, surgery and other procedures, hypoxic states (e.g., acute congestive heart failure), excessive alcohol intake, and hepatic impairment.", "Risk mitigation measures and management of metformin-associated lactic acidosis in high-risk patient groups are detailed in the full prescribing information.", "If metformin-associated lactic acidosis is suspected, general supportive measures should be instituted promptly in a hospital setting, along with immediate discontinuation of metformin. In patients treated with metformin who have a diagnosis or strong suspicion of lactic acidosis, prompt hemodialysis is recommended to correct the acidosis and remove accumulated metformin (metformin hydrochloride is dialyzable with a clearance of up to 170 mL/min under good hemodynamic conditions). Hemodialysis has often resulted in reversal of symptoms and recovery.", "Educate patients and their families about the symptoms of lactic acidosis and, if these symptoms occur, instruct them to discontinue metformin and report these symptoms to their physician.", "Specific recommendations to minimize the risk of and manage metformin-associated lactic acidosis for each related risk factor are as follows:", "Renal impairment: Postmarketing cases of metformin-associated lactic acidosis have occurred primarily in patients with significant renal impairment. The accumulation of metformin and risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis increase with the severity of renal impairment since metformin is substantially excreted by the kidney. Clinical recommendations based upon the patient's renal function include:", "Prior to initiation of metformin therapy, estimate the patient's glomerular filtration rate (eGFR).", "Metformin is contraindicated in patients with an eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73m - .", "Initiation of metformin therapy is not recommended in patients with an eGFR between 30 - 45 mL/min/1.73m - .", "Obtain an eGFR at least annually in all patients taking metformin. In patients at increased risk for the development of renal impairment (e.g., the elderly), renal function should be assessed more frequently.", "In patients taking metformin whose eGFR falls below 45 mL/min/1.73m - , assess the risk-benefit of continuing treatment.", "Drug interactions: The concomitant use of metformin with specific drugs may increase the risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis: those that impair renal function, result in significant hemodynamic change, interfere with acid-base balance, or increase metformin accumulation. Consider more frequent monitoring of such patients.", "Patients aged 65 years and older: The risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis increases with the patient's age because elderly patients are more likely to have hepatic, renal, or cardiac impairment than younger patients. Assess renal function more frequently in elderly patients.", "Radiologic studies involving the use of iodinated contrast agents: Administration of intravascular iodinated contrast agents in metformin-treated patients has led to an acute decrease in renal function and the occurrence of lactic acidosis. Stop metformin at the time of or prior to an iodinated radiocontrast imaging procedure in patients with an eGFR between 30 - 60 mL/min/1.73m - , in patients with a history of hepatic impairment, alcoholism, or heart failure, or in patients who will receive intra-arterial iodinated contrast. Re-evaluate eGFR 48 hours after the procedure, and restart metformin if renal function is stable.", "Surgery and other procedures: Withholding of food and fluids during surgical or other procedures may increase the risk for volume depletion, hypotension, and renal impairment. Metformin should be temporarily discontinued while patients have restricted food and fluid intake.", "Hypoxic states: Postmarketing cases of metformin-associated lactic acidosis have occurred in the setting of acute congestive heart failure (particularly when accompanied by hypoperfusion and hypoxemia). Cardiovascular collapse (shock), acute myocardial infarction, sepsis, and other conditions characterized by hypoxemia have been associated with lactic acidosis and may cause prerenal azotemia. When such events occur, discontinue metformin.", "Alcohol intake: Alcohol is known to potentiate the effect of metformin on lactate metabolism and may increase the risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis. Warn patients against excessive alcohol consumption while receiving metformin.", "Hepatic impairment: Patients with hepatic impairment have developed cases of metformin-associated lactic acidosis. This may be due to impaired lactate clearance resulting in higher blood lactate levels. Therefore, avoid use of metformin in patients with clinical or laboratory evidence of hepatic disease.", "Patients should be advised on dietary regulation, as nutritional therapy is a critical component in the management of diabetes mellitus. Metformin treatment should be considered as an adjunct to, and not a substitute for, a proper diet.", "The administration of oral antidiabetic drugs has been reported to be associated with increased cardiovascular mortality compared to treatment with diet alone or diet plus insulin.", "Driving and operating machinery: Monotherapy with metformin hydrochloride does not cause hypoglycemia and therefore has no effect on the ability to drive or operate machinery. However, patients should be alerted to the risk of hypoglycemia when metformin hydrochloride is used in combination with other antidiabetic agents (such as sulfonylureas, insulin, or repaglinide).", "Pregnancy and lactation:\\nPregnancy: Safety in pregnant women has not been established. A review of epidemiological and clinical data indicates that metformin crosses the placenta. Abnormal blood glucose levels during pregnancy are associated with a higher incidence of congenital abnormalities; therefore, most experts recommend that insulin be used during pregnancy to maintain blood glucose levels as close to normal as possible.", "Lactation: Studies in lactating rats show that metformin is excreted into milk and reaches levels comparable to those in plasma. Similar studies have not been conducted in nursing mothers. A decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother. If metformin is discontinued and diet alone is inadequate for controlling blood glucose, insulin therapy should be considered."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:16:03.889	metformin-stella-500mg-film-coated-tablets-for-type-2-diabetes-mellitus-box-of-3-blister-packs-x-10	39
49	Gabahasan 300 mg Capsule for Adjunctive Treatment of Peripheral Neuropathic Pain (Box of 3 Blisters x 10 Capsules)	P02913	\N	\N	Hasan - Dermapharm	Vietnam	147011.00	127900.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250728082826-0-P02913.png?versionId=J2Q74BXm5bt96AMBG63UZTtalRSYQo4P	Gabahasan 300 mg capsules are indicated for the adjunctive treatment of peripheral neuropathic pain and epilepsy (Box of 3 blisters x 10 capsules).	{"indications": ["The medication is effective in the treatment of epilepsy, peripheral neuralgia, and post-herpetic neuralgia."], "sideEffects": ["Adverse reactions are generally mild to moderate in intensity and tend to diminish within 2 weeks of continued treatment.", "Common: Ataxia, nystagmus, fatigue, dizziness, edema, somnolence, amnesia; children aged 3 to 12 years experience behavioral and neurological problems such as anxiety, emotional lability (crying, feelings of euphoria or depression, hyperkinesia, oppositional attitude), dyspepsia, dry mouth, constipation, abdominal pain, diarrhea; peripheral edema; rhinitis, pharyngitis, cough, pneumonia; amblyopia, decreased visual acuity; arthralgia, myalgia, pruritus, rash, leukopenia; impotence, viral infection.", "Uncommon: Amnesia, aphasia, depression, irritability or psychiatric and mood changes, hypokinesia, decreased or loss of libido, headache; gastrointestinal disorders, gingival bleeding, stomatitis, ageusia or taste disorders, hypotension, angina pectoris, peripheral vascular disorder, palpitation, weight gain, hepatomegaly.", "Rare: Nerve palsy, personality disorder, hyperesthesia, abnormal locomotor function, psychosis; peptic ulcer, esophagitis, colitis, proctitis; cough, hoarseness, respiratory tract mucosal inflammation, pulmonary hypoventilation, pulmonary edema; eye pruritus, lacrimation, retinopathy, iritis; chondritis, osteoporosis, back pain; leukopenia (usually asymptomatic), prolonged bleeding time; fever or chills; Stevens-Johnson syndrome."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Common antiepileptic drugs (carbamazepine, phenytoin, valproic acid, phenobarbital, diazepam)", "effect": "Gabapentin does not alter their pharmacokinetics."}, {"drug": "Antacids", "effect": "Decreases the bioavailability of Gabapentin by approximately 20% due to impaired absorption. Gabapentin must be administered at least 2 hours after antacid intake."}], "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to Gabapentin or any of the excipients."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Hasan", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place, below 30°C", "packaging": "Box of 03 blisters x 10 hard capsules. Clear PVC-Al blister", "manufacturer": "Hasan - Dermapharm Co., Ltd. (Vietnam)"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Epilepsy: Adults and children over 12 years: Day 1: 300mg once daily. Day 2: 300mg twice daily. Day 3: 300mg three times daily. Thereafter, the dose may be increased in increments of 300mg per day based on patient response until an effective therapeutic dose is reached, typically 900 - 1800mg/day divided into 3 doses; maximum not to exceed 2400mg/day. The total daily dose should be divided equally among administrations, and the interval between doses should not exceed 12 hours. For high doses, the regimen may be divided into 4 times/day. Patients with renal impairment and undergoing hemodialysis require dosage reduction; appropriate doses must be adjusted according to creatinine clearance. Children aged 6 to 12 years: Day 1: 10mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses. Day 2: 20mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses. Day 3: 25 - 35mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses. Maintenance dose is 900mg/day for children weighing 26 - 36kg and 1200mg/day for children weighing 37 - 50kg, total daily dose divided into 3 doses. Children aged 3 to under 6 years: Initial dose of 10 - 15mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses; titrated upward over 3 days to reach 25 - 30mg/kg/day for children aged 3 - 4 years. For children aged 5 - 6 years, the recommended dose is 25 - 30mg/kg/day, total daily dose divided into 3 doses. Treatment of peripheral neuropathic pain and post-herpetic neuralgia: Adults: Not exceeding 1800mg/day divided into 3 doses, or administered as follows: Day 1: 300mg once. Day 2: 300mg twice daily. Day 3: 300mg three times daily. Thereafter, the dose may be increased in increments of 300mg per day based on patient response up to a maximum dose of 1800mg/day, total daily dose divided into 3 doses. Elderly: Dosage may need to be reduced due to impaired renal function.", "administration": "Administer orally with or without food. Swallow whole; do not chew or crush."}, "generalDescription": "Gabapentin 300mg is an anticonvulsant and neuropathic pain-relieving medication used in adults and children over 3 years of age. It is effective in treating epilepsy, peripheral neuralgia, and post-herpetic neuralgia. The dosage is initiated at a low level and titrated upward based on patient response, with a maximum dose of up to 2400mg/day. Caution should be exercised in elderly patients and those with impaired renal function. The drug may cause adverse effects such as dizziness, somnolence, and gastrointestinal disturbances. Always keep the medication out of reach of children.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Lactose monohydrate, talc, corn starch, size 1 capsule shell.", "activeIngredients": "Gabapentin 300mg"}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Use with caution in patients with a history of psychiatric disorders, renal impairment, and those undergoing hemodialysis.", "The drug may cause false-positive results in urine protein tests.", "Discontinuation should not be abrupt as it may precipitate an increased frequency of seizures.", "If Stevens-Johnson syndrome is suspected, the medication must be discontinued immediately."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:16:07.099	gabahasan-300-mg-capsule-for-adjunctive-treatment-of-peripheral-neuropathic-pain-box-of-3-blisters	217
50	Eucalyptin 100mg Respiratory Antiseptic Soft Capsules for Respiratory Tract Antisepsis (Box of 10 Blister Packs x 10 Capsules)	P03178	\N	\N	F.T Pharma (3/2)	Vietnam	5488.00	4500.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250415090445-0-P03178.jpg?versionId=tnPSjnxvRmkte3FgqJzwzCpwdsVV8MTy	Eucalyptin soft gelatin capsules containing eucalyptol 100mg for adjunctive respiratory antisepsis, antitussive, and expectorant effects (10 blisters x 10 capsules per box).	{"indications": ["Adjunctive treatment for respiratory tract antisepsis, relief of cough, and promotion of expectoration."], "sideEffects": ["Hypersensitivity reactions or respiratory depression may occur.", "Gastrointestinal symptoms: Epigastric burning sensation, nausea, and vomiting in cases of overdose may also be observed."], "effectsOnDriving": "No formal clinical studies evaluating the effects of the product on the ability to drive and operate machinery have been conducted.", "contraindications": ["Do not use in cases of cough caused by asthma and in patients with respiratory depression (due to the respiratory depressant properties of Eucalyptin).", "Contraindicated in children under 3 years of age (used with caution due to the presence of camphor)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "F.T. Pharma", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place at temperatures below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.", "packaging": "Box of 10 blister packs x 10 capsules", "manufacturer": "F.T. Pharma"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Average dosage: Children aged 12 years and older and adults take 1 - 2 capsules per dose, 4 - 6 times daily, or as directed by a physician. This dosage form is not suitable for precise dose titration in children under 12 years of age. Note: If coughing does not improve after treatment with the standard dose, do not arbitrarily increase the dosage. Note: The dosages provided above are for reference only. The specific dosage depends on the patient's clinical condition and disease progression. To obtain an appropriate dosage regimen, consult a physician or healthcare professional.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, take it as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and resume the regular dosing schedule. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Eucalyptin is administered orally; swallow the capsules whole with an adequate amount of water.", "overdoseManagement": "No specific cases of overdose have been reported. In the event of an overdose, symptomatic and supportive treatment should be administered immediately."}, "generalDescription": "Eucalyptin, manufactured by F.T. Pharma, contains eucalyptol as its primary active ingredient. It provides respiratory antiseptic, antitussive, and expectorant effects, and is indicated as an adjunctive treatment for respiratory tract antisepsis. The medication is formulated as soft gelatin capsules, with each capsule containing 100 mg of eucalyptol.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Camphor, Bromoform, Ginger essential oil, Soybean oil, Gelatin, Glycerin, Liquid sorbitol, Titanium dioxide, Methylparaben, Propylparaben, Vanillin, Patent Blue V, Tartrazine, Green colorant, Purified water.", "activeIngredients": "Eucalyptol 100mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Data on the excretion of eucalyptol in human breast milk and its effects on nursing infants are not available; therefore, use with caution.", "pregnancy": "Adequate and well-controlled data on the use of eucalyptol in pregnant women are not available; therefore, use with caution only if clinically indicated."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Caution should be exercised when administering Eucalyptin to patients with a history of asthma and respiratory depression."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue use immediately, notify a prescribing physician, or report to the nearest medical facility for timely clinical management."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:16:11.134	eucalyptin-100mg-respiratory-antiseptic-soft-capsules-for-respiratory-tract-antisepsis-box-of-10	156
51	Calcium D (Calcium Carbonate 750mg / Colecalciferol 60 IU) Film-Coated Tablets for Prevention of Rickets and Osteoporosis (Box of 10 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P00269	\N	\N	Mekophar	Vietnam	5675.00	4370.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520034551-0-P00269.png?versionId=p_.VVLa41iEKWUUE.uv1_5WYDyTr0yqE	Calcium D film-coated tablets for calcium and vitamin D3 supplementation to prevent rickets and osteoporosis (Box of 10 blisters x 10 tablets).	{"indications": ["Calcium supplementation in the following clinical scenarios: systemic calcium deficiency, pediatric patients during growth spurts, pregnant women, nursing mothers, elderly patients with osteoporosis, and patients undergoing systemic corticosteroid therapy."], "sideEffects": ["Metabolic disorders: Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Constipation, flatulence, nausea, abdominal pain, diarrhea.", "Dermatologic disorders: Pruritus, rash, urticaria.", "Metabolic disorders: Milk-alkali syndrome.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Dyspepsia.", "In patients with impaired renal function: Risk of hyperphosphatemia, nephrolithiasis.", "Risk of hypercalcemia and hyperphosphatemia: Acute manifestations: Anorexia, headache, nausea, emesis, abdominal pain, constipation. Chronic manifestations: Soft tissue calcification, calcinosis, sensory neurological disorders."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Thiazide diuretics, cardiac glycosides, and verapamil", "effect": "Elevates serum calcium concentrations, increasing the risk of hypercalcemia and potential arrhythmias."}, {"drug": "Corticosteroids", "effect": "Decreases intestinal calcium absorption. If concurrent administration is necessary, upward dosage titration of Calcium D may be required."}, {"drug": "Preparations containing tetracyclines", "effect": "Calcium carbonate impairs gastrointestinal absorption of tetracyclines; tetracycline-class antibiotics should be administered at least 2 hours before or 4 to 6 hours after ingesting Calcium D."}, {"drug": "Cholestyramine or colestipol hydrochloride", "effect": "Reduces gastrointestinal absorption of vitamin D."}, {"drug": "Mineral oil", "effect": "Chronic or excessive administration of mineral oil may interfere with the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamin D."}, {"drug": "Quinolone antibiotics", "effect": "Bioavailability and absorption are significantly reduced when administered concurrently; fluoroquinolones should be taken 2 hours before or 6 hours after Calcium D administration."}, {"drug": "Calcium salts", "effect": "May attenuate the absorption of divalent zinc ions; oral supplementation should be spaced by at least 2 hours."}, {"drug": "Rifampicin, phenobarbital, phenytoin", "effect": "Accelerates the hepatic metabolism and elimination of vitamin D via induction of inactive metabolites."}, {"drug": "Oxalic acid (found in spinach, sorrel, rhubarb) and phytic acid (found in cereals)", "effect": "Can chelate with calcium ions to form insoluble, non-absorbable complexes, thereby inhibiting calcium absorption. Patients should avoid consuming calcium supplements within 2 hours of ingesting foods rich in oxalic or phytic acid."}], "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to any of the active substances or excipients of the medicinal product.", "Pathological conditions and diseases predisposing to hypercalcemia (such as multiple myeloma, bone metastases, or other malignant bone neoplasms, sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disorders, primary hyperparathyroidism, and vitamin D toxicity).", "Severe renal impairment (severe renal failure).", "Osteoporosis secondary to prolonged immobilization, active nephrolithiasis, severe hypercalciuria."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Mekophar", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry location, protected from light, at a controlled room temperature not exceeding 30°C.", "packaging": "Box of 10 blisters x 10 tablets", "manufacturer": "Mekophar Chemical - Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and adolescents aged > 12 years: 1 tablet 3 times daily. Pediatric patients aged 6 - 12 years: 1 tablet 2 times daily. Note: The aforementioned dosage guidelines are for reference only. Individualized dosing depends upon the patient's clinical status and disease progression. Consult a physician or qualified healthcare professional to determine the optimal therapeutic dosage.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, administer it as soon as remembered. However, if the scheduled time for the subsequent dose is near, omit the missed dose and resume the standard dosing schedule. Do not administer a double dose to compensate for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "For oral administration, may be ingested with or without food. There are no specialized handling requirements prior to or following administration.", "overdoseManagement": "Supratherapeutic doses may precipitate clinical manifestations of hypercalcemia. Initial symptoms of hypercalcemia include: muscular weakness, fatigue, lethargy, headache, anorexia, xerostomia (dry mouth), dysgeusia (metallic taste), nausea, emesis, abdominal pain, constipation, dizziness, tinnitus, ataxia, rash, hypotonia, myalgia, and bone pain. Delayed complications of hypercalcemia comprise: nephrocalcinosis, nephrolithiasis, and renal impairment (manifested as polyuria, nocturia, polydipsia, or impaired urine-concentrating ability). Management protocol: Discontinue administration of the medicinal product and any concurrent calcium or vitamin D supplements; institute a low-calcium diet and ensure adequate hydration via oral or intravenous fluids. If clinically indicated, administer loop diuretics (e.g., furosemide, ethacrynic acid) or corticosteroids to enhance urinary calcium excretion and lower serum calcium levels. Hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis may be implemented in severe refractory cases. If acute ingestion occurred recently, perform gastric lavage or induce emesis. If the medication has transited beyond the stomach, administer mineral oil to facilitate fecal elimination. Once serum calcium normalizes, therapy may be cautiously reinstituted at a reduced dosage if medically necessary. Maintain rigorous clinical and laboratory monitoring."}, "generalDescription": "Calcium D, manufactured by Mekophar Chemical - Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company, contains calcium carbonate and colecalciferol (vitamin D3) as its primary active pharmaceutical ingredients. This medicinal product is indicated for calcium supplementation in states of calcium deficiency, growing pediatric populations, pregnant and lactating women, geriatric patients with osteoporosis, and patients receiving corticosteroid therapy. Calcium D is formulated as green, oblong, film-coated tablets featuring a bilateral break score line.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Magnesium stearate, Sodium starch glycolate, Colloidal silicon dioxide, Talc, Sodium lauryl sulfate, Cellactose 80, Hydroxypropylcellulose, Opadry green.", "activeIngredients": "Calcium carbonate 750 mg (equivalent to Elemental Calcium 300 mg), Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) 60 IU."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Safe and suitable for geriatric patients under appropriate monitoring.", "lactation": "Can be safely administered during lactation.", "pediatric": "Safe and effective for pediatric patients over 6 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Can be safely administered during pregnancy when indicated for nutritional supplementation."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Administer with extreme caution in patients with renal impairment, nephrolithiasis, cardiovascular disease, or atherosclerosis. In the event of hypercalcemia or clinical evidence of declining renal function, the dosage must be reduced or treatment permanently discontinued.", "Reduce the dose or temporarily suspend therapy if urinary calcium excretion exceeds 7.5 mmol/24 hours (300 mg/24 hours).", "Exercise heightened caution when prescribing this medicinal product to patients concurrently receiving cardiac glycosides or thiazide diuretics, as acute hypercalcemia can precipitate lethal cardiac arrhythmias in this population.", "The use of calcium supplements in immobilized patients with osteoporosis significantly increases the propensity for severe hypercalcemia.", "Monitor serum inorganic phosphate levels during protracted therapy to mitigate the risk of ectopic soft-tissue calcification.", "Regularly monitor serum calcium levels, particularly during the initiation phase of therapy or when clinical signs of potential toxicity emerge.", "This medicinal product contains lactose; consequently, it should be administered with caution in patients with rare hereditary conditions such as galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Regularly monitor serum calcium concentrations, maintaining target levels within 9 - 10 mg/dL (4.5 - 5 mEq/L). Serum calcium levels should generally not surpass 11 mg/dL. Encourage high fluid intake or administer intravenous hydration to secure high urine output and prevent renal stone formation in patients exhibiting hypercalciuria. Promptly report any adverse events or unexpected systemic reactions observed during therapy to a physician or pharmacist."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:16:14.397	calcium-d-calcium-carbonate-750mg-colecalciferol-60-iu-film-coated-tablets-for-prevention-of	63
