 NSAIDs."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Voltaren", "origin": "No detailed information provided in the source data.", "storage": "Store in a cool place, protected from light, at temperatures below 30°C.", "packaging": "No detailed information provided in the source data.", "manufacturer": "No detailed information provided in the source data."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "The recommended starting daily dose is 100 mg to 150 mg, administered as one 100 mg Voltaren sustained-release tablet or two 75 mg Voltaren sustained-release tablets. For milder cases and long-term therapy, 75°100 mg daily is usually sufficient. If symptoms are predominantly nocturnal or morning-related, Voltaren 75 mg or 100 mg sustained-release tablets should be taken in the evening. Not suitable for children and adolescents. No dosage adjustment is initially required in elderly patients. Caution should be exercised when administered to patients with mild to moderate renal or hepatic impairment.", "missedDose": "If you miss a dose, take it as soon as you remember. However, if it is almost time for your next dose, skip the missed dose and resume your regular dosing schedule. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Swallow the tablet whole with liquid, preferably with a meal, and do not crush or chew.", "overdoseManagement": "In case of emergency, call 911 immediately or go to the nearest emergency medical facility."}, "generalDescription": "Voltaren SR 75mg is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) possessing analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory properties.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "No detailed excipient information provided in the source data.", "activeIngredients": "Diclofenac sodium"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "No dosage adjustment of the starting dose is required in elderly patients. Caution should be exercised in elderly patients with underlying medical conditions. The lowest effective dose is specifically recommended for frail or low-weight elderly patients.", "lactation": "Voltaren should not be used during breastfeeding to avoid potential adverse effects on the nursing infant.", "pediatric": "Due to the dosage strength, Voltaren 75mg and 100mg sustained-release tablets are not suitable for use in children and adolescents.", "pregnancy": "Voltaren should not be used during the first and second trimesters of pregnancy unless the expected benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risk to the fetus. Diclofenac is strictly contraindicated during the third trimester of pregnancy."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Gastrointestinal effects: Ulceration, bleeding, or gastrointestinal perforation, which can be fatal, have been reported with all NSAIDs, including diclofenac. Combination therapy with protective agents (e.g., proton pump inhibitors or misoprostol) should be considered for these patients, as well as for patients requiring concomitant use of low-dose acetylsalicylic acid (ASA)/aspirin or other drugs likely to increase gastrointestinal risk. Close medical supervision and caution are mandatory in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease, as their condition may be exacerbated.", "Cardiovascular effects: Treatment with NSAIDs, including diclofenac, particularly at high doses and for long-term use, may be associated with a small increased risk of serious cardiovascular thrombotic events. Combination therapy with protective agents should be considered, and close monitoring is advised.", "Hematologic effects: During prolonged treatment with Voltaren, as with other NSAIDs, blood cell counts should be monitored. Careful monitoring is required for patients with coagulation disorders.", "Respiratory effects (pre-existing asthma): Reactions to NSAIDs such as asthma exacerbations, angioedema, or urticaria are more frequent in these patients. Special caution is required (emergency readiness). Particular attention must also be paid to patients with a history of other allergies, such as skin rashes, pruritus, or urticaria.", "Hepatobiliary effects: Close medical surveillance is required when prescribing Voltaren to patients with impaired hepatic function, as their condition may worsen. Caution should be exercised when administering Voltaren to patients with hepatic porphyria, as it may trigger an acute attack.", "Skin reactions: Serious skin reactions, some fatal, including exfoliative dermatitis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and toxic epidermal necrolysis, have been reported very rarely in association with the use of NSAIDs, including Voltaren. Allergic reactions, including anaphylactic/anaphylactoid reactions, may also occur in rare cases with diclofenac without prior exposure.", "Renal effects: Treatment with NSAIDs, including diclofenac, is frequently associated with fluid retention and edema; therefore, special caution is warranted in patients with impaired cardiac or renal function, a history of hypertension, elderly patients, patients receiving concomitant diuretic therapy or drugs that significantly impact renal function, and patients with extracellular volume depletion from any cause.", "Elderly patients: Caution is indicated in elderly patients with underlying medical conditions. In particular, the use of the lowest effective dose is recommended in frail elderly patients or those with a low body weight.", "Masking signs of infection: Voltaren may mask fever and other signs of infection."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue use immediately and notify a physician or proceed to the nearest medical facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:16:20.947	voltaren-sr-75mg-controlled-release-tablets-for-inflammation-and-joint-pain-blister-pack-of-10-x-10	285
