.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Lactose monohydrate, maize starch, talc, magnesium stearate.", "activeIngredients": "Loperamide hydrochloride 2mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "No dosage adjustment is required.", "lactation": "Not recommended during breastfeeding. Consult a physician before use.", "pregnancy": "Not recommended, especially during the first trimester of pregnancy. Consult a physician before use."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Symptomatic treatment only; fluid and electrolyte replacement must be instituted when appropriate. Do not use prolonged therapy without determining the underlying cause. Discontinue use if clinical improvement is not observed within 48 hours. Use with caution in patients with hepatic impairment, AIDS patients (due to the risk of toxic megacolon), and individuals with galactose intolerance. Special warning for patients with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) over 40 years of age or presenting with alarm symptoms (rectal bleeding, severe constipation, weight loss, fever, etc.)."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Notify your healthcare provider or physician immediately if you experience any adverse reactions or side effects while taking this medication."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:17:40.573	imodium-2mg-loperamide-hydrochloride-capsules-for-acute-diarrhea-box-of-25-blister-packs-x-4	289
76	Lipanthyl Supra 160mg Fenofibrate Film-Coated Tablets for Dyslipidemia (Box of 3 Blisters x 10 Tablets)	P00901	\N	\N	\N	Vietnam	482564.00	376400.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520034945-0-P00901.png?versionId=f9ujxYcXTCsgJVH5fbR_XwSTPsKHWPGf	Lipanthyl Supra 160mg film-coated tablets containing fenofibrate, indicated for the treatment of dyslipidemia (Box of 3 blisters x 10 tablets).	{"detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Fenofibrate 160mg"}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["<h2>Potential Adverse Effects</h2>", "<p>When using Lipanthyl Supra 160 mg, patients may experience certain adverse drug reactions (ADRs). Below is detailed information on possible adverse effects:</p>", "<h4>Common Adverse Reactions (incidence &gt; 1/100)</h4>", "<ul>", "<li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Moderate gastrointestinal disturbances such as abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and flatulence.</li>", "<div><strong>Hepatobiliary:</strong> Moderate elevations in serum transaminases.</div>", "</ul>", "<h4>Uncommon Adverse Reactions (1/1000 &lt; ADR &lt; 1/100)</h4>", "<ul>", "<li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Acute pancreatitis.</li>", "<li><strong>Hepatobiliary:</strong> Cholelithiasis (increased gallstone size).</li>", "<li><strong>Skin:</strong> Rash, pruritus, urticaria.</li>", "<li><strong>Musculoskeletal and Connective Tissue:</strong> Diffuse myalgia, myositis, muscle cramps, and muscle weakness.</li>", "<li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Thromboembolic events, including pulmonary embolism and deep vein thrombosis.</li>", "<li><strong>Nervous System:</strong> Headache.</li>", "<li><strong>Reproductive:</strong> Sexual dysfunction.</li>", "<li><strong>Renal:</strong> Increased serum creatinine and urea, elevated blood homocysteine levels.</li>", "</ul>", "<h4>Rare Adverse Reactions (1/10000 &lt; ADR &lt; 1/1000)</h4>", "<ul>", "<li><strong>Skin:</strong> Alopecia, photosensitivity reactions presenting with erythema, vesiculation, or papules on skin areas exposed to sunlight or artificial UV radiation.</li>", "<li><strong>Blood and Lymphatic System:</strong> Decreased hemoglobin levels and white blood cell count.</li>", "</ul>", "<h4>Very Rare Adverse Reactions (ADR &lt; 1/10000)</h4>", "<ul>", "<li><strong>Hepatobiliary:</strong> Hepatitis.</li>", "</ul>", "<h4>Adverse Reactions with Unknown Frequency</h4>", "<ul>", "<li><strong>Musculoskeletal and Connective Tissue:</strong> Rhabdomyolysis.</li>", "<li><strong>Respiratory, Thoracic, and Mediastinal:</strong> Interstitial lung disease.</li>", "</ul>", "<h4>Management of Adverse Reactions</h4>", "<p>If adverse reactions occur while using Lipanthyl Supra, patients should immediately inform their physician for timely clinical evaluation and management.</p>", "<h3><strong>Precautions</strong></h3>", "<p>Before using this medication, read the instructions carefully and refer to the clinical information below.</p>", "<h4>Contraindications</h4>", "<p><strong>Lipanthyl Supra 160 mg</strong> is contraindicated in the following clinical scenarios:</p>", "<ul>", "<li>Pediatric patients;</li>", "<li>Pregnant or breastfeeding women;</li>", "<li>Patients with severe hepatic impairment (including primary biliary cirrhosis and unexplained persistent liver function test abnormalities);</li>", "<li>Patients with severe renal impairment (estimated creatinine clearance &lt; 20 mL/min or equivalent eGFR);</li>", "<li>Known hypersensitivity to fenofibrate or any of the inactive excipients;</li>", "<li>History of photoallergy or phototoxicity during treatment with fibrates or ketoprofen;</li>", "<li>Acute or chronic pancreatitis, except when associated with severe hypertriglyceridemia;</li>", "<li>Gallbladder disease;</li>", "<li>Lipanthyl Supra must not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to peanuts, peanut oil, soybean lecithin, or related derivatives due to the risk of allergic reactions.</li>", "</ul>", "<h4>Warnings and Precautions</h4>", "<p>Secondary causes of hypercholesterolemia, such as poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus, primary hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, dysproteinemia, obstructive liver disease undergoing treatment, and chronic alcoholism, should be adequately diagnosed and treated prior to initiating fenofibrate therapy.</p>", "<p>Monitor therapeutic response by periodic measurement of serum lipid profiles (total cholesterol, LDL-C, triglycerides). If an adequate therapeutic response is not achieved after several months (e.g., 3 months), consideration should be given to combination therapy or alternative therapeutic interventions.</p>", "<p>In hyperlipidemic patients concurrently taking estrogens or estrogen-containing oral contraceptives, it must be determined whether the hyperlipidemia is primary or secondary, as lipid elevations may be directly attributable to estrogen administration.</p>", "<p><em>Hepatic Function:</em></p><p>Elevations in serum transaminases have been reported in some patients. Most of these increases were transient, minor, and clinically asymptomatic. Routine monitoring of serum transaminase levels is recommended every 3 months during the first 12 months of treatment and periodically thereafter.</p>", "<p>Clinical vigilance is required for patients who develop rising transaminase levels, and therapy must be permanently discontinued if AST and ALT levels persistently exceed 3 times the upper limit of normal (ULN).</p>", "<p><em>Pancreatitis:</em></p><p>Pancreatitis has been reported in patients receiving fenofibrate. This may represent treatment failure in patients with severe hypertriglyceridemia or a secondary phenomenon related to biliary sludge or common bile duct calculi.</p>", "<p><em>Musculoskeletal System:</em></p><p>Skeletal muscle toxicity, including rare cases of rhabdomyolysis, has been reported with fibrates and other lipid-lowering agents. The incidence of this condition is increased in the presence of hypoalbuminemia and pre-existing renal insufficiency.</p>", "<p>The risk of rhabdomyolysis may be increased in patients with predisposing risk factors for myopathy and/or rhabdomyolysis, including age over 70 years, personal or family history of hereditary muscular disorders, renal impairment, hypothyroidism, and heavy alcohol intake. The benefit-risk ratio of fenofibrate therapy must be carefully evaluated in such patients.</p>", "<p>Myotoxicity should be suspected in patients presenting with diffuse myalgia, myositis accompanied by painful muscle spasms, cramps, and profound muscle weakness, and/or marked creatine phosphokinase (CPK) elevations exceeding 5 times the upper limit of normal. Fenofibrate therapy must be discontinued immediately if myopathy is diagnosed or suspected.</p>", "<p>The risk of muscle toxicity is significantly increased if fenofibrate is co-administered with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins) or other fibrates, particularly in patients with pre-existing muscle disease. Therefore, combination therapy of fenofibrate with a statin or another fibrate should be prescribed with extreme caution in patients without a history of muscular disease but with severe mixed dyslipidemia and high cardiovascular risk. Close clinical and biochemical monitoring for myotoxicity is mandatory.</p>", "<p>The recommended dosage for elderly patients without renal impairment is identical to the adult dosage. Since this medicinal product contains lactose, patients with rare hereditary conditions of galactose intolerance, total lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medication.</p>", "<p><em>Renal Function:</em></p><p>Fenofibrate therapy should be discontinued if serum creatinine levels increase by more than 50% above the upper limit of normal (ULN). Routine serum creatinine monitoring is recommended during the first 3 months of treatment.</p>", "<h4>Effects on Ability to Drive and Use Machines</h4>", "<p>Fenofibrate has no or negligible influence on the ability to drive and operate machinery.</p>", "<h4>Pregnancy</h4>", "<p>There are no adequate and well-controlled clinical data on the use of fenofibrate in pregnant women. Animal reproductive studies have not demonstrated teratogenic effects; however, embryotoxic and fetotoxic effects have been observed at maternally toxic doses. Therefore, fenofibrate should only be prescribed during pregnancy after a rigorous individual benefit-risk assessment.</p>", "<h4>Breastfeeding</h4>", "<p>It remains unknown whether fenofibrate and/or its active metabolites are excreted in human breast milk. A potential risk to breastfed newborns and infants cannot be excluded. Consequently, fenofibrate must not be used during lactation.</p>", "<h4>Drug Interactions</h4>", "<p>Concomitant administration of fenofibrate and oral anticoagulants (such as warfarin) is not recommended. Fenofibrate may potentiate the anticoagulant effect of vitamin K antagonists, thereby increasing the risk of hemorrhage. If co-administration is clinically necessary, the anticoagulant dosage should be reduced by approximately one-third at the start of treatment and gradually retitrated based on frequent International Normalized Ratio (INR) monitoring.</p>", "<p>Reversible acute renal impairment has been reported during concurrent administration of fenofibrate and calcineurin inhibitors such as cyclosporine. Renal function must be closely monitored, and fenofibrate therapy must be discontinued in the event of severe deterioration of renal laboratory parameters.</p>", "<p>The risk of severe myotoxicity is markedly elevated when fibrates are used concomitantly with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors or other fibrates. Extreme caution and rigorous clinical monitoring are required for such combination regimens.</p>", "<p>In vitro and clinical studies indicate that at therapeutic concentrations, fenofibrate is a weak inhibitor of cytochrome P450 enzymes CYP2C19 and CYP2A6, and a mild-to-moderate inhibitor of CYP2C9. Patients co-administered fenofibrate and drugs metabolized by CYP2C19, CYP2A6, and particularly CYP2C9 with a narrow therapeutic index should be carefully monitored, and dosage adjustments of those concomitantly administered drugs are recommended when clinically indicated.</p>", "<p><em>Disclaimer: All the clinical and pharmacological information above is provided for reference purposes only. The administration of this medication must strictly follow the direct instructions of a licensed physician or clinical pharmacist. Please consult the complete prescribing information leaflet enclosed within the product packaging.</em></p>"]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:17:45.041	lipanthyl-supra-160mg-fenofibrate-film-coated-tablets-for-dyslipidemia-box-of-3-blisters-x-10	\N
