professional if symptoms persist, worsen, or if additional clinical guidance is required.", "Exercise caution in young children due to the potential choking hazard associated with hard lozenges."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If any adverse drug reactions or hypersensitivity symptoms occur, discontinue use immediately and consult a qualified physician or seek prompt medical attention at the nearest healthcare facility for appropriate clinical management."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:21:49.863	strepsils-throat-irritation-and-cough-ambroxol-hydrochloride-15mg-lozenges-for-sore-throat-and	300
150	Simvastatin Stella 20mg Tablets for Hypercholesterolemia and Dyslipidemia (3 Blister Packs of 10 Tablets)	P05477	\N	\N	\N	Vietnam	54375.00	43500.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250417102203-0-P05477.jpg?versionId=eS3Six73ku7w0xzGuPOOmDBRtOqn9GCJ	Simvastatin Stella 20mg tablets contain simvastatin, indicated for the treatment of hypercholesterolemia and mixed dyslipidemia, and the reduction of cardiovascular risk.	{"indications": ["Treatment of primary hypercholesterolemia or mixed dyslipidemia, as an adjunct to diet, when response to diet and other non-pharmacological measures (exercise, weight reduction) is inadequate.", "Treatment of homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia as an adjunct to diet and other lipid-lowering therapies (e.g., LDL apheresis) or when such therapies are not appropriate.", "Reduction of cardiovascular morbidity and mortality in patients with symptomatic atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease or diabetes mellitus, with either normal or elevated cholesterol levels."], "sideEffects": ["Gastrointestinal disorders: Diarrhea, constipation, flatulence, abdominal pain, nausea.", "Nervous system disorders: Headache, dizziness, blurred vision, insomnia, asthenia.", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Myalgia, arthralgia, myopathy, myositis, rhabdomyolysis.", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Elevations of serum transaminases.", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Rash.", "Respiratory, thoracic, and mediastinal disorders: Rhinitis, sinusitis, pharyngitis, cough.", "Metabolism and nutrition disorders: Cognitive impairment, hyperglycemia, elevated HbA1c."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Potent CYP3A4 inhibitors", "effect": "Significantly increased systemic exposure to simvastatin, leading to an increased risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis."}, {"drug": "HIV protease inhibitors", "effect": "Potentiated risk of skeletal muscle toxicity, myopathy, and acute renal failure."}, {"drug": "Amiodarone, verapamil, danazol", "effect": "Increased risk of statin-induced myopathy and rhabdomyolysis."}, {"drug": "Amlodipine, fusidic acid", "effect": "Increased risk of severe myopathy and rhabdomyolysis."}, {"drug": "Rifampicin", "effect": "Decreased area under the plasma concentration-time curve (AUC) of simvastatin, potentially reducing therapeutic efficacy."}, {"drug": "Coumarin anticoagulants", "effect": "Potential enhancement of the anticoagulant effect, leading to increased risk of bleeding and prolonged prothrombin time / INR."}, {"drug": "Grapefruit juice", "effect": "Inhibition of intestinal CYP3A4, resulting in elevated plasma concentrations of simvastatin."}, {"drug": "Gemfibrozil, fibrates, niacin, colchicine", "effect": "Additive risk of severe skeletal muscle injury and rhabdomyolysis."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Simvastatin has negligible or no influence on the ability to drive and use machines. However, dizziness has been rarely reported in post-marketing surveillance, and patients experiencing this adverse reaction should exercise caution.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Selectively and competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-coenzyme A to mevalonate, an early precursor in hepatic cholesterol biosynthesis.", "pharmacologicalClass": "HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors (Statins)."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Simvastatin is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract. Peak plasma concentrations are attained approximately 1 to 2 hours post-dose. Food intake does not significantly affect systemic absorption.", "metabolism": "Simvastatin is an inactive lactone prodrug that undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism in the liver, where it is hydrolyzed to its active beta-hydroxyacid form, a potent HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor. It is a primary substrate of the cytochrome P450 enzyme CYP3A4.", "elimination": "Approximately 13% of the administered radiolabeled dose is recovered in urine and 60% in feces within 96 hours. The mean elimination half-life of the active beta-hydroxyacid metabolite is approximately 1.9 hours."}, "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to simvastatin or any excipients contained in the formulation.", "Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations of serum transaminases.", "Pregnancy and lactation.", "Concomitant administration with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., itraconazole, ketoconazole, posaconazole, erythromycin, clarithromycin, telithromycin, HIV protease inhibitors, boceprevir, telaprevir, nefazodone, gemfibrozil, cyclosporine, and danazol)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"storage": "Store in airtight containers in a dry place, protected from light, at temperatures not exceeding 30°C."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "The usual initial dosage range is 10 mg to 20 mg once daily. The maximum recommended dose is 80 mg daily. For patients with severe renal impairment, the recommended starting dose is 5 mg daily. Dosage adjustments should be made at intervals of no less than 4 weeks.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. If it is almost time for the subsequent scheduled dose, omit the missed dose and resume the standard dosing schedule. Do not double doses.", "administration": "Administer orally as a single daily dose in the evening, with or without food.", "overdoseManagement": "There is no specific antidote for simvastatin overdose. Administer symptomatic and supportive care as clinically indicated."}, "generalDescription": "Simvastatin is a lipid-lowering agent derived synthetically from a fermentation product of Aspergillus terreus, belonging to the class of 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-coenzyme A (HMG-CoA) reductase inhibitors.", "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Simvastatin 20mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Baseline renal function should be assessed prior to initiating treatment in elderly patients.", "lactation": "Contraindicated during lactation. It is unknown whether simvastatin is excreted in human breast milk.", "pediatric": "Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients under 10 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated during pregnancy. Cholesterol and its biosynthetic pathway derivatives are essential for fetal development."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Liver function tests and baseline creatine kinase (CK) levels should be evaluated prior to initiating therapy. Exercise caution in patients over 65 years of age, those with renal impairment, hypothyroidism, a personal or familial history of hereditary muscular disorders, or a history of substantial alcohol consumption. Therapy must be promptly discontinued if markedly elevated CK levels occur or if myopathy is diagnosed or suspected."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:21:53.075	simvastatin-stella-20mg-tablets-for-hypercholesterolemia-and-dyslipidemia-3-blister-packs-of-10	\N
