agonism with lincomycin due to competitive binding at the 50S ribosomal subunit; co-administration should be avoided."}, {"drug": "Food and Sodium Cyclamate", "effect": "Ingestion of food significantly decreases and delays the absorption rate of oral lincomycin."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No formal studies on the effects of lincomycin on the ability to drive or operate machinery have been performed; however, adverse effects such as dizziness or fatigue may impair performance.", "contraindications": ["Known hypersensitivity to lincomycin, clindamycin, or any components of the formulation. Pre-existing non-antibiotic-associated colitis, or minor bacterial/viral infections."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Vidipha", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, protected from direct light and moisture.", "packaging": "Box of 10 blisters x 10 capsules", "manufacturer": "Vidipha Central Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults: 500 mg orally every 6 to 8 hours for serious infections, or up to 500 mg every 8 hours for less severe infections. Pediatric patients over 1 month of age: 30 to 60 mg/kg/day divided into 3 or 4 equal doses, depending on infection severity. Renal Impairment: Dose reduction to 25-30% of the normal dosage interval is recommended for severe renal dysfunction.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. If it is almost time for the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and resume the regular dosing schedule. Do not double doses to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "For oral administration. To ensure optimal absorption, take with a full glass of water on an empty stomach, at least 1 to 2 hours before or after meals.", "overdoseManagement": "Clinical Manifestations: Symptoms of overdosage may include abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, gastrointestinal irritation, fatigue, dizziness, hypotension, and neuromuscular blockade. Management: Discontinue the drug immediately and institute symptomatic and supportive measures. Gastric lavage may be indicated."}, "generalDescription": "Lincomycin 500mg is a lincosamide antibiotic that inhibits protein synthesis in bacteria by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit, indicated for infections susceptible to its antimicrobial spectrum.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Magnesium stearate, colloidal silicon dioxide, talc, microcrystalline cellulose", "activeIngredients": "Lincomycin 500mg (as Lincomycin hydrochloride monohydrate)"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Increased susceptibility to severe diarrhea and pseudomembranous colitis; monitor clinical response carefully.", "lactation": "Lincomycin is excreted in human breast milk. Due to the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants, breastfeeding should be discontinued during treatment.", "pediatric": "Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients under 1 month of age have not been established; use is discouraged.", "pregnancy": "Lincomycin readily crosses the placental barrier. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed and after a careful risk-benefit assessment by a physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Prescribe with extreme caution in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, or antibiotic-associated colitis.", "Elderly patients and debilitated females may experience more severe episodes of diarrhea; monitor closely for changes in bowel frequency.", "Use with caution in patients with severe renal or hepatic impairment, and perform periodic organ function tests during prolonged therapy.", "Long-term administration necessitates regular monitoring of complete blood counts, renal function, and hepatic enzymes.", "Due to intrinsic neuromuscular blocking properties, administer cautiously to patients receiving concurrent neuromuscular blocking agents.", "Safety and effectiveness in neonates under 1 month of age have not been established."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Discontinue therapy immediately upon the onset of severe diarrhea, hypersensitivity reactions, or pseudomembranous colitis, and contact a physician for appropriate medical intervention."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:27:21.524	vdp-lincomycin-500mg-capsules-for-bacterial-infections-10-blisters-x-10-capsules	1
1231	Anpemux 250mg Capsules (Carbocisteine) for Mucolysis, Expectorant Action, and Cough Relief (6 Blister Packs x 10 Capsules)	P13319	\N	\N	Ha Tay Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company	Group 4, La Khe Ward, Ha Dong District, Hanoi, Vietnam	20833.00	15000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250415090926-0-P13319.jpg?versionId=mIaWYhgsMRXBz81qHZZ2BLSO9QlnPj7L	Anpemux 250mg capsules are indicated for mucolytic and expectorant therapy, as well as symptomatic relief of cough in respiratory disorders.	{"indications": ["Indicated as a mucolytic and expectorant agent for the symptomatic treatment of acute and chronic bronchopulmonary diseases characterized by abnormal mucus secretion and impaired mucus transport."], "sideEffects": ["Common: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, anorexia", "Uncommon: Erythematous rash, pruritic urticaria, skin eruptions"], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Amoxicillin sodium", "effect": "Concomitant administration of carbocisteine increases the bioavailability and tissue penetration of amoxicillin sodium into pulmonary secretions."}, {"drug": "Cimetidine", "effect": "Decreases the renal clearance and urinary excretion of Carbocisteine."}], "effectsOnDriving": "This medicinal product has no or negligible influence on the ability to drive and use machines.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to Carbocisteine or any of the excipients contained in the formulation", "Pediatric patients under 2 years of age", "Patients with active peptic ulcer disease"], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Ha Tay", "origin": "Group 4, La Khe Ward, Ha Dong District, Hanoi, Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place at temperatures below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.", "packaging": "Carton containing 6 blister packs of 10 capsules each", "manufacturer": "Ha Tay Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and adolescents over 15 years of age: 3 capsules (750 mg) administered three times daily. Pediatric patients aged 5 to 15 years: 1 capsule (250 mg) administered three times daily.", "administration": "Administer orally with lukewarm or cooled boiled water, 1 to 2 hours postprandially. Swallow capsules whole without chewing, breaking, or crushing. Avoid co-administration with tea, carbonated soft drinks, coffee, fruit juices, or alcoholic beverages."}, "generalDescription": "Anpemux is a respiratory therapeutic agent manufactured by Hatay Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company, Vietnam, possessing mucolytic, expectorant, and secretolytic properties.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Talc, magnesium stearate, microcrystalline cellulose, orange flavor powder, peach flavor powder, q.s. for one capsule.", "activeIngredients": "Carbocisteine 250mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "May be used during lactation when clearly indicated and clinically necessary.", "pregnancy": "Use during pregnancy only if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus, as determined by the treating physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Use with caution in patients with a history of peptic ulcer disease or gastrointestinal bleeding. Discontinue use immediately if gastrointestinal bleeding occurs."], "adverseReactionManagement": "In the event of severe adverse reactions or overdosage, discontinue treatment immediately and consult a physician, pharmacist, or seek professional medical assistance for appropriate supportive management."}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:27:28.331	anpemux-250mg-capsules-carbocisteine-for-mucolysis-expectorant-action-and-cough-relief-6-blister	312
1240	Natrixam 1.5mg/5mg Prolonged-Release Tablets (Indapamide/Amlodipine) for Essential Hypertension (6 Blister Packs x 5 Tablets)	P12613	\N	\N	Servier	France	271831.00	193000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520071517-0-P12613.png?versionId=K31wxQKQPWgYGpHLHtDHbq0vEwfbxnz.	Natrixam 1.5mg/5mg prolonged-release tablets contain a fixed-dose combination of indapamide and amlodipine, indicated for the management of essential hypertension.	{"indications": ["This medicinal product is indicated for the treatment of essential hypertension in patients already controlled with indapamide and amlodipine administered concurrently at the same dose levels."], "sideEffects": ["Reported adverse drug reactions (ADRs) include hypokalemia, somnolence, dizziness, headache, visual impairment, diplopia, palpitations, flushing, dyspnea, abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia, altered bowel habits, diarrhea, constipation, maculopapular rash, ankle swelling, muscle cramps, peripheral edema, fatigue, and asthenia."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Indapamide", "effect": "Contraindicated or Not Recommended Combinations: Lithium: Concomitant administration may increase plasma lithium concentrations, resulting in clinical symptoms of overdose comparable to a sodium-restricted diet due to decreased renal lithium clearance. If diuretic therapy is unavoidable, rigorous monitoring of serum lithium levels and dosage titration are mandatory.\\nPrecautions Required: Torsades de Pointes-inducing agents (e.g., Class Ia and III antiarrhythmics, certain antipsychotics, benzamides, butyrophenones, bepridil, cisapride, diphemanil, intravenous erythromycin, halofantrine, mizolastine, pentamidine, sparfloxacin, moxifloxacin, intravenous vincamine): Elevated risk of ventricular arrhythmias, particularly Torsades de Pointes, facilitated by hypokalemia. Serum potassium must be evaluated and corrected prior to initiating therapy, alongside continuous clinical, electrolyte, and electrocardiographic (ECG) monitoring. Non-Torsades-inducing alternatives should be utilized if hypokalemia is present.\\nSystemic Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs), including selective COX-2 inhibitors and high-dose acetylsalicylic acid ( - 3 g/day): Potential attenuation of the antihypertensive efficacy of indapamide and increased risk of acute renal failure in volume-depleted patients secondary to decreased glomerular filtration. Volume repletion and baseline renal function monitoring are required upon initiation.\\nAngiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) Inhibitors: Risk of abrupt hypotension and/or acute renal failure upon initiation of ACE inhibitor therapy in patients with pre-existing sodium depletion, particularly in renal artery stenosis. If prior diuretic therapy induced hyponatremia, the diuretic must be discontinued 3 days prior to initiating the ACE inhibitor (with subsequent reintroduction of the potassium-depleting diuretic if necessary) or the ACE inhibitor must be initiated at a low dose with gradual upward titration. In congestive heart failure, ACE inhibitor therapy should be initiated at a very low dose, potentially following a reduction in the concomitant diuretic dosage. Renal function (plasma creatinine) must be monitored during the initial weeks of ACE inhibitor therapy.\\nOther Potassium-Depleting Agents (intravenous amphotericin B, systemic glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids, tetracosactide, stimulant laxatives): Synergistic risk of hypokalemia. Regular monitoring of serum potassium and correction when indicated are necessary, with particular caution during concurrent digitalis therapy. Non-stimulant laxatives should be preferred.\\nDigitalis Glycosides: Hypokalemia exacerbates digitalis-induced cardiac toxicity. Serum potassium levels and ECG parameters must be monitored closely, and treatment adjusted as clinically appropriate.\\nBaclofen: Potentiation of the antihypertensive effect. Volume repletion and renal function monitoring are required at treatment initiation.\\nAllopurinol: Co-administration with indapamide may increase the incidence of hypersensitivity reactions to allopurinol.\\nCombinations Requiring Consideration: Potassium-sparing diuretics (amiloride, spironolactone, triamterene): While beneficial in specific populations, the risk of hypokalemia or hyperkalemia remains, particularly in patients with renal impairment or diabetes mellitus. Serum potassium and ECG monitoring are required.\\nMetformin: Lactic acidosis secondary to metformin may be precipitated by functional renal impairment associated with diuretics, particularly loop diuretics. Metformin is contraindicated if plasma creatinine exceeds 15 mg/L (135°mol/L) in males and 12 mg/L (110°mol/L) in females.\\nIodinated Contrast Media: Diuretic-induced dehydration increases the risk of acute renal failure, particularly when high doses of iodinated contrast media are administered. Rehydration must be performed prior to contrast administration.\\nImipramine-like Antidepressants, Neuroleptics: Additive antihypertensive effect and increased risk of orthostatic hypotension.\\nCalcium Salts: Risk of hypercalcemia secondary to decreased urinary calcium excretion.\\nCyclosporine, Tacrolimus: Risk of elevated plasma creatinine concentrations without alterations in circulating cyclosporine levels, even in non-dehydrated/non-hyponatromic patients.\\nSystemic Corticosteroids, Tetracosactide: Attenuation of the antihypertensive effect due to sodium and fluid retention."}, {"drug": "Amlodipine", "effect": "Dantrolene (Intravenous): Animal studies have reported lethal ventricular fibrillation and cardiovascular collapse associated with hyperkalemia following the co-administration of verapamil and intravenous dantrolene. Due to the risk of hyperkalemia, concurrent administration of calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine with dantrolene should be avoided in malignant hyperthermia-susceptible patients and during the management of malignant hyperthermia.\\nGrapefruit and Grapefruit Juice: Co-administration is not recommended as systemic bioavailability may be increased in certain patients, resulting in an enhanced blood pressure-lowering effect.\\nCYP3A4 Inhibitors: Concomitant use with strong or moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., protease inhibitors, azole antifungals, macrolides such as erythromycin and clarithromycin, verapamil, or diltiazem) may significantly increase systemic exposure to amlodipine, with heightened clinical manifestations in geriatric patients. Clinical monitoring and dose adjustments may be warranted. Increased risk of hypotension is observed when clarithromycin is co-administered with amlodipine.\\nCYP3A4 Inducers: Pharmacokinetic data regarding CYP3A4 inducers on amlodipine are lacking. Co-administration with strong CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., rifampicin, St. John's Wort [Hypericum perforatum]) may decrease plasma concentrations of amlodipine; therefore, clinical caution is advised.\\nEffects of Amlodipine on Other Medicinal Products: Amlodipine exerts additive antihypertensive effects when combined with other blood pressure-lowering agents. Clinical drug-interaction trials demonstrated that amlodipine does not alter the pharmacokinetics of atorvastatin, digoxin, or warfarin.\\nTacrolimus: Risk of increased blood concentrations of tacrolimus when co-administered with amlodipine. Therapeutic drug monitoring of tacrolimus blood levels and appropriate dosage adjustment are mandatory to prevent tacrolimus toxicity.\\nCyclosporine: Formal drug-interaction studies with cyclosporine and amlodipine have not been performed in healthy volunteers or other populations, excluding renal transplant patients, where variable trough concentrations of cyclosporine (mean increase of 0°40%) have been observed. Cyclosporine blood concentrations should be monitored in renal transplant recipients receiving amlodipine, and dose reductions of cyclosporine should be implemented as clinically necessary.\\nSimvastatin: Co-administration of multiple 10 mg doses of amlodipine with 80 mg simvastatin resulted in a 77% increase in systemic exposure to simvastatin compared to simvastatin monotherapy. The daily dose of simvastatin should be restricted to a maximum of 20 mg in patients receiving amlodipine."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Natrixam exerts minor to moderate influence on the ability to drive and operate machinery. Although indapamide presents a low risk, hypotensive reactions may occur in susceptible patients, particularly at treatment initiation or upon the addition of another antihypertensive agent. Similarly, amlodipine may moderately impair psychomotor performance due to potential adverse reactions such as dizziness, headache, fatigue, or nausea. Special caution is advised at the onset of therapy.", "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to the active substances, other sulfonamide derivatives, dihydropyridine derivatives, or to any of the excipients.", "Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min).", "Severe hepatic impairment or hepatic encephalopathy.", "Refractory hypokalemia.", "Lactation and breastfeeding.", "Severe hypotension.", "Shock, including cardiogenic shock.", "Obstruction of the left ventricular outflow tract (e.g., clinically significant aortic stenosis).", "Hemodynamically unstable heart failure following acute myocardial infarction."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Servier", "origin": "France", "storage": "Store below 30°C. Store in the original packaging to protect from moisture.", "packaging": "Box of 6 blister packs containing 5 prolonged-release tablets each", "manufacturer": "Les Laboratoires Servier Industrie (France)"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "The recommended dosage is one prolonged-release tablet once daily, taken orally. This fixed-dose combination is not indicated for initial therapy; it should be reserved for patients already stabilized on individual components administered concurrently at equivalent doses. Any required dosage titration must be performed using the individual monotherapy components. Contraindicated in severe renal or hepatic impairment. No dose adjustment is required in mild-to-moderate renal impairment. Due to a lack of established guidelines for amlodipine in mild-to-moderate hepatic impairment, dosage selection must be approached with caution, initiating treatment at the lowest end of the dosing range.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as remembered. However, if the time for the next scheduled dose is near, the missed dose should be omitted, and the normal dosing schedule resumed. Do not double the dose to compensate for a forgotten tablet.", "administration": "For oral administration. Tablets should be ingested whole in the morning with an adequate volume of liquid, without chewing or crushing.", "overdoseManagement": "Indapamide Overdose: Initial management involves rapid gastrointestinal decontamination via gastric lavage or administration of activated charcoal, followed by restoration of fluid and electrolyte homeostasis at a specialized medical facility. Amlodipine Overdose: Clinically significant overdosage manifests as pronounced systemic hypotension requiring active cardiovascular support, including frequent monitoring of cardiac and respiratory parameters, elevation of the lower extremities, and careful evaluation of circulating blood volume and urinary output. Administration of a vasopressor may assist in restoring vascular tone and arterial pressure in the absence of contraindications. Intravenous calcium gluconate may reverse the pharmacological blockade of calcium channels. Gastric lavage may be beneficial in selected cases. Administration of activated charcoal up to 2 hours post-ingestion of a 10 mg amlodipine dose has been shown to reduce drug absorption in healthy volunteers. Hemodialysis is ineffective due to the high plasma protein binding of amlodipine."}, "generalDescription": "Natrixam 1.5mg/5mg is a combination antihypertensive agent comprising indapamide (a thiazide-like diuretic) and amlodipine (a calcium channel blocker). Each prolonged-release tablet contains 1.5mg of indapamide and 5mg of amlodipine. This synergistic combination effectively reduces systemic blood pressure through vasodilation and natriuresis, and must be administered strictly in accordance with medical prescription.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Natrixam 1.5mg/5mg and 1.5mg/10mg Tablet Core: Lactose monohydrate, hypromellose (E464), magnesium stearate (E572), povidone (E1201), colloidal anhydrous silica, calcium hydrogen phosphate dihydrate, microcrystalline cellulose (E460), croscarmellose sodium (E468), pregelatinized maize starch. Film-coating for Natrixam 1.5mg/5mg tablets: Glycerol (E422), hypromellose (E464), macrogol 6000, magnesium stearate (E572), titanium dioxide (E171). Film-coating for Natrixam 1.5mg/10mg tablets: Glycerol (E422), hypromellose (E464), red iron oxide (E172), macrogol 6000, magnesium stearate (E572), titanium dioxide (E171).", "activeIngredients": "Each Natrixam 1.5mg/5mg prolonged-release tablet contains 1.5mg of indapamide and 6.935mg of amlodipine besilate, equivalent to 5mg of amlodipine base."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Geriatric patients should be treated with Natrixam with dosage adjustment guided strictly by individual renal function.", "lactation": "Natrixam is contraindicated throughout the duration of breastfeeding.", "pediatric": "The safety and efficacy of Natrixam in pediatric patients and adolescents below 18 years of age have not been established. No clinical data are currently available.", "pregnancy": "Natrixam is not recommended during pregnancy due to the lack of clinical safety data regarding the combination."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Hepatic Encephalopathy: Thiazide-related diuretics may precipitate hepatic encephalopathy in patients with compromised hepatic function, particularly in the presence of electrolyte imbalances. Treatment with Natrixam must be discontinued immediately if hepatic encephalopathy develops.", "Photosensitivity: Photosensitivity reactions have been documented with thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics. If a photosensitivity reaction occurs during therapy, discontinuation of treatment is recommended. If re-administration is deemed clinically essential, sun-exposed areas must be adequately protected against solar radiation or artificial UVA exposure.", "Hypertensive Crisis: The safety and therapeutic efficacy of amlodipine in hypertensive crises have not been established.", "Fluid and Electrolyte Balance:\\n- Serum Sodium: Baseline serum sodium levels must be assessed prior to treatment initiation and monitored regularly thereafter. As hyponatremia may initially be asymptomatic, regular surveillance is mandatory, with increased frequency in elderly patients and those with cirrhosis. Diuretic-induced hyponatremia can occasionally lead to severe complications, volume depletion, dehydration, and orthostatic hypotension. Concomitant chloride depletion may result in secondary metabolic alkalosis.\\n- Serum Potassium: Hypokalemia represents the principal hazard associated with thiazide and thiazide-related diuretics. The prevention of initial serum potassium depletion (< 3.4 mmol/L) is critical in high-risk cohorts, including geriatric patients, malnourished individuals, polypharmacy patients, cirrhotic patients with ascites and edema, patients with coronary artery disease, and patients with heart failure. Hypokalemia potentiates the cardiotoxic effects of cardiac glycosides and increases the incidence of cardiac arrhythmias, particularly Torsades de Pointes (which can be fatal), especially in the presence of congenital or acquired QT prolongation or bradycardia. Regular monitoring of plasma potassium is required, with the initial evaluation performed during the first week of therapy.\\n- Serum Calcium: Thiazide and thiazide-related diuretics decrease urinary calcium excretion, leading to mild and transient hypercalcemia. Marked hypercalcemia may unmask latent hyperparathyroidism, necessitating discontinuation of the diuretic prior to parathyroid function evaluation.\\n- Blood Glucose: Glycemic control is vital in diabetic patients due to the indapamide component, particularly when concurrent hypokalemia is present.\\n- Heart Failure: Patients with heart failure must be managed with caution. In a long-term placebo-controlled trial involving patients with severe heart failure (NYHA Class III and IV), pulmonary edema was reported with a higher incidence in the amlodipine-treated group compared to placebo. Calcium channel blockers should be administered cautiously in congestive heart failure due to potential risks of future cardiovascular events and mortality.\\n- Renal Function: Thiazide and thiazide-related diuretics achieve optimal efficacy only when renal function is normal or mildly impaired (plasma creatinine levels < 25 mg/L, corresponding to ~220°mol/L in adults). Initial volume depletion induced by diuresis may cause transient reductions in glomerular filtration rate, resulting in elevated blood urea and serum creatinine. While clinically insignificant in patients with normal baseline renal function, this may exacerbate pre-existing renal insufficiency. Amlodipine may be utilized at standard doses in renal impairment, as plasma concentrations are independent of the degree of renal dysfunction; however, amlodipine is non-dialyzable. The combination product must adhere to the individual component dosing guidelines in renal impairment.\\n- Uric Acid: Hyperuricemia and an increased tendency toward acute gout attacks may occur due to the indapamide component.\\n- Hepatic Function: The elimination half-life of amlodipine is prolonged and systemic exposure is increased in patients with hepatic impairment. Initial dosing must be set at the lowest therapeutic range, and extreme caution must be exercised during dose escalation. Natrixam is contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment and applied with caution in mild-to-moderate impairment.\\n- Geriatric Patients: Dosage administration must be adjusted according to renal function status.\\n- Galactose Intolerance: Patients with rare hereditary disorders of galactose intolerance, total lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medicinal product."], "adverseReactionManagement": "In the event of adverse drug reactions, treatment should be suspended immediately, and the prescribing physician or nearest medical center should be consulted for prompt clinical management and supportive care."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:27:58.629	natrixam-1-5mg-5mg-prolonged-release-tablets-indapamide-amlodipine-for-essential-hypertension-6	17
1244	Alzental 400mg Tablets (Albendazole) for Intestinal Helminthiasis (1 Blister Pack x 1 Tablet)	P13233	\N	\N	Shinpoong Daewoo Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.	South Korea	5263.00	4000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20241107084414-0-P13233.png?versionId=EqHXZjHkm6L2J4ZLzxLniNeawQ9M3eZ_	Alzental 400mg tablets, containing albendazole, are indicated for the treatment of various intestinal helminthic infections, including roundworm, pinworm, whipworm, and hookworm.	{"indications": ["Treatment of various intestinal helminthic infections such as roundworm (Ascaris lumbricoides), pinworm (Enterobius vermicularis), whipworm (Trichuris trichiura), and hookworm (Ancylostoma duodenale, Necator americanus)."], "sideEffects": ["With short-term treatment (< 3 days), some cases of gastrointestinal discomfort (epigastric pain, diarrhea) and headache may occur.", "Common adverse effects (ADRs > 1/100): Fever, headache, dizziness, cerebral manifestations, increased intracranial pressure, abnormal liver function, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, alopecia (reversible).", "Uncommon adverse effects (1/1000 < ADR < 1/100): Allergic reactions, leukopenia, rash, urticaria, acute renal failure.", "Rare adverse effects (ADR < 1/1000): Agranulocytopenia, pancytopenia, agranulocytosis, thrombocytopenia."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Dexamethasone", "effect": "Steady-state trough plasma concentrations of albendazole sulfoxide are increased by approximately 50%."}, {"drug": "Praziquantel", "effect": "Increases mean plasma concentration and area under the curve of albendazole sulfoxide by approximately 50%."}, {"drug": "Cimetidine", "effect": "Biliary and cystic fluid concentrations of albendazole sulfoxide are increased by approximately 2-fold."}, {"drug": "Theophylline", "effect": "Theophylline pharmacokinetics are unchanged."}], "effectsOnDriving": "If dizziness or headache occurs, patients should not drive or operate machinery.", "contraindications": ["History of hyper