 of corticosteroid-induced adrenal suppression."}, {"drug": "Amphotericin B, potassium-depleting diuretics", "effect": "Increased risk of hypokalemia, cardiac enlargement, and congestive heart failure."}, {"drug": "Macrolide antibiotics", "effect": "Significant decrease in corticosteroid clearance."}, {"drug": "Anticoagulants (Warfarin)", "effect": "Inhibition of response to warfarin; coagulation parameters should be closely monitored."}, {"drug": "Antidiabetic agents", "effect": "Dosage adjustment of antidiabetic drugs is required as corticosteroids may increase blood glucose levels."}, {"drug": "Cyclosporine", "effect": "Increased activity of both agents, with a potential risk of convulsions."}, {"drug": "Ketoconazole", "effect": "Decreased metabolism of corticosteroids, increasing the risk of adverse effects."}], "effectsOnDriving": "If patients experience adverse effects such as syncope, dizziness, or convulsions, they should refrain from driving and operating machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Hydrocortisone is an adrenocortical steroid that exerts anti-inflammatory, anti-allergic, antipruritic, and immunosuppressive effects by binding to glucocorticoid receptors, modulating gene transcription, and inhibiting inflammatory mediators.", "pharmacologicalClass": "Glucocorticoid"}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "When administered intravenously, water-soluble hydrocortisone sodium succinate is rapidly distributed and achieves high concentrations in body fluids.", "metabolism": "Hydrocortisone is metabolized primarily in the liver and most body tissues to reduced and degraded forms such as tetrahydrocortisone and tetrahydrocortisol.", "elimination": "The elimination half-life is approximately 100 minutes. Metabolites are excreted via the kidneys, primarily as glucuronide conjugates with a small amount excreted unchanged."}, "contraindications": ["Patients with systemic fungal infections unless specific anti-infective therapy is employed.", "A