r's syndrome, berylliosis, fulminating or disseminated pulmonary tuberculosis when used concurrently with appropriate antituberculous chemotherapy, and aspiration pneumonitis.", "Hematological disorders: Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura in adults, secondary thrombocytopenia in adults, acquired (autoimmune) hemolytic anemia, erythroblastopenia, and congenital hypoplastic anemia.", "Neoplastic diseases: Palliative management of leukemias and lymphomas in adults, and acute leukemia in pediatric patients.", "Edematous states: To induce diuresis or remission of proteinuria in idiopathic nephrotic syndrome or due to lupus erythematosus.", "Gastrointestinal diseases: To tide the patient over a critical period of the disease in ulcerative colitis and regional enteritis.", "Nervous system: Acute exacerbations of multiple sclerosis and cerebral edema associated with primary or metastatic brain tumor.", "Organ transplantation.", "Other indications: Tuberculous meningitis with subarachnoid block or impending block when used concurrently with appropriate antituberculous chemotherapy, and trichinosis with neurologic or myocardial involvement."], "sideEffects": ["Infections and infestations: Increased susceptibility to infections, masking of infections, opportunistic infections.", "Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity reactions, suppressed skin test reactivity.", "Endocrine disorders: Cushingoid state, suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, growth suppression in pediatric patients, secondary adrenocortical and pituitary unresponsiveness.", "Metabolism and nutrition disorders: Fluid retention, sodium retention, potassium loss, hypokalemic alkalosis, negative nitrogen balance due to protein catabolism, increased appetite, impaired glucose tolerance, increased requirement for insulin or oral hypoglycemic agents.", "Psychiatric disorders: Affective disorders, depression, euphoria, mood swings, psychotic reactions, personality changes, anxiety, insomnia.", "Nervous system disorders: Convulsions, increased intracranial pressure with papilledema (pseudotumor cerebri usually after treatment), vertigo, headache.", "Eye disorders: Posterior subcapsular cataracts, increased intraocular pressure, glaucoma, exophthalmos.", "Cardiac disorders: Congestive heart failure in susceptible patients, hypertension.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Peptic ulcer with potential perforation and hemorrhage, gastric hemorrhage, perforation of the small and large bowel, ulcerative esophagitis, pancreatitis, abdominal distension, nausea, vomiting.", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Muscle weakness, steroid myopathy, osteoporosis, tendon rupture, vertebral compression fractures, aseptic necrosis of femoral and humeral heads.", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Angioedema, petechiae, ecchymoses, skin atrophy, striae, hirsutism, delayed wound healing, acne.", "General disorders and administration site conditions: Impaired healing, fatigue, malaise."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Isoniazid", "effect": "Methylprednisolone may increase the hepatic metabolism and clearance of isoniazid."}, {"drug": "Rifampicin, Carbamazepine, Phenobarbital, Phenytoin", "effect": "CYP3A4 inducers that may increase the hepatic clearance of methylprednisolone, resulting in decreased plasma concentrations and therapeutic efficacy."}, {"drug": "Coumarin Anticoagulants", "effect": "Effects of oral anticoagulants may be potentiated or diminished; close monitoring of coagulation indices (INR/PT) is required."}, {"drug": "Anticholinergic Agents", "effect": "May increase the risk of severe myopathy or acute myasthenia."}, {"drug": "Antidiabetic Agents", "effect": "Corticosteroids may increase blood glucose concentrations; dosage adjustments of antidiabetic medications may be necessary."}, {"drug": "Itraconazole, Ketoconazole, Diltiazem, Cyclosporine, Clarithromycin, Erythromycin", "effect": "CYP3A4 inhibitors that may decrease the clearance of methylprednisolone, increasing the risk of systemic corticosteroid toxicity."}, {"drug": "Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)", "effect": "Concurrent use increases the risk of gastrointestinal ulceration and hemorrhage."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Corticosteroids may cause adverse effects such as dizziness, vertigo, visual disturbances, and fatigue. Patients experiencing these side effects should not drive or operate machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Methylprednisolone is a potent anti-inflammatory steroid with greater anti-inflammatory potency than prednisolone and a lower tendency to induce sodium and water retention. Its relative potency is at least four times that of hydrocortisone.", "pharmacologicalClass": "Systemic Corticosteroid / Anti-inflammatory Agent"}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, with peak plasma concentrations achieved approximately 1.5 to 2.3 hours post-dose. The absolute bioavailability is approximately 82% to 89%.", "metabolism": "Metabolized primarily in the liver by CYP3A4 into inactive metabolites.", "elimination": "The average elimination half-life ranges from 1.8 to 5.2 hours. Total body clearance is approximately 5 to 6 mL/min/kg."}, "contraindications": ["Systemic fungal infections.", "Known hypersensitivity to methylprednisolone or any component of the formulation.", "Administration of live or live-attenuated vaccines is contraindicated in patients receiving immunosuppressive doses of corticosteroids."], "manufacturingInfo": {"storage": "Store in a dry place, protected from light, at temperatures below 30°C. Keep out of reach of children."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dosage must be individualized based on the disease entity and patient response. High-dose therapy may be required for specific conditions, such as multiple sclerosis (200 mg/day), cerebral edema (200 to 1000 mg/day), or organ transplantation (up to 7 mg/kg/day). Tapering of dosage is necessary upon discontinuation after prolonged therapy.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. If it is almost time for the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and resume the regular dosing schedule. Do not double doses.", "administration": "For oral administration. Tablets should be swallowed whole with an adequate amount of water, preferably with food to minimize gastrointestinal irritation.", "overdoseManagement": "There is no specific antidote for acute methylprednisolone overdose; treatment is supportive and symptomatic. Methylprednisolone is dialyzable."}, "generalDescription": "Medrol 4mg is an oral medication containing methylprednisolone, a synthetic glucocorticoid with potent anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive properties used to treat a wide variety of inflammatory and autoimmune conditions.", "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Methylprednisolone 4mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Corticosteroids are excreted in breast milk and may suppress growth or cause other unwanted effects. Use only if the anticipated benefits outweigh the potential risks to the infant.", "pediatric": "Carefully monitor growth and development during prolonged treatment due to the risk of growth retardation and increased intracranial pressure.", "pregnancy": "Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. Infants born to mothers receiving substantial doses of corticosteroids during pregnancy must be carefully observed for signs of hypoadrenalism."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Increased susceptibility to bacterial, viral, fungal, or parasitic infections, which may mask signs of infection.", "Use with extreme caution in patients with latent tuberculosis or tuberculin reactivity.", "Risk of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression following prolonged high-dose therapy.", "Exercise caution in patients with seizure disorders, myasthenia gravis, hypertension, renal impairment, or active peptic ulcer disease.", "Monitor growth and development in pediatric patients receiving prolonged therapy.", "Risk of severe psychiatric disturbances, ranging from euphoria to acute psychosis."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Discontinue use and consult a physician or seek immediate medical attention if severe adverse reactions or signs of infection occur."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:47:48.08	medrol-4mg-oral-tablets-methylprednisolone-4mg-3-blisters-x-10-tablets-anti-inflammatory-and	\N
