ic shock or other severe IgE-mediated hypersensitivity reactions."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Vidipha", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, protected from light.", "packaging": "Box of 10 blisters x 10 capsules.", "manufacturer": "Vidipha"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults: 250 mg to 500 mg every 6 hours, depending on the severity of the infection; the dosage may be increased up to 4 g daily. Treatment duration should be at least 7 to 10 days. For complicated, recurrent, or chronic urinary tract infections: 1 g twice daily for 2 weeks. Gonorrhea: a single dose of 3 g with 1 g of probenecid for males, or 2 g with 0.5 g of probenecid for females.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. If it is almost time for the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose. Do not double the dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "For oral administration.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea; high doses may cause neuromuscular hyperexcitability and seizures (particularly in patients with renal impairment). Management: Maintain a patent airway, provide ventilation and fluid support, administer multiple doses of activated charcoal, or perform gastric lavage. Hemodialysis may assist in drug elimination but is generally unnecessary."}, "generalDescription": "Cephalexin 500mg Vidipha capsules are an antibiotic medication indicated for the treatment of bacterial infections caused by susceptible organisms, including both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Lactose, magnesium stearate", "activeIngredients": "Cephalexin 500mg (as cephalexin monohydrate)"}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Use with caution in patients allergic to penicillin due to the potential for cross-hypersensitivity.", "Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.", "Dosage reduction is required in patients with renal impairment.", "Evaluate for pseudomembranous colitis in patients who develop severe diarrhea during or following antibiotic therapy.", "Use with caution during the first trimester of pregnancy.", "Cephalexin may cause a false-positive direct Coombs test."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse effects occur, discontinue use immediately and notify a physician or seek medical attention at the nearest healthcare facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:49:34.921	cephalexin-500mg-vidipha-capsules-for-bacterial-infections-10-blisters-x-10-capsules	1
1720	Panadol Cold & Flu 500mg/25mg/5mg Tablets for Cold and Flu Symptom Relief (Box of 15 Blisters x 12 Tablets)	P03029	\N	\N	Sanofi	Vietnam	16941.00	14400.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250415090511-0-P03029.jpg?versionId=Cs52MNQdH.ZbBRxNDZOQ_IaJ...GMB2b	Panadol Cold & Flu Tablets provide rapid relief from cold and flu symptoms, including headache, sore throat, nasal congestion, and body aches, while helping to reduce fever.	{"sideEffects": ["Paracetamol: Adverse drug reactions identified from clinical trial data are uncommon and affect only a small number of patients. Therefore, adverse reactions reported during post-marketing surveillance at recommended doses are presented in the table below by MedDRA system organ class. Because these reactions are reported voluntarily from an undetermined population size, their frequency is classified as very rare (<1/10,000).", "Blood and lymphatic system disorders: Thrombocytopenia.", "Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity reactions. Cutaneous allergic reactions including skin rash, angioedema, and Stevens-Johnson syndrome.", "Respiratory, thoracic, and mediastinal disorders: Bronchospasm in patients sensitive to aspirin and other NSAIDs.", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Liver function abnormalities.", "Caffeine: Adverse reactions associated with the use of caffeine reported during post-marketing experience are listed below. The frequency of these adverse reactions cannot be estimated from the available data.", "Nervous system disorders: Restlessness, dizziness. When recommended doses of paracetamol-caffeine are taken in conjunction with a high-dietary-caffeine intake, caffeine-related adverse effects such as insomnia, restlessness, anxiety, irritability, headaches, gastrointestinal disturbances, and palpitations may occur.", "Phenylephrine: The following adverse reactions have been observed in clinical trials with phenylephrine and may represent the most common adverse events. These reactions are categorized by MedDRA system organ class.", "Psychiatric disorders: Restlessness.", "Nervous system disorders: Headache, dizziness, insomnia.", "Cardiac disorders: Hypertension.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Nausea, vomiting.", "Post-marketing adverse reactions: The frequency of these adverse reactions is not clearly established but appears to be rare (<1/1,000).", "Eye disorders: Mydriasis, acute angle-closure glaucoma, most commonly occurring in patients with a history of angle-closure glaucoma.", "Cardiac disorders: Tachycardia, palpitations.", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Allergic reactions (such as rash, urticaria, allergic dermatitis).", "Renal and urinary disorders: Dysuria, urinary retention. Most commonly occurring in patients with urinary tract obstruction such as prostatic hyperplasia.", "Patients should be advised to report any adverse reactions experienced while using this medication to a healthcare professional."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs)", "effect": "Hypertensive interactions occur between sympathomimetic amines, such as phenylephrine, and monoamine oxidase inhibitors."}, {"drug": "Other Sympathomimetic Amines", "effect": "Concomitant use of phenylephrine with other sympathomimetic amines may increase the risk of cardiovascular adverse effects."}, {"drug": "Beta-blockers and Other Antihypertensives (including debrisoquine, guanethidine, reserpine, and methyldopa)", "effect": "Phenylephrine may reduce the efficacy of beta-blockers and other antihypertensive medications. The risk of hypertension and other cardiovascular adverse effects may be increased."}, {"drug": "Tricyclic Antidepressants", "effect": "May increase the risk of cardiovascular adverse effects associated with phenylephrine."}, {"drug": "Digoxin and Cardiac Glycosides", "effect": "Concomitant administration of phenylephrine with digoxin or cardiac glycosides may increase the risk of irregular heartbeat or stroke."}, {"drug": "Warfarin and Other Coumarins", "effect": "Prolonged daily use of paracetamol enhances the anticoagulant effect of warfarin and other coumarins, increasing the risk of bleeding; occasional use has no significant effect."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Patients experiencing dizziness while taking this medication should not drive or operate machinery.", "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "GlaxoSmithKline.", "storage": "Store in a dry place at temperatures below 30°C.", "packaging": "Box of 15 blisters x 12 tablets.", "manufacturer": "Sanofi-Synthelabo Vietnam Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults (including the elderly) and children aged 12 years and over: Take 1 to 2 tablets (500 mg paracetamol / 25 mg caffeine / 5 mg phenylephrine hydrochloride up to 1000 mg paracetamol / 50 mg caffeine / 10 mg phenylephrine hydrochloride) every 4 to 6 hours as needed, up to a maximum of 4 doses in any 24-hour period. Maximum daily dose: 8 tablets (4000 mg paracetamol / 200 mg caffeine / 40 mg phenylephrine hydrochloride) within 24 hours. Minimum dosing interval: 4 hours. Maximum duration of continuous use for fever without medical supervision: 3 days. Children under 12 years: Not recommended for children under 12 years of age. Note: The dosages listed above are for reference only. Specific dosages depend on individual physical condition and disease severity. Consult a doctor or medical professional for appropriate dosing.", "missedDose": "If you miss a dose of Panadol, take it as soon as possible. However, if it is almost time for your next dose, skip the missed dose and resume your regular dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "For oral administration.,Do not exceed the stated dose.,Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration necessary for treatment.", "overdoseManagement": "Paracetamol: Signs and Symptoms: Paracetamol overdose can cause hepatic toxicity and liver failure. Symptoms of paracetamol overdose within the first 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain. Liver damage may become apparent 12 to 48 hours after ingestion. Abnormalities of glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur. In severe poisoning, hepatic failure may progress to encephalopathy, hemorrhage, hypoglycemia, cerebral edema, and death. Acute renal failure with acute tubular necrosis, manifested by flank pain, hematuria, and proteinuria, may develop even in the absence of severe liver injury. Cardiac arrhythmias and pancreatitis have been reported. Management: Immediate medical management is essential in the event of paracetamol overdose. Even in the absence of significant early symptoms, patients should be transferred to a hospital urgently for immediate medical care. Symptoms may be limited to nausea or vomiting and may not reflect the severity of the overdose or the risk of organ damage. Management must be in accordance with established treatment guidelines. Treatment with activated charcoal should be considered if the overdose is detected within 1 hour. Plasma paracetamol concentrations should be measured 4 hours or more after ingestion (earlier concentrations are unreliable). Treatment with N-acetylcysteine may be used up to 24 hours after paracetamol ingestion; however, the maximum protective effect is obtained up to 8 hours post-ingestion. The effectiveness of the antidote drops sharply after this period. If necessary, patients may be administered intravenous N-acetylcysteine in accordance with established dosage regimens. If emesis is not contraindicated, oral methionine may be a suitable alternative for remote areas far from a hospital. Management of patients with severe hepatic dysfunction presenting >24 hours following ingestion should be discussed with the National Poisons Information Centre or a specialized hepatology unit. Caffeine: Signs and Symptoms: Caffeine overdose may cause epigastric pain, vomiting, diuresis, tachycardia, central nervous system stimulation (insomnia, restlessness, agitation, anxiety, confusion, tremors, convulsions). In cases of overdose, it should be noted that in addition to symptoms caused by caffeine, severe hepatic toxicity due to paracetamol overdose may also occur. Management: There is no specific antidote, but supportive management measures may be used. Phenylephrine: Signs and Symptoms: Phenylephrine overdose is likely to result in effects similar to those listed under adverse reactions. Other symptoms may include irritability, restlessness, hypertension, and reflex bradycardia. In severe cases, confusion, hallucinations, seizures, and arrhythmias may occur. However, the amount of medication required to cause serious toxicity related to phenylephrine is typically much greater than the amount required to cause serious hepatic toxicity related to paracetamol. Management: Clinical management should be appropriate to the severity of the clinical symptoms. Severe hypertension should be treated with alpha-adrenergic blocking agents such as phentolamine."}, "generalDescription": "Panadol Cold & Flu tablets are a medication used for pain relief and fever reduction, specifically indicated for the management of cold and flu symptoms. It is a widely used product for relieving discomfort such as headaches, sore throats, nasal congestion, and body aches during illness.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Maize starch, pre-gelatinised starch, povidone, Sunset Yellow (E110), sodium lauryl sulfate, potassium sorbate, stearic acid, talc, microcrystalline cellulose, hydroxypropyl methylcellulose, polyethylene glycol, titanium dioxide, quinoline yellow lake (E104), Sunset Yellow aluminum lake (E110).", "activeIngredients": "Paracetamol 500mg, Caffeine 25mg, Phenylephrine hydrochloride 5mg."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "This medication should not be used during breastfeeding unless specifically directed by a physician. Paracetamol: Human studies with paracetamol at recommended doses have not identified any risk to breastfeeding women or breastfed infants. Caffeine: Caffeine in breast milk may have a stimulating effect on breastfed infants, although significant toxicity has not been observed. Phenylephrine: Phenylephrine may be excreted into breast milk.", "pediatric": "Not recommended for children under 12 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Fertility: No data available. Pregnancy: This medication is not recommended during pregnancy. Paracetamol: Human and animal studies have not identified any risk of paracetamol to pregnancy or embryonic development. Caffeine: This medication is not recommended during pregnancy due to the potential increased risk of spontaneous abortion associated with caffeine accumulation in the body. Phenylephrine: No data available."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Physicians should warn patients about the signs of serious skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS), toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) or Lyell's syndrome, and acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP).", "Contains paracetamol. Do not use with other medications containing paracetamol, decongestants, or other cold and flu relief medications. Concurrent use of other products containing paracetamol may lead to overdose. Paracetamol overdose may cause liver failure, which may require a liver transplant or result in death.", "Cases of hepatic impairment/failure have been reported in patients with glutathione depletion states, such as malnutrition, severe anorexia, low body mass index, or chronic alcoholism.", "Consult a doctor before using this medication if the patient has any of the following conditions:", "Hypertension.", "Cardiovascular disease.", "Diabetes mellitus.", "Hyperthyroidism.", "Angle-closure glaucoma.", "Phaeochromocytoma.", "Prostatic hyperplasia.", "Occlusive vascular disease (such as Raynaud's syndrome).", "Hepatic or renal impairment. There is an increased risk of paracetamol-induced hepatotoxicity in patients with underlying liver disease.", "Patients in states of glutathione depletion, such as sepsis; the use of paracetamol may increase the risk of metabolic acidosis.", "Exercise caution when administering this medication to patients taking beta-blockers or other antihypertensive medications.", "Exercise caution when administering this medication to patients taking tricyclic antidepressants.", "This medication should not be used by patients taking sympathomimetic agents (such as decongestants, appetite suppressants, and amphetamine-like psychostimulants).", "Avoid excessive caffeine intake (e.g., from coffee, tea, and certain canned beverages) while taking this medication.", "If symptoms persist, consult a doctor.", "Keep out of reach of children.", "This product contains Sunset Yellow (E110), which may cause allergic reactions."]}	f	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:50:09.493	panadol-cold-and-flu-500mg-25mg-5mg-tablets-for-cold-and-flu-symptom-relief-box-of-15-blisters-x-12	85
