vere hepatic impairment or severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min for formulations containing 875 mg amoxicillin).", "Concurrent history of infectious mononucleosis."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "DHG Pharmaceutical.", "storage": "Store in a dry place below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.", "packaging": "Box of 2 blisters x 7 tablets.", "manufacturer": "DHG Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and adolescents aged 12 years and older: One tablet twice daily. The usual duration of therapy is 5 to 10 days. Treatment should not be extended beyond 14 days without clinical re-evaluation.", "administration": "Administer at the start of a meal to minimize gastrointestinal intolerance and optimize absorption. Tablets must be swallowed whole without chewing; if necessary, scored tablets may be divided in half and swallowed immediately without chewing.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms: Gastrointestinal effects (abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea), fluid and electrolyte imbalance, crystalluria (potentially leading to renal failure), and neurotoxicity (convulsions in patients with renal impairment or following high doses). Management: Discontinue treatment immediately, institute symptomatic and supportive care, maintain hydration and electrolyte balance, and consider hemodialysis for drug removal if clinically indicated."}, "generalDescription": "Klamentin 1g is a combination penicillin-class antibiotic and beta-lactamase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of various bacterial infections. Amoxicillin exerts bactericidal activity by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis, while Clavulanic Acid inactivates beta-lactamase enzymes, thereby protecting Amoxicillin from degradation and expanding its antibacterial spectrum.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Colloidal anhydrous silica (Aerosil), crospovidone (polyplasdon XL), microcrystalline cellulose (avicel M112), magnesium stearate, hypromellose (sepifilm LP914), titanium dioxide, talc.", "activeIngredients": "Amoxicillin (equivalent to amoxicillin trihydrate) 875mg, Clavulanic Acid (equivalent to potassium clavulanate) 125mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Trace quantities of amoxicillin and clavulanate are excreted in breast milk. Exercise caution and monitor breastfed infants for diarrhea or mucosal candidiasis.", "pregnancy": "Reproduction studies in animals have shown no evidence of fetal harm; however, safety in human pregnancy has not been definitively established. Use only if clearly needed and prescribed by a physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Dosage adjustment is strongly recommended in patients with moderate to severe renal impairment.", "Prolonged use may occasionally result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (superinfection)."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:20:03.812	klamentin-875mg-125mg-film-coated-tablets-for-bacterial-infections-2-blisters-x-7-tablets	62
125	Avamys Fluticasone Furoate 27.5mcg/Actuation Metered Nasal Spray for Allergic Rhinitis (1 Bottle x 60 Doses)	P01733	\N	\N	Glaxo Operations Uk Limited	Vietnam	213793.00	186000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250520035311-0-P01733.png?versionId=WY28LGB4QxbJX4rw5y9qSIvwIwtlo8am	Avamys 27.5mcg Nasal Spray, containing fluticasone furoate as the active pharmaceutical ingredient, is indicated for the treatment of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis, including nasal congestion, rhinorrhea, and sneezing. Supplied in a metered-dose glass bottle containing 60 doses.	{"manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) (United Kingdom)", "manufacturer": "Glaxo Operations UK Limited"}, "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Fluticasone Furoate 27.5mcg/actuation"}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["<h2 class=\\"css-8uyn92\\">Adverse Reactions</h2>", "<p>During clinical use of Avamys, the following adverse drug reactions (ADRs) may occur:</p>", "<p><strong><em>Common, ADR &gt;1/100&nbsp;</em></strong></p>", "<p>Respiratory, thoracic and mediastinal disorders: Epistaxis, nasal ulceration.</p>", "<p>Nervous system disorders: Headache.</p>", "<p><strong><em>Uncommon, 1/1000 &lt; ADR &lt; 1/100</em></strong></p>", "<p>Respiratory, thoracic and mediastinal disorders: Nasal pain, nasal discomfort (including nasal burning sensation, nasal irritation, and nasal soreness), nasal dryness.</p>", "<p><strong><em>Rare, 1/10,000 &lt; ADR &lt; 1/1000</em></strong></p>", "<p>Respiratory, thoracic and mediastinal disorders: Nasal septal perforation.</p>", "<p>Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity reactions including anaphylaxis, angioedema, rash, and urticaria.</p>", "<p><strong><em>Frequency Not Known</em></strong></p>", "<p>Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Growth retardation in pediatric patients.</p>", "<p><strong><em>Instructions for Managing ADRs</em></strong></p>", "<p>Patients should promptly report any adverse events or side effects to their prescribing physician or clinical pharmacist.</p><h2 class=\\"css-8uyn92\\">Warnings and Precautions</h2>", "<p>Prior to initiating therapy with this medicinal product, carefully read the complete prescribing information and adhere to the guidelines outlined below.</p><h3>Contraindications&nbsp;</h3>", "<p>Avamys nasal spray is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to fluticasone furoate or any of the inactive excipients contained in the formulation.</p>", "<h3>Precautions for Use</h3>", "<p>Systemic effects of intranasal corticosteroids may occur, particularly when prescribed at high doses for extended periods. These systemic manifestations are significantly less frequent than those observed with oral corticosteroid therapies and may exhibit inter-individual variability among patients and specific drug formulations.</p>", "<p>Reductions in growth velocity have been documented in pediatric patients receiving fluticasone furoate at 110 micrograms daily for a duration of one year. Consequently, pediatric patients should be maintained on the lowest effective dose required for adequate symptom control. Prescribers should remain vigilant regarding potential systemic corticosteroid effects, including ocular manifestations, as is standard practice for intranasal steroid therapies.</p>", "<p>Nasal adverse events: Clinical trials frequently reported epistaxis and nasal ulceration. Post-marketing surveillance has also identified rare reports of nasal septal perforation associated with fluticasone furoate nasal spray usage.</p>", "<p>Intranasal and inhaled corticosteroids carry a potential risk for the development of glaucoma and/or posterior subcapsular cataracts. Therefore, regular monitoring is strongly advised for patients presenting with visual disturbances or a documented medical history of elevated intraocular pressure (IOP), glaucoma, and/or cataracts.&nbsp;</p>", "<p>Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, and urticaria, have been reported following administration.</p>", "<p>When administered at doses exceeding recommendations, or in particularly sensitive individuals at standard recommended dosages, systemic corticosteroid effects such as Cushing's syndrome may manifest. If such systemic changes occur, discontinuation of fluticasone furoate nasal spray should be executed gradually, mirroring the tapering protocols utilized for oral corticosteroid regimens.</p>", "<p>Transitioning patients from systemic corticosteroids to topical intranasal alternatives may provoke clinical signs of transient adrenal insufficiency. Furthermore, certain individuals may experience corticosteroid withdrawal symptoms, such as arthralgia, myalgia, fatigue, and depressive states. Patients previously maintained on long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy who are transferred to topical regimens require rigorous clinical monitoring for acute adrenal crisis during periods of physiological stress.</p>", "<p>In patients with bronchial asthma or other clinical conditions necessitating long-term systemic corticosteroid support, an overly rapid taper of systemic therapy can precipitate severe exacerbations of underlying symptoms.</p>", "<h3>Effects on Ability to Drive and Use Machines&nbsp;</h3>", "<p>Clinical pharmacology data indicate that Avamys nasal spray has no or negligible influence on the patient's capacity to drive or operate heavy machinery.</p>", "<h3>Pregnancy and Lactation</h3>", "<p>Clinical data regarding the safety of AVAMYS nasal spray in pregnant women are currently limited. The administration of AVAMYS nasal spray during pregnancy should be restricted to clinical scenarios where the anticipated therapeutic benefits to the mother clearly outweigh potential risks to the developing fetus.</p>", "<h3>Lactation</h3>", "<p>It remains unestablished whether intranasal fluticasone furoate or its metabolites are excreted into human breast milk. Administration of fluticasone furoate to nursing mothers should only be considered if the expected clinical benefit to the mother outweighs potential risks to the breastfed infant.</p>", "<h3>Drug Interactions</h3>", "<p>Fluticasone furoate undergoes rapid systemic clearance predominantly via extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism mediated by the Cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) isoenzyme system.</p>", "<p>Based on pharmacokinetic extrapolation from other glucocorticoids metabolized via CYP3A4 (such as fluticasone propionate), concurrent administration with ritonavir is strongly discouraged due to the elevated risk of significantly increased systemic exposure and subsequent corticosteroid-related adverse effects.</p>"]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:20:26.79	avamys-fluticasone-furoate-27-5mcg-actuation-metered-nasal-spray-for-allergic-rhinitis-1-bottle-x	385
