165	Haloperidol 2mg Danapha Tablets for Psychotic Disorders and Behavioral Disturbances (Bottle of 200 Tablets)	P10460	\N	\N	Danapha	Vietnam	49351.00	38000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250814075005-0-P10460.jpg?versionId=4BqyxSg9ODwROO3ujc9ZsMmQNmXi2Cdw	Haloperidol 2mg Danapha Tablets are an antipsychotic medication indicated for the chronic treatment of psychotic disorders, bipolar mania, Tourette's syndrome, and short-term management of acute conditions such as alcohol withdrawal symptoms and severe nausea/vomiting.	{"indications": ["Chronic treatment: Control symptoms of psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, psychosis due to brain injury, neurosis, senile confusion, manic states of bipolar disorder, and Tourette's syndrome.", "Short-term treatment:", "Acute alcoholism, reducing hallucinations and confusion, and controlling alcohol-induced tremor and aggressive behavior.", "Treatment of nausea and vomiting associated with radiation therapy or malignancy unresponsive to other therapies.", "Sedative pain relief adjunct."], "sideEffects": ["Blood and lymphatic system disorders: Uncommon: Leukopenia. Not known: Agranulocytosis, neutropenia, aplastic anemia, thrombocytopenia.", "Immune system disorders: Uncommon: Hypersensitivity. Not known: Anaphylactic reaction.", "Endocrine disorders: Rare: Hyperprolactinemia. Not known: Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH).", "Metabolic disorders: Not known: Hypoglycemia.", "Psychiatric disorders: Very common: Anxiety, insomnia. Common: Depression, psychotic disorder. Uncommon: Confusional state, decreased libido, restlessness.", "Nervous system disorders: Very common: Extrapyramidal disorder, hyperkinesia, headache. Common: Tardive dyskinesia, dyskinesia, dystonia, akathisia, somnolence, masked facies, tremor, dizziness. Uncommon: Convulsion, Parkinsonism, akinesia, sedation, involuntary muscle contractions. Rare: Motor dysfunction, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS), nystagmus.", "Visual disorders: Common: Visual impairment. Uncommon: Blurred vision.", "Cardiac disorders: Uncommon: Tachycardia. Not known: Ventricular fibrillation, Torsade de pointes, ventricular tachycardia, extrasystoles.", "Vascular disorders: Common: Orthostatic hypotension, hypotension.", "Respiratory disorders: Uncommon: Dyspnea. Rare: Bronchospasm. Not known: Laryngeal edema, laryngospasm.", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Common: Constipation, dry mouth, salivary hypersecretion, nausea, vomiting.", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Common: Abnormal liver function tests. Uncommon: Hepatitis, jaundice. Not known: Acute hepatic failure, cholestasis.", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Common: Rash. Uncommon: Photosensitivity reaction, urticaria, pruritus, hyperhidrosis. Not known: Hypersensitivity vasculitis, exfoliative dermatitis.", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Uncommon: Torticollis, muscle rigidity, muscle spasm, muscular twitching. Rare: Trismus, muscle contracture.", "Renal and urinary disorders: Common: Urinary retention.", "Pregnancy: Not known: Neonatal drug withdrawal syndrome.", "Reproductive system and breast disorders: Common: Erectile dysfunction. Uncommon: Amenorrhea, dysmenorrhea, galactorrhea, breast pain. Rare: Menorrhagia, menstrual disorder, sexual dysfunction. Not known: Gynaecomastia, priapism.", "General disorders: Uncommon: Gait disturbance, hyperthermia, edema. Not known: Sudden death, facial edema, hypothermia.", "Investigations: Common: Weight increased, weight decreased. Rare: Electrocardiogram QT prolonged.", "Additional information: QT prolongation, Torsade de pointes, ventricular arrhythmias, including ventricular fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia, and cardiac arrest have been reported. These effects may occur more frequently with high doses and in patients at risk. Toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome have been reported in patients taking haloperidol. Cases of venous thromboembolism, including pulmonary embolism and deep vein thrombosis, have been reported with antipsychotic drugs."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Antidepressants (tricyclic, fluvoxamine, nefazodone, paroxetine, venlafaxine), quinidine, buspirone, fluoxetine", "effect": "Haloperidol inhibits CYP2D6, increasing plasma concentrations of tricyclic antidepressants and enhancing their toxicity/anticholinergic effects. Other antidepressants, quinidine, buspirone, or fluoxetine may increase haloperidol concentrations, and a dose reduction of haloperidol may be necessary."}, {"drug": "Olanzapine", "effect": "May increase the risk of extrapyramidal symptoms and Parkinsonism."}, {"drug": "Enzyme inducers (carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, rifampin)", "effect": "Increase haloperidol metabolism via CYP3A4, significantly reducing serum haloperidol concentrations. A haloperidol dose increase is required during concomitant use, and a dose reduction is necessary upon discontinuation."}, {"drug": "Smoking", "effect": "Increases haloperidol clearance and lowers serum concentrations; clinical monitoring is recommended."}, {"drug": "Adrenaline and other sympathomimetics, antiadrenergic drugs (guanethidine), propranolol", "effect": "Haloperidol inhibits adrenaline activity, potentially reversing its hypotensive effects. Concomitant propranolol may cause severe hypotension and cardiopulmonary arrest."}, {"drug": "QT-prolonging drugs, antiarrhythmic drugs", "effect": "May synergistically prolong the QT interval and lead to severe cardiac adverse effects."}, {"drug": "Methyldopa (high doses)", "effect": "May potentiate pharmacologic effects, causing confusion and increased central nervous system toxicity."}, {"drug": "Levodopa, cabergoline (D2 dopamine agonists)", "effect": "Haloperidol competitively inhibits dopamine receptors, interfering with the therapeutic efficacy of these antiparkinsonian agents."}, {"drug": "Tacrine", "effect": "May cause a Parkinson-like syndrome by increasing central acetylcholine activity."}, {"drug": "Alcohol, central nervous system depressants (anesthetics, opiates, barbiturates)", "effect": "Potentiate the sedative and respiratory depressant effects of haloperidol, potentially causing profound hypotension and increased central nervous system depression."}, {"drug": "Lithium", "effect": "Concomitant use may precipitate an acute encephalopathy-like syndrome; close clinical monitoring is required, and the drug should be discontinued immediately if neurotoxicity occurs."}, {"drug": "Dextromethorphan", "effect": "May lead to increased dextromethorphan plasma concentrations and associated adverse effects; a dose reduction of dextromethorphan may be necessary."}, {"drug": "Anticoagulants (phenindione, coumarins)", "effect": "Haloperidol may antagonize the anticoagulant effects of these agents."}, {"drug": "Anticholinergic drugs (including anti-Parkinsonian agents), antihistamines, MAO inhibitors", "effect": "May increase intraocular pressure and severely enhance anticholinergic adverse effects."}, {"drug": "Amphetamine", "effect": "May reduce the stimulant effect of amphetamine and diminish the antipsychotic efficacy of haloperidol."}, {"drug": "Anticonvulsants, tramadol", "effect": "May alter seizure threshold, change seizure frequency, or increase the risk of seizures; dosage adjustment of anticonvulsants may be necessary."}, {"drug": "Bromocriptine", "effect": "Haloperidol may antagonize the therapeutic effects of bromocriptine and increase serum prolactin levels; bromocriptine dosage adjustment may be necessary."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Haloperidol may impair mental alertness and/or physical coordination, which could adversely affect the performance of hazardous tasks such as operating heavy machinery or driving motor vehicles. Outpatients must be cautioned regarding this risk.", "contraindications": ["Comatose patients.", "Central nervous system depression due to alcohol or other central nervous system depressant drugs.", "Severe depression.", "Individuals with a history of degenerative diseases.", "Elderly patients with Parkinson's disease symptoms.", "Patients diagnosed with Parkinson's disease.", "Hypersensitivity to haloperidol or any excipient of the drug.", "Patients with severe basal ganglia lesions.", "Patients with prolactin-dependent tumors.", "Haloperidol has the potential to prolong the QT interval, although this is rare. Haloperidol should not be used in patients with severe cardiac disorders such as acute myocardial infarction, decompensated heart failure, arrhythmias treated with Class IA and III antiarrhythmics, prolonged QT interval, history of ventricular arrhythmias or Torsade de pointes, second- or third-degree heart block, and uncorrected hypokalemia. Do not use haloperidol concomitantly with other QT-prolonging drugs."], "manufacturingInfo": {"origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place, protected from light, at a temperature not exceeding 30°C.", "packaging": "Bottle of 200 tablets", "manufacturer": "Danapha Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dosage must be individualized based on patient needs and clinical response. Titration (dose increases or decreases) should be performed as rapidly as possible to achieve optimal therapeutic control. Initial dosing should account for the patient's age, clinical condition, response to prior antipsychotics, and concurrent medications or medical comorbidities. Pediatric patients, debilitated or elderly individuals, and patients with a history of adverse reactions to antipsychotics may require lower initial and maintenance doses. Adults: Moderate symptoms: 0.5 to 2 mg per dose, 2 to 3 times daily. Severe symptoms: 3 to 5 mg per dose, 2 to 3 times daily. For rapid control, higher doses may be required in select cases. Elderly or debilitated patients: 0.5 to 2 mg per dose, 2 to 3 times daily. Chronic or treatment-resistant patients: 3 to 5 mg per dose, 2 to 3 times daily. If symptom control is incomplete, dosage titration may be necessary, with daily doses up to 100 mg. In severely treatment-resistant patients, doses exceeding 100 mg should not be used routinely. Pediatric population: Haloperidol is not indicated for children under 3 years of age. Children aged 3 to 12 years (weight 15 kg to 40 kg): Initiate at the lowest possible starting dose (0.5 mg/day), increased by 0.5 mg every 5 to 7 days until the desired therapeutic response is achieved, administered in 2 to 3 divided doses daily. Psychotic disorders: 0.05 to 0.15 mg/kg/day. Behavioral disorders and Tourette's syndrome: 0.05 to 0.075 mg/kg/day. In severe cases, higher doses may be required but should not exceed 6 mg/day.", "administration": "Administer orally as directed by a qualified physician. Alternative dosage forms with different strengths may be utilized to achieve precise individualized dosing.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms: Haloperidol overdose manifests as severe extrapyramidal symptoms, profound hypotension, and marked sedation. Patients may experience coma, respiratory depression, and hypotension leading to shock-like states. Extrapyramidal manifestations include muscle weakness, rigidity, and localized or generalized tremors. The potential risk of electrocardiographic changes associated with Torsade de pointes must be evaluated. Management: Perform gastric lavage or induce emesis immediately, followed by the administration of activated charcoal. There is no specific pharmacological antidote; management is strictly supportive. Maintain a patent airway and perform endotracheal intubation if necessary. Respiratory depression should be managed with supplemental oxygen and mechanical ventilation. Hypotension and circulatory collapse should be treated with intravenous fluid resuscitation (plasma expanders, albumin) and vasopressors such as metaraminol, phenylephrine, or norepinephrine; epinephrine must be avoided as it may paradoxically worsen hypotension. Severe extrapyramidal symptoms should be treated with appropriate antiparkinsonian or anticonvulsant agents. Continuously monitor electrocardiogram (ECG) and vital signs, particularly for signs of QT prolongation and cardiac arrhythmias, until the ECG normalizes. Treat severe arrhythmias with standard antiarrhythmic therapy."}, "generalDescription": "Haloperidol 2mg Danapha Tablets are an antipsychotic medication containing haloperidol as the active pharmaceutical ingredient, utilized for the management of various psychotic disorders and behavioral disturbances.", "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Haloperidol 2mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Dosage reduction is required in elderly patients. Increased overall mortality is observed in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis treated with antipsychotics. The use of haloperidol is contraindicated in elderly patients presenting with Parkinsonian symptoms.", "lactation": "Breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment with haloperidol, as the drug is excreted in human breast milk.", "pediatric": "Haloperidol is contraindicated and not indicated for children under 3 years of age. The safety and efficacy of haloperidol in pediatric patients have not been fully established.", "pregnancy": "There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of haloperidol in pregnant women. Neonates exposed to antipsychotic drugs during the third trimester of pregnancy are at risk for developing extrapyramidal symptoms and/or neonatal withdrawal symptoms following delivery, including agitation, hypertonia, hypotonia, tremor, somnolence, respiratory distress, and feeding disorders. These complications may be self-limiting or require intensive supportive care and prolonged hospitalization. Congenital malformations have also been reported following maternal exposure to haloperidol concurrently with other potentially teratogenic medications during the first trimester. Haloperidol should be used during pregnancy or in women of childbearing potential only if the anticipated clinical benefit clearly outweighs the potential risks to the fetus."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Increased mortality in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis: Haloperidol is not approved for the management of patients with dementia-related psychosis.", "Cardiovascular effects: Cases of sudden death, QT prolongation, and Torsade de pointes have been reported in patients receiving haloperidol, even in the absence of pre-existing risk factors. Extreme caution is warranted in patients with known risk factors for QT prolongation (electrolyte disturbances, particularly hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia; concomitant administration of drugs that prolong the QT interval; underlying cardiac abnormalities; hypothyroidism; and familial long QT syndrome).", "Tardive Dyskinesia: A syndrome characterized by potentially irreversible, involuntary, dyskinetic movements may develop in patients treated with antipsychotic drugs. The prevalence is highest among elderly patients, particularly elderly females. Both the risk of developing tardive dyskinesia and the likelihood of its irreversibility increase with prolonged treatment duration and cumulative total dose. For patients requiring long-term therapy, prescribe the lowest effective dose and shortest duration necessary to achieve the desired clinical response. Symptoms may be temporarily masked upon drug re-initiation, dosage increases, or switching to an alternative antipsychotic. Discontinuation of therapy should be considered at the earliest sign of tardive dyskinesia, though some patients may require continued treatment despite the syndrome.", "Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS): A potentially fatal symptom complex associated with antipsychotic drugs, characterized clinically by hyperpyrexia, severe muscle rigidity, altered mental status, and autonomic instability (irregular pulse or blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis, and cardiac arrhythmias). Additional laboratory findings may include elevated serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK), myoglobinuria (rhabdomyolysis), and acute renal failure. Hyperthermia is frequently an early manifestation. Immediately discontinue antipsychotics and all non-essential medications, initiate intensive symptomatic treatment, and provide rigorous medical monitoring. If a patient requires subsequent antipsychotic therapy after recovering from NMS, the reintroduction of drug therapy must be approached with extreme caution, and patients must be closely monitored for recurrence.", "Falls: Haloperidol may cause somnolence, postural (orthostatic) hypotension, and motor and sensorimotor instability, which can lead to falls resulting in fractures or other traumatic injuries. Evaluate fall risk upon treatment initiation and periodically for patients on chronic antipsychotic therapy.", "Concomitant use of haloperidol and lithium: An encephalopathic syndrome (characterized by weakness, lethargy, fever, tremulousness, confusion, extrapyramidal symptoms, leukocytosis, elevated serum enzymes, blood urea nitrogen, and hyperglycemia) leading to irreversible brain damage has been reported in some patients treated concomitantly with lithium and haloperidol. Monitor closely for early clinical signs of neurotoxicity and discontinue therapy immediately if such signs emerge.", "Other Warnings: Cases of bronchopneumonia, some fatal, have been reported following the administration of antipsychotic drugs, including haloperidol. Coma and reduced thirst perception secondary to central nervous system inhibition can lead to dehydration, hemoconcentration, and diminished pulmonary ventilation. Prompt therapeutic intervention by a physician is essential if these signs and symptoms appear, particularly in elderly patients. Alcohol must be avoided during haloperidol therapy to prevent additive central nervous system depression and profound hypotension.", "Precautions: Leukopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis (potentially fatal) have been reported with antipsychotic agents. Risk factors include pre-existing low white blood cell (WBC) count and a history of drug-induced leukopenia or neutropenia. Perform complete blood cell counts (CBC) frequently during the first few months of therapy, and discontinue haloperidol immediately at the first sign of a clinically significant decline in WBC count in the absence of other causative factors. Patients with severe neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count < 1,000/mm - ) should discontinue haloperidol and have their WBC monitored until recovery. Exercise caution in patients with severe cardiovascular disease due to the risk of transient hypotension and/or anginal exacerbation. If hypotension occurs, manage with appropriate vasopressors; epinephrine must not be used, as haloperidol blocks its vasoconstrictive properties and may cause a further drop in blood pressure. Preferentially utilize metaraminol, phenylephrine, or norepinephrine. Haloperidol lowers the seizure threshold; exercise caution in patients receiving anticonvulsant therapy, with a history of epilepsy, or with electroencephalographic abnormalities. If continued use is mandatory, maintain adequate anticonvulsant therapy. Assess for allergies or a history of drug hypersensitivity. Exercise caution during concomitant use of anticoagulants such as phenindione. If co-administration of anti-Parkinsonian agents is necessary, continuation of the anti-Parkinsonian drug may be required after haloperidol discontinuation to prevent precipitation of extrapyramidal symptoms; acute angle-closure glaucoma may occur due to anticholinergic properties when anticholinergic anti-Parkinsonian agents are combined with haloperidol. Severe neurotoxicity characterized by muscle rigidity and inability to walk or speak has been reported in hyperthyroid patients receiving antipsychotic medications, including haloperidol."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:22:42.745	haloperidol-2mg-danapha-tablets-for-psychotic-disorders-and-behavioral-disturbances-bottle-of-200	163
