ry, working at heights, or performing hazardous tasks.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Amoxicillin is a semisynthetic antibiotic with a broad spectrum of bactericidal activity against many Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms. It acts by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis. Clavulanic Acid possesses a similar beta-lactam structure to penicillins and has the ability to inactivate a wide variety of beta-lactamase enzymes commonly found in microorganisms resistant to penicillins and cephalosporins."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Both amoxicillin and clavulanic acid are fully dissociated in aqueous solution at physiological pH. Both components are rapidly and well absorbed orally when taken at the start of a meal.", "metabolism": "Amoxicillin is partly excreted in the urine as inactive penicilloic acid. Clavulanic acid is extensively metabolized in man to 2,5-dihydro-4-(2-hydroxyethyl)-5-oxo-1H-pyrrole-3-carboxylic acid and 1-amino-4-hydroxybutan-2-one and eliminated in urine and feces as well as in carbon dioxide in expired air."}, "contraindications": ["Patients with a history of hypersensitivity to penicillins, cephalosporins, or any beta-lactam antibacterial agents, or to any of the excipients.", "Patients with a history of jaundice or hepatic dysfunction associated with amoxicillin/clavulanic acid."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "DHG Pharma", "origin": "Vietnam", "storage": "Store in a dry place, at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, protect from light.", "packaging": "Box of 3 blisters x 4 Tablets", "manufacturer": "DHG Pharmaceutical Joint Stock Company (Vietnam)"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dosage depends on the expected pathogens and their susceptibility to antibacterial agents, the severity and site of infection, and the age, weight, and renal function of the patient.\\nFor adults and pediatric patients - 40 kg: 500 mg / 125 mg 3 times daily. Total daily dose is 1500 mg amoxicillin / 375 mg clavulanic acid.\\nFor pediatric patients < 40 kg: 20 mg/5 mg/kg/day to 60 mg/15 mg/kg/day divided in 3 doses. Maximum daily dose does not exceed 2400 mg amoxicillin / 500 mg clavulanic acid. No dosage recommendation is available for children < 2 years old.\\nRenal impairment: Adjust dosage based on the maximum recommended level of amoxicillin. No adjustment is required in patients with creatinine clearance (CrCl) > 30 mL/min.\\nAdults and pediatric patients - 40 kg:\\n- CrCl 10 to 30 mL/min: 500 mg / 125 mg twice daily.\\n- CrCl < 10 mL/min: 500 mg / 125 mg once daily.\\n- Hemodialysis: 500 mg / 125 mg every 24 hours, plus 500 mg / 125 mg during dialysis, repeated at the end of dialysis (due to decreased serum concentrations of amoxicillin and clavulanic acid).\\nPediatric patients < 40 kg:\\n- CrCl 10 to 30 mL/min: 15 mg / 3.75 mg/kg twice daily (Max 500 mg / 125 mg twice daily).\\n- CrCl < 10 mL/min: Single dose of 15 mg / 3.75 mg/kg/day (Max 500 mg / 125 mg).\\n- Hemodialysis: 15 mg / 3.75 mg/kg once daily. Prior to hemodialysis: 15 mg / 3.75 mg/kg. To restore circulating drug levels, administer 15 mg / 3.75 mg/kg after hemodialysis.\\nHepatic impairment: Use with caution and monitor hepatic function at regular intervals. Data are insufficient to recommend a specific dosage.\\nDuration of treatment should be determined by the patient's response. Certain infections (e.g., osteomyelitis) require prolonged treatment. Treatment should not exceed 14 days without a medical review.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, take it as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and resume the regular dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Swallow Klamentin tablets whole without chewing. If necessary, tablets may be broken in half and swallowed without chewing. Use within 30 days after opening the blister pouch. Administer at the start of meals to minimize gastrointestinal intolerance and optimize the absorption of amoxicillin/clavulanic acid. Treatment may be initiated with intravenous therapy and continued with oral therapy, or as directed by a physician.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms: Gastrointestinal symptoms and disturbance of fluid and electrolyte balances may be evident. Amoxicillin crystalluria, in some cases leading to renal failure, has been observed. Convulsions may occur in patients with impaired renal function or those receiving high doses.\\nManagement: Symptomatic treatment of gastrointestinal manifestations with attention to fluid and electrolyte balance. Amoxicillin/clavulanic acid can be removed from the circulation by hemodialysis."}, "generalDescription": "Klamentin 500mg/125mg Tablets is a combination medication containing two active ingredients: Amoxicillin (a penicillin-class antibiotic) and Clavulanic Acid (a beta-lactamase inhibitor). It is used as an antimicrobial agent to treat various infections caused by susceptible bacteria that may lead to significant health complications.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Aerosil, Crospovidone (Polyplasdon XL), Microcrystalline cellulose (Avicel M112), Magnesium stearate, Sepifilm LP914, Titanium dioxide, Talc", "activeIngredients": "Amoxicillin (as Amoxicillin Trihydrate) 500mg, Clavulanic Acid (as Potassium Clavulanate) 125mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "[object Object]", "lactation": "Both substances are excreted in breast milk (minus quantities of clavulanic acid). Consequently, diarrhea and fungus infection of the mucous membranes are possible in the nursing infant, so that nursing might have to be discontinued. Amoxicillin/clavulanic acid should only be used during lactation after benefit-risk assessment by the physician.", "pediatric": "[object Object]", "pregnancy": "Animal studies do not indicate direct or indirect harmful effects with respect to pregnancy, embryonal/fetal development, parturition, or postnatal development. Human data from a single study in women with preterm premature rupture of the membranes indicated that prophylactic treatment with amoxicillin/clavulanic acid may be associated with an increased risk of necrotizing enterocolitis in neonates. Use should be avoided unless considered essential by the physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Careful inquiry should be made concerning previous hypersensitivity reactions to penicillins, cephalosporins, or other allergens prior to initiating therapy with amoxicillin/clavulanic acid.", "Where an infection is proven to be due to an amoxicillin-susceptible organism, consideration should be given to switching from amoxicillin/clavulanic acid to amoxicillin in accordance with official guidance.", "Klamentin is not suitable for use when there is a high risk that the presumed pathogens have reduced susceptibility or resistance to beta-lactams not mediated by beta-lactamases susceptible to clavulanic acid. It should not be used to treat penicillin-resistant S. pneumoniae.", "Convulsions may occur in patients with impaired renal function or in those receiving high doses.", "Avoid using amoxicillin/clavulanic acid if infectious mononucleosis is suspected, as the occurrence of a morbilliform rash has been associated with this condition following the use of amoxicillin.", "Concomitant use of allopurinol during treatment with amoxicillin can increase the likelihood of allergic skin reactions.", "Prolonged treatment may occasionally result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms. Discontinue use and institute appropriate therapy if superinfections occur.", "Exercise caution when administering amoxicillin/clavulanic acid to patients with evidence of hepatic impairment.", "Antibiotic-associated colitis has been reported, ranging in severity from mild to life-threatening.", "In renal impairment, dosage should be adjusted according to the degree of impairment. Adequate fluid intake and urinary output should be maintained during administration of high doses of amoxicillin to reduce the possibility of amoxicillin crystalluria.", "When testing for glucose in urine during amoxicillin treatment, enzymatic glucose oxidase methods should be used, as non-enzymatic methods may yield false-positive results. Positive results with the Bio-Rad Laboratories Platelia Aspergillus EIA test have been reported."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If an allergic reaction occurs (e.g., rash, angioedema, anaphylaxis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome), discontinue amoxicillin therapy immediately and initiate emergency treatment with adrenaline, oxygen, intravenous corticosteroids, and airway management including intubation. Never administer penicillins or cephalosporins again.\\nPseudomembranous colitis: If mild, discontinue medication. If severe (likely due to Clostridium difficile), administer fluid and electrolyte replacement, and anti-clostridial antibiotics (e.g., oral metronidazole or vancomycin)."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:48:17.768	klamentin-500mg-125mg-625mg-amoxicillin-and-clavulanate-potassium-film-coated-tablets-for-bacterial	62
