ablets", "manufacturer": "Gedeon Richter Plc."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Use the medication strictly as prescribed by a physician. In the absence of a prescription, the standard recommended dosage is 3 tablets daily, divided into 3 doses.", "administration": "Take the medication orally after meals."}, "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Colloidal anhydrous silica, magnesium stearate, talc, maize starch, lactose monohydrate", "activeIngredients": "Vinpocetine 10mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Contraindicated.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Patients with lactose intolerance should note that each tablet contains 83 mg of lactose.", "Inform your physician if you have any other medical conditions (especially heart failure or electrocardiographic abnormalities).", "Electrocardiographic monitoring is recommended in cases of long QT syndrome or when administered concomitantly with medications that prolong the QT interval."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:50:02.848	cavinton-forte-10mg-vinpocetine-tablets-for-cerebral-circulatory-disorders-and-hearing-impairment-2	272
1736	Clopalvix 75mg Tablets for Atherosclerotic Thrombotic Events and Vascular Occlusion (5 Blisters x 14 Tablets)	P02709	\N	\N	\N	[object Object]	432584.00	385000.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250728082923-0-P02709.png?versionId=pAzSGv_OibvlHZyY3C4yHnsv3_9EAZFJ	Clopalvix 75mg is an antiplatelet medication containing Clopidogrel indicated for the prevention of atherothrombotic events and vascular occlusion.	{"indications": ["Recent myocardial infarction (from a few days up to less than 35 days), recent ischemic stroke (from 7 days up to less than 6 months), or established peripheral arterial disease.", "Acute coronary syndrome without ST-segment elevation (unstable angina or non-Q-wave myocardial infarction), including patients undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention, in combination with aspirin.", "Acute myocardial infarction with ST-segment elevation: In combination with aspirin in medical treatment for thrombolytic therapy.", "In patients with atrial fibrillation who are unsuited for treatment with vitamin K antagonists and who have low bleeding risk, clopidogrel is co-administered with aspirin for the prevention of atherothrombotic and thromboembolic events, including stroke."], "sideEffects": ["Bleeding: Gastrointestinal hemorrhage (2.0%), intracranial hemorrhage (0.4%), purpura, bruising, hematoma, and epistaxis. Less frequently reported events include hematuria, eye hemorrhage (mainly conjunctival).", "Hematological: Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia.", "Central and peripheral nervous system: Headache, dizziness, vertigo.", "Gastrointestinal system: Abdominal pain, dyspepsia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, gastritis.", "Skin: Rash, pruritus."], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Aspirin", "effect": "Aspirin did not modify the ADP-induced platelet aggregation inhibited by clopidogrel, but clopidogrel potentiated the effect of aspirin on collagen-induced platelet aggregation. However, concomitant administration of 500 mg of aspirin twice a day for 1 day did not significantly increase the prolongation of bleeding time induced by clopidogrel. Pharmacodynamic interaction between clopidogrel and aspirin is possible, leading to an increased risk of bleeding. Therefore, caution should be exercised with concomitant use."}, {"drug": "Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)", "effect": "In a clinical study involving healthy volunteers, the concomitant administration of naproxen and clopidogrel was associated with increased occult gastrointestinal blood loss. Caution should be used when co-administering NSAIDs with clopidogrel."}, {"drug": "Warfarin", "effect": "Because the co-administration of warfarin with clopidogrel may increase the risk of bleeding, caution should be exercised when combining these medications."}, {"drug": "Heparin", "effect": "In a clinical study in healthy subjects, clopidogrel did not necessitate modification of the heparin dose or alter the effect of heparin on coagulation. Co-administration of heparin had no effect on the inhibition of platelet aggregation induced by clopidogrel. Pharmacodynamic interaction between clopidogrel and heparin is possible, leading to an increased risk of bleeding. Therefore, caution is warranted with concomitant use."}, {"drug": "Thrombolytic agents", "effect": "The safety of the combination of clopidogrel and thrombolytic agents has not been officially established; therefore, caution should be exercised when combining these agents."}, {"drug": "Atenolol, nifedipine, or both atenolol and nifedipine", "effect": "There were no clinically significant pharmacodynamic interactions when clopidogrel was co-administered with atenolol, nifedipine, or both."}, {"drug": "Phenobarbital, cimetidine, or estrogen", "effect": "The pharmacodynamic activity of clopidogrel was not significantly influenced by the co-administration of phenobarbital, cimetidine, or estrogen."}, {"drug": "Digoxin or theophylline", "effect": "The pharmacokinetics of digoxin or theophylline were not modified by the co-administration of clopidogrel."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Caution should be exercised when driving vehicles and operating machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Clopidogrel selectively inhibits the binding of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) to its platelet receptor and the subsequent ADP-mediated activation of the glycoprotein GPIIb/IIIa complex, thereby inhibiting platelet aggregation. Clopidogrel also inhibits platelet aggregation induced by agonists other than ADP by blocking the amplification of platelet activation by released ADP. Clopidogrel acts by irreversible modification of the platelet ADP receptor. Consequently, platelets exposed to clopidogrel are affected for the remainder of their lifespan.", "pharmacologicalClass": "Thienopyridine antiplatelet agent"}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Clopidogrel is rapidly but incompletely absorbed after oral administration, with at least 50% of the dose absorbed.", "metabolism": "Clopidogrel is a prodrug and is extensively metabolized in the liver into its inactive carboxylic acid derivative.", "elimination": "Clopidogrel and its carboxylic acid derivative are highly bound to plasma proteins. Clopidogrel and its metabolites are eliminated via urine and feces, with approximately 50% of the oral dose recovered in urine and 46% in feces over a 5-day period."}, "contraindications": ["Hypersensitivity to clopidogrel or any component of the medication.", "Severe hepatic impairment.", "Active pathological bleeding such as peptic ulcer or intracranial hemorrhage, or any other condition causing severe bleeding.", "Breastfeeding women."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Clopalvix", "origin": "[object Object]", "storage": "Store in a dry place, below 30°C, protected from light.", "packaging": "[object Object]", "manufacturer": "[object Object]"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults and elderly: 75 mg once daily. Pediatric patients: There is no clinical experience in children; therefore, the medication is not recommended for pediatric use. Patients with acute coronary syndrome: Non-ST-segment elevation acute coronary syndrome (unstable angina or non-Q-wave myocardial infarction): Initiate medical treatment with a single 300 mg loading dose of clopidogrel on day one, followed by 75 mg once daily thereafter. Combined with aspirin 75 - 325 mg daily starting on subsequent days (aspirin doses under 100 mg daily are generally recommended). Acute myocardial infarction with ST-segment elevation: 75 mg once daily, initiated with a 300 mg loading dose in combination with aspirin, with or without thrombolytics. In patients over 75 years of age, treatment should be initiated without a loading dose of 300 mg. Treatment should be continued for at least 4 weeks. Atrial fibrillation: 75 mg once daily, administered in combination with aspirin (75 - 100 mg daily). Note: The above dosages are for reference only. The specific dosage depends on the patient's physical condition and disease severity. Consult a physician or healthcare professional for appropriate dosing.", "missedDose": "If a dose is missed, take it as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and resume the regular dosing schedule. Do not double the dose to make up for a forgotten one.", "administration": "Clopalvix is administered orally and can be taken with or without food.", "overdoseManagement": "Overdose of clopidogrel can lead to prolonged bleeding time and subsequent hemorrhagic complications. Symptoms may include dyspnea, fatigue, prolonged bleeding, gastrointestinal bleeding, and vomiting; appropriate therapy should be instituted if bleeding occurs. There is no known antidotal pharmacological activity reversal for clopidogrel. Platelet transfusion may be used to reverse the pharmacological effects of clopidogrel."}, "generalDescription": "Clopidogrel is a thienopyridine antiplatelet inhibitor structurally and pharmacologically related to ticlopidine. Clopidogrel selectively inhibits the binding of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) to its platelet receptor and the subsequent ADP-mediated activation of the glycoprotein GPIIb/IIIa complex, thereby inhibiting platelet aggregation. Clopidogrel also inhibits platelet aggregation induced by agonists other than ADP by blocking the amplification of platelet activation by released ADP. Clopidogrel acts by irreversible modification of the platelet ADP receptor. Consequently, platelets exposed to clopidogrel are affected for the remainder of their lifespan.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "75mg", "activeIngredients": "Clopidogrel"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Administer 75 mg once daily. For patients over 75 years of age, initiate treatment with a daily dose of 75 mg without a loading dose.", "lactation": "Contraindicated in breastfeeding women.", "pediatric": "There is no clinical experience with this medication in children. The medication is not recommended for pediatric use.", "pregnancy": "There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Clopidogrel should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["In patients with acute myocardial infarction, treatment with clopidogrel should not be initiated within the first few days following myocardial infarction.", "Clopidogrel prolongs bleeding time and should be used with caution in patients who may be at risk of increased bleeding from trauma, surgery, or other pathological conditions (particularly gastrointestinal and intraocular).", "Clopidogrel should be used with caution in patients with lesions prone to bleeding (such as ulcers).", "If a patient is scheduled to undergo elective surgery and antiplatelet effect is temporarily not desired, clopidogrel should be discontinued 5 to 7 days prior to surgery.", "There is limited clinical experience with clopidogrel in patients with hepatic and renal impairment; therefore, caution should be exercised when administering the medication to these patient populations.", "Patients must be informed that when taking clopidogrel for an extended duration, they should stop the medication if abnormal bleeding occurs and report any unusual bleeding to their physician.", "Patients should inform physicians and dentists that they are taking clopidogrel before any scheduled surgery and prior to taking any new medication."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If any adverse reactions occur, discontinue use and inform a physician or report to the nearest medical facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:51:02.528	clopalvix-75mg-tablets-for-atherosclerotic-thrombotic-events-and-vascular-occlusion-5-blisters-x-14	\N
